/* English translations of the question bank — keyed by question id */
const QEN = {"anatomy-1r8fob4":{"q":"Which muscle is a prime mover in elbow flexion when the forearm is supinated?","e":"The biceps assists elbow flexion and forearm supination, so it is especially active when the palm faces up.","o":["The biceps (Biceps brachii)","The triceps of the arm","The anterior and middle deltoid","The latissimus dorsi and trapezius"]},"anatomy-1j09tby":{"q":"Which muscle is on the back of the upper arm and is responsible for elbow extension?","e":"The triceps is the main elbow extensor – active in presses, push-ups and elbow extensions.","o":["The triceps (Triceps brachii)","The biceps (Biceps brachii)","The brachialis","The anterior deltoid"]},"anatomy-1qnql18":{"q":"Which muscle group is the prime mover in knee extension?","e":"The quadriceps extend the knee – for example in the upward phase of a squat or on the leg extension machine.","o":["The quadriceps","The hamstrings and calves","The calves","The hip flexors"]},"anatomy-1ps6wca":{"q":"Which plane of motion divides the body into right and left sides?","e":"Flexion and extension happen in the sagittal plane – like squats, lunges and elbow curls.","o":["The sagittal plane","The frontal plane","The transverse (horizontal) plane","The oblique plane"]},"anatomy-c6tao5":{"q":"A lateral raise of the arms is performed mainly in which plane?","e":"The frontal plane divides the body into front and back; abduction and adduction happen in it.","o":["The frontal plane","The sagittal plane","The transverse plane","It has no defined plane"]},"anatomy-1nzgnl":{"q":"Trunk rotation (e.g. Russian twist or woodchop) is performed mainly in which plane?","e":"The transverse (horizontal) plane divides the body into upper and lower parts; rotational movements happen in it.","o":["The transverse plane","The sagittal plane","The frontal plane","Equally in all three"]},"anatomy-tgqshx":{"q":"Which is usually the largest muscle in the human body?","e":"The gluteus maximus is considered the largest muscle in the body and the strongest hip extensor.","o":["The gluteus maximus","The latissimus dorsi","The quadriceps","The upper and middle trapezius"]},"anatomy-i8qnbr":{"q":"Which four muscles make up the rotator cuff?","e":"The four cuff muscles (SITS) stabilize the head of the humerus in the joint and rotate the shoulder.","o":["Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis","Anterior, middle and posterior deltoid and upper trapezius","Teres major, latissimus dorsi, pectoralis major and rhomboid major","Biceps, triceps, brachialis and coracobrachialis"]},"anatomy-2sb7v1":{"q":"What are the main actions of the latissimus dorsi at the shoulder?","e":"The lats are very active in pull-ups, lat pulldowns and rows – movements of arm adduction and extension.","o":["Adduction, extension and internal rotation","Abduction, flexion and external rotation","Forward flexion and scapular elevation","Scapular elevation and external rotation"]},"anatomy-5ox47q":{"q":"Tendons connect muscle to bone, and ligaments connect bone to bone.","e":"True. The tendon transmits muscle force to the bone, and the ligament stabilizes the joint between two bones."},"anatomy-18konbn":{"q":"During the lowering phase of a squat, which joint actions occur?","e":"On the way down all three joints 'close' together: the hip and knee flex and the shin tilts forward over the foot.","o":["Hip flexion, knee flexion and ankle dorsiflexion","Hip extension, knee extension and ankle plantar flexion","Hip abduction, knee flexion and hip external rotation","Knee flexion only, with no movement at the hip or ankle"]},"anatomy-5o2ddh":{"q":"The soleus muscle crosses the knee joint.","e":"False. The soleus starts below the knee and crosses only the ankle. The gastrocnemius is the one that also crosses the knee."},"anatomy-dwtjan":{"q":"Why does a standing calf raise with a straight knee emphasize the gastrocnemius more than a seated calf raise?","e":"The gastrocnemius is a two-joint muscle. With a bent knee it is relatively 'slack', so when seated the soleus does most of the work.","o":["The gastrocnemius crosses the knee; when the knee bends it is shortened and produces less force","When seated the calf muscles don't work at all, so all the work goes to the thigh","When standing the soleus can't work, so only the gastrocnemius lifts the heel","There is no difference – both versions work the same muscles equally"]},"anatomy-1h7zn79":{"q":"What is 'active insufficiency'?","e":"Example: it's hard to make a strong fist when the wrist is fully flexed, because the finger flexors are already shortened across both joints.","o":["A two-joint muscle that is shortened across both joints at once and therefore produces less force","Muscle weakness caused by lack of training over a long period, even at normal length","A state where the opposing muscle (antagonist) blocks the movement","Loss of force caused by fatigue of the central nervous system"]},"anatomy-sw3ry7":{"q":"Which muscle is the main hip flexor?","e":"The iliopsoas (psoas and iliacus) is the strongest hip flexor – active in knee raises and running.","o":["The iliopsoas","The gluteus maximus","The hamstrings","The adductors"]},"anatomy-bsxb07":{"q":"What are the main actions of the hamstrings?","e":"The hamstrings cross both the hip and the knee, so they work in knee curls and in hip-hinge movements like the RDL.","o":["Knee flexion and hip extension","Knee extension and hip flexion","Hip abduction","Ankle dorsiflexion"]},"anatomy-rmnwoi":{"q":"What is the main role of the serratus anterior?","e":"Weakness of the serratus anterior may show up as a 'winged scapula' – a shoulder blade that lifts off the rib cage.","o":["Protracting the scapula, rotating it upward and holding it against the rib cage","Extending the elbow and stabilizing the shoulder during overhead presses","Externally rotating the shoulder and abducting the arm","Shrugging the shoulders to the ears and retracting the scapulae"]},"anatomy-u19rof":{"q":"How many bones are there usually in the adult skeleton?","e":"Babies have more bones, and some fuse during growth until there are 206 in adulthood.","o":["206","180","250","300"]},"anatomy-bse600":{"q":"What type of joint is the shoulder (glenohumeral) joint?","e":"A ball-and-socket joint allows movement in three planes – so the shoulder is very mobile but also less stable.","o":["Ball-and-socket joint","Hinge joint","Plane (gliding) joint","Saddle joint"]},"anatomy-yjtfh1":{"q":"How is the knee joint mainly classified?","e":"The knee works mainly in flexion and extension, with a little rotation when it is bent.","o":["A hinge joint (with limited rotation)","A ball-and-socket joint (movement in all directions)","A plane joint (gliding only)","A saddle joint (like at the base of the thumb)"]},"anatomy-1qmnsvt":{"q":"When raising the arm overhead, the scapula rotates upward.","e":"True. This is part of scapulohumeral rhythm – the combined motion of the arm and shoulder blade that allows full elevation."},"anatomy-13oms14":{"q":"What is an 'agonist' in a movement?","e":"For example, in an elbow curl the biceps is the agonist and the triceps is the antagonist.","o":["The muscle mainly performing the movement","The muscle performing the opposite movement","A muscle that only stabilizes","The tendon connecting two muscles"]},"anatomy-auzv8p":{"q":"Briefly explain the difference between concentric, eccentric and isometric contractions, and give one example of each.","e":"Look for in your answer: change in muscle length, force production, and a matching example for each type.","a":"Concentric: the muscle shortens while producing force, e.g. the lifting phase of a curl. Eccentric: the muscle lengthens while producing force, e.g. lowering the dumbbell under control. Isometric: the muscle produces force without a noticeable change in length, e.g. holding a plank."},"anatomy-g4we0f":{"q":"Describe the length-tension relationship in muscle and its implications for choosing the range of motion in an exercise.","e":"A good answer mentions: intermediate length = maximal force, reduced force at the extremes, and the practical implication for choosing range and position.","a":"A muscle produces maximal active force at an intermediate length, where actin and myosin overlap optimally. When the muscle is very short or very stretched, active force drops. So in different parts of an exercise's range the muscle is 'stronger' or 'weaker', and the choice of range, angle and joint position affects which muscle works and how much load can be moved."},"physio-1brdzrs":{"q":"What is the main role of ATP in muscle cells?","e":"ATP is the cell's immediate 'energy currency', used among other things for muscle contraction and relaxation.","o":["Supplying readily available energy to the cell","Storing calcium in the bones","Transporting oxygen in the blood vessels","Building connective tissue and tendons"]},"physio-ej4rc3":{"q":"What is the most accurate description of a motor unit?","e":"When the neuron fires, all the muscle fibers in its unit contract together.","o":["One motor neuron and all the fibers it innervates","All the muscles that move one joint together in a given movement","All the muscle fibers in one muscle","One tendon together with the muscle attached to it"]},"physio-f8ph9i":{"q":"Which energy system dominates a maximal effort of about 10 seconds, such as a short sprint?","e":"Creatine phosphate stores supply energy very quickly but run out within seconds.","o":["The ATP–CP (phosphocreatine) system","The aerobic (oxidative) system","Fat breakdown in the presence of oxygen","Protein breakdown into amino acids"]},"physio-1lkgby8":{"q":"In an intense effort of about 30 to 90 seconds, which energy system contributes the most?","e":"Glycolysis breaks down glucose quickly without depending on oxygen and supplies most of the energy in such efforts.","o":["Anaerobic glycolysis","The ATP–CP system alone","Fat oxidation","The aerobic system alone"]},"physio-ztxsgz":{"q":"Which energy system does a long, moderate effort such as a 40-minute brisk walk rely on mainly?","e":"In long, moderate-intensity efforts most of the energy comes from oxidizing carbohydrates and fats in the mitochondria.","o":["The aerobic (oxidative) system","The ATP–CP system","Anaerobic glycolysis alone","Protein breakdown as the main source"]},"physio-iek1bu":{"q":"Lactic acid is the main cause of delayed-onset muscle soreness (DOMS) one or two days after training.","e":"False. Lactate is cleared from the blood shortly after exercise. DOMS is linked mainly to micro-damage in the fibers and an inflammatory repair process, especially after eccentric work."},"physio-1iuhyis":{"q":"What is the simple, common formula for estimating maximal heart rate?","e":"This is only a rough estimate – the variation between people can be large, so it's worth combining it with an effort scale.","o":["220 minus age","200 minus age","180 plus age","Resting heart rate times 2"]},"physio-1fafy61":{"q":"A 40-year-old trainee. Using 220 minus age, what is the 70%–80% range of estimated maximal heart rate?","e":"Estimated max HR: 220 − 40 = 180. 70% = 126, 80% = 144.","o":["126–144","112–128","140–160","133–152"]},"physio-1dbfyns":{"q":"What is VO₂max?","e":"VO₂max is a key measure of aerobic fitness, usually expressed in ml of oxygen per kg body weight per minute.","o":["The maximal rate of oxygen uptake and use during exercise","The amount of oxygen left in the blood at rest","The maximal volume of air the lungs can hold","The maximal heart rate measured during hard running"]},"physio-1x9imv4":{"q":"What characterizes type I (slow-twitch) muscle fibers?","e":"Type I fibers are suited to prolonged aerobic work. Type II fibers produce force and speed but fatigue faster.","o":["Fatigue-resistant and rich in mitochondria","High explosive force and very fast fatigue","No mitochondria and reliant on glycogen","Found only in trained endurance athletes"]},"physio-1a5rcfe":{"q":"What is the size principle of recruitment (Henneman's size principle)?","e":"So to recruit the strongest fibers you need a heavy load or an effort close to failure.","o":["Small units are recruited first, and large ones join as the required force increases","Large units are always recruited first, and small ones join only with fatigue","All motor units in a muscle are recruited together in every movement and intensity","Large muscles always fatigue before small muscles, regardless of load"]},"physio-d77r0l":{"q":"What is muscle hypertrophy?","e":"Hypertrophy is the enlargement of the fibers themselves, in response to resistance training with enough volume, effort, protein and recovery.","o":["An increase in the cross-sectional area of muscle fibers","An increase in the number of fat cells between muscle fibers","A decrease in muscle mass","Shortening of the muscle"]},"physio-1us9nd3":{"q":"Drinking before, during and after training is important for maintaining performance, especially in hot weather.","e":"True. Even mild dehydration can impair performance and increase the load on the cardiovascular system."},"physio-14sbjwy":{"q":"What is EPOC?","e":"After exercise the body keeps consuming extra oxygen to restore stores, clear by-products and return to balance.","o":["Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption","The maximal rate of oxygen uptake during intense exercise","A measure of maximal strength","Another name for resting heart rate"]},"physio-1ro5dx7":{"q":"What is mainly responsible for the fast strength gains in the first weeks of resistance training in beginners?","e":"In the first stage the nervous system 'learns' the movement. Significant hypertrophy takes longer.","o":["Neural adaptations – better recruitment and synchronization","Rapid growth in the number of fibers in each muscle","Changes in the structure and density of load-bearing bones","An increase in lung volume and breathing capacity"]},"physio-1w5yr2c":{"q":"What is the main role of hemoglobin?","e":"Hemoglobin in red blood cells binds oxygen in the lungs and releases it in the tissues.","o":["Carrying oxygen in the blood","Breaking down fats","Contracting muscle","Regulating temperature"]},"physio-baj1bk":{"q":"Regular aerobic training can lower resting heart rate.","e":"True. The heart gets stronger and stroke volume increases, so it needs fewer beats to deliver the same amount of blood."},"physio-mad6tc":{"q":"What is the 'lactate threshold'?","e":"Above the threshold lactate production exceeds its clearance, and the effort becomes hard to sustain.","o":["The intensity above which blood lactate starts rising sharply","The moment the muscle stops producing lactate completely and switches to fat","Blood lactate concentration at complete rest","The heart rate at which the body stops sweating during exercise"]},"physio-1hu1g8o":{"q":"What is systolic blood pressure?","e":"In a 120/80 reading, systolic is 120 (contraction) and diastolic is 80 (relaxation).","o":["The pressure in the arteries when the heart contracts","The pressure in the arteries when the heart relaxes","The pressure in the lungs","The heart rate per minute"]},"physio-1mxix75":{"q":"What is the principle that the body adapts specifically to the demand placed on it called?","e":"SAID = Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands. Want to get better at something? Train it.","o":["The SAID principle","The size principle","The reversibility principle","The balance principle"]},"physio-1jvjm":{"q":"When you stop training for a long time, some of the fitness gains gradually disappear.","e":"True – this is the reversibility principle. Keeping a minimum of training helps preserve most of the gains."},"physio-1bs7o8j":{"q":"Explain, based on the energy systems, why 2–5 minutes of rest between maximal-strength sets is recommended.","e":"Look for: the ATP–CP system, the time to restore stores, and the link to the quality of the next set.","a":"In short, heavy sets the energy comes mainly from the ATP–CP system. Creatine phosphate stores deplete quickly and are mostly restored within about 3–5 minutes. Rest that is too short doesn't allow enough restoration, so strength and quality drop in the next set. Long rest keeps the load high and technique good."},"technique-1l963jc":{"q":"In a squat, what is true about the direction of the knees?","e":"Knees caving in (valgus) under load is a common mistake. The goal is for the knee to 'track' over the toes.","o":["The knees track in line with the direction of the feet","The knees should cave inward","The knees should stay straight","The knees should point out much more than the feet"]},"technique-idyg7o":{"q":"In a squat the knees must never pass the line of the toes.","e":"False. For many people this is natural and depends on bone lengths, ankle range and squat style. What matters is control, heels on the floor and knee in line with the foot."},"technique-12lrj1z":{"q":"What is the standard breathing instruction for an average trainee in strength exercises?","e":"Prolonged breath-holding raises blood pressure. Advanced lifters sometimes use controlled breathing with heavy loads, but that is not the default.","o":["Exhale on the effort and inhale on the return, without holding the breath for long","Hold the breath for the whole set to keep full stability","Breathe only in the rests between sets, not during the reps","Inhale during the effort and exhale only at the end of the whole set"]},"technique-1w5i6f6":{"q":"What is the purpose of keeping a 'neutral spine' in the deadlift?","e":"A neutral, braced spine lets the hips and legs transfer force efficiently.","o":["Maintaining the natural curves and distributing the load well","Rounding the back to reach a larger range of motion","Looking at the ceiling the whole time to straighten the neck","Shifting most of the work from the legs to the arms and back"]},"technique-h0lep3":{"q":"Which common bench press mistake may increase the load on the shoulder?","e":"Most trainees feel more comfortable with the elbows at a moderate angle to the body and stable shoulder blades on the bench.","o":["Flaring the elbows to 90 degrees from the body","Lowering the bar until it touches the chest","Keeping the shoulder blades retracted and depressed","Placing the feet firmly on the floor"]},"technique-mmf1uc":{"q":"What indicates a poorly performed plank?","e":"When the hips sag, the core is no longer holding the position and the load shifts to the lower back. Better to shorten the hold and keep quality.","o":["The hips sag and the lower back arches","A straight line from head to heels throughout the hold","Calm breathing and eyes on the floor","Squeezing the glutes and abs"]},"technique-za6gwv":{"q":"In a lat pulldown, where should the bar usually be pulled?","e":"Pulling to the chest with an open chest lets the back work well and is comfortable for the shoulder. Pulling behind the head requires an extreme shoulder range.","o":["Toward the upper chest","Behind the head to the nape of the neck, with an upright back","To the belly","Only to the forehead"]},"technique-ndg2fu":{"q":"What is the main difference between a Romanian deadlift (RDL) and a conventional deadlift?","e":"The RDL emphasizes the hamstrings and glutes through a hip hinge. The conventional deadlift starts from the floor and involves more knee bend.","o":["In the RDL mostly the hip bends, the knee stays fixed, and you start from the top","In the RDL the knees bend much more, and the bar starts from the floor","The RDL is done only with dumbbells, and the regular deadlift only with a barbell","There's no real difference – they're two names for exactly the same exercise"]},"technique-1cb9pwo":{"q":"What is the main movement in a hip hinge?","e":"The hip hinge is the basis for the deadlift, RDL, kettlebell swing and more. The hips move back and the back stays long.","o":["Hip flexion and extension with a neutral spine","Flexing and rounding the lower back","Dropping into a deep squat with an upright torso","Rotating the trunk while leaning forward"]},"technique-kxgrid":{"q":"A general warm-up before training raises muscle temperature and prepares the body for exertion.","e":"True. A good warm-up starts general (brisk walking, cycling) and continues with a warm-up specific to the session's exercises."},"technique-1ksbmvl":{"q":"What indicates proper form in a forward lunge?","e":"Control and stability matter more than depth or weight. You can start with hand support.","o":["A stable torso, knee in line with the foot and a controlled descent","A front knee that caves in slightly on the way down, and a lifting heel","A body that leans sideways to keep balance","A front heel that lifts at the bottom of the movement"]},"technique-1v0iskb":{"q":"A trainee squats and their heels lift off the floor. What is a possible, common cause?","e":"You can work on ankle mobility, widen the stance a little, use a small heel lift, or choose a variation like the goblet squat.","o":["Limited ankle dorsiflexion range","Weakness in the deep abdominal muscles and lower back","A stance too wide relative to the pelvis","Too narrow a grip on the bar on the back"]},"technique-1uzsp0n":{"q":"In a pull-up, what is the role of depressing the shoulder blades at the start of the movement?","e":"Starting with the shoulder blades 'down and back' prepares the back to work and reduces passive hanging on the shoulder joint.","o":["A stable base for the shoulder and engagement of the back muscles","Relieving the hands and forearms only, unrelated to the back","Lengthening the neck and relieving tension in it","Increasing momentum for the pull"]},"technique-twr6fy":{"q":"What does a 3-1-1-0 tempo mean in an exercise?","e":"The first number is usually the eccentric (lowering) phase, followed by the pause at the bottom, the lift and the pause at the top.","o":["3 s lowering, 1 s pause, 1 s lifting, no pause at the top","3 s lifting, 1 s pause, 1 s lowering, no pause at the bottom","3 reps, 1 set, 1 minute rest and zero extra sets","3 sets, 10 reps each, 10 seconds rest between them"]},"technique-jerrrh":{"q":"Lifting straps can help when grip strength limits the weight in pulling exercises.","e":"True. They let the back and legs work at the load suited to them. It's worth adding dedicated grip work too."},"technique-1kfm7oa":{"q":"In a standing overhead press, which common compensation indicates poor core stability?","e":"Squeezing the glutes and abs, keeping the ribs 'down' and using an appropriate weight help keep the torso stable.","o":["Excessive lower-back arch and leaning back","Looking straight ahead throughout the rep","Elbows slightly in front of the bar at the start","A grip at shoulder width or slightly wider"]},"technique-19d2lg3":{"q":"What is the role of a spotter in the bench press?","e":"A good spotter stays close to the bar, coordinates with the trainee in advance and steps in only when needed.","o":["Helping when the trainee can't complete a rep","Lifting part of the weight on every rep of the set","Counting the reps and giving technical feedback","Choosing the right weight instead of the trainee"]},"technique-b0fj9p":{"q":"What is a goblet squat?","e":"The weight in front helps keep the torso upright – so it's an excellent version for learning the squat.","o":["A squat with a weight held in front of the chest","A single-leg squat with wall support","A squat with a barbell on the upper back","A squat with the feet together"]},"technique-abgbpx":{"q":"A trainee deadlifts with a clearly rounded lower back. What corrections and adjustments would you suggest?","e":"Look for: reduced load, teaching the hip hinge, bracing, adjusting range, feedback and gradual progression.","a":"Reduce the weight and return to technique; teach the hip hinge with drills (a dowel along the back, light RDLs); practice breathing and core bracing before each rep; raise the starting point (bar on blocks) if range is limited; check hip and hamstring mobility; and use video for feedback. Progress the load only when the position holds."},"program-17ba12h":{"q":"What does progressive overload mean?","e":"Progressive overload is a planned, gradual increase in training demand – weight, reps, sets or difficulty – in line with ability and recovery.","o":["Gradually increasing the demand to encourage adaptation","Taking every set of every workout to complete failure","Replacing all the exercises in the program every week","Constantly reducing the load to prevent injuries"]},"program-1yijha4":{"q":"A trainee did 4 sets of 10 reps at 60 kg. What is the volume load for the exercise?","e":"Sets × reps × weight: 4 × 10 × 60 = 2,400 kg.","o":["2,400 kg","600 kg","240 kg","4,060 kg"]},"program-1rwh7dw":{"q":"What does training periodization describe?","e":"Periodization organizes training variables over weeks and months to manage adaptation, load and recovery.","o":["Systematic planning of loads and variables over time","Doing exactly the same workout every day of the week","Increasing the weight every set without prior planning","A complete break of several weeks between programs"]},"program-5yov5m":{"q":"What does the principle of specificity say?","e":"If you want to run faster you need to run, and if you want a strong squat you need to squat or do a similar exercise.","o":["The body's adaptation matches the type of training performed","Every workout improves all abilities equally, regardless of type","You should train only on machines","Combining different types of training is forbidden"]},"program-1i9kzae":{"q":"What does RPE 8 mean on a 1–10 effort scale in strength training?","e":"RPE 8 is roughly equal to RIR 2 – two reps left 'in the tank'.","o":["Hard, with about two reps left in reserve","Very easy, with many reps in reserve","Complete failure, with no reps in reserve","Exactly 8 reps in the set, regardless of effort"]},"program-1hj3174":{"q":"Which rep range is mainly associated with developing maximal strength?","e":"Hypertrophy happens across a wide rep range, but maximal strength is trained mainly with heavy loads and few reps.","o":["1–5 reps with a heavy load","15–25 reps with a light load","50 reps or more","There's no link between reps and strength"]},"program-1rn86u3":{"q":"What is 1RM?","e":"1RM is used as a reference point for setting loads, e.g. 75% of 1RM.","o":["The maximum weight for one proper repetition","The maximum number of reps in one minute","The trainee's body weight in kilograms","The recommended rest between heavy sets"]},"program-1721zfy":{"q":"A trainee lifts 100 kg for 5 reps. Using the Epley formula (1RM ≈ weight × (1 + reps/30)), what is the estimated 1RM?","e":"100 × (1 + 5/30) = 100 × 1.167 ≈ 117 kg. It's an estimate – less accurate with many reps.","o":["About 117 kg","About 105 kg","About 125 kg","About 150 kg"]},"program-hu7klq":{"q":"A beginner who can train 3 times a week. Which split usually suits them?","e":"Full-body training 3 times a week lets them practice each movement pattern several times a week and improve quickly.","o":["Full-body training in each session","Chest day, arms day, shoulders day – no legs","Training one muscle group per week","Only cardio in the first weeks, no strength"]},"program-1wuxgbo":{"q":"What is a superset?","e":"A superset saves time. You can pair opposing muscles (row + press) or the same muscle to increase the effort.","o":["Two exercises in a row with no rest","One extra-long set of 30 reps","One set with the maximum possible weight","A day with two separate workouts"]},"program-f7xcb8":{"q":"What is a deload?","e":"A deload week helps reduce accumulated fatigue, and performance often improves afterwards.","o":["A planned reduction in load for recovery","A complete two-month break from training","Increasing the weight every set for a whole week","A week of extra-heavy training before a peak"]},"program-i7pan4":{"q":"Rest days are part of the training program, because much of the adaptation happens during recovery.","e":"True. Training creates the stimulus, and recovery (sleep, nutrition, rest) lets the body improve."},"program-174tatp":{"q":"What is the World Health Organization (WHO) recommendation for adults regarding moderate-intensity aerobic activity?","e":"Alternatively 75–150 minutes at vigorous intensity, or a combination of the two.","o":["150–300 minutes per week","30 minutes per week","75 minutes per week at moderate intensity only","60 minutes every day"]},"program-2eqgey":{"q":"How many days a week of strength training for the major muscle groups does the WHO recommend for adults?","e":"The recommendation is at least two days a week of strength training, in addition to aerobic activity.","o":["At least two days","Once a week","Every day, with no rest","There's no recommendation for strength"]},"program-1tzdfhs":{"q":"What is the difference between linear and undulating periodization?","e":"For example in undulating: a heavy day (3–5 reps), a medium day (8–10) and a light day (12–15) in the same week.","o":["In linear, intensity rises from phase to phase; in undulating it changes frequently","In linear you change exercises every workout; in undulating the exercises are completely fixed","Linear suits only runners, and undulating suits only weightlifters","No real difference – both raise intensity at the same rate and in the same way"]},"program-1i9llaf":{"q":"Which variable is considered one of the main predictors of hypertrophy, when sets are close enough to failure?","e":"Within a reasonable range, more quality sets per week usually lead to more growth – as long as recovery allows.","o":["The number of effective sets per muscle per week","The exact tempo of each rep in the set","The type of equipment – machine or free weight","The time of day the workout is done"]},"program-1cvw699":{"q":"What is circuit training?","e":"Circuit training combines strength and endurance and is especially efficient when time is limited or in groups.","o":["Exercises in sequence with short rest, for several rounds","Continuous aerobic training at a steady pace for a long time","One exercise repeated throughout the whole workout","Long static stretches between every two exercises"]},"program-14uaae1":{"q":"What is HIIT?","e":"HIIT is time-efficient but demanding. Beginners should build up to it gradually.","o":["Intense intervals alternating with recovery","Slow, continuous walking for a long time at a low heart rate","Maximal strength training with single reps only","High-intensity, fast static stretching"]},"program-1xemc0a":{"q":"Last week a trainee did 3 sets × 8 reps at 50 kg and completed them easily. What is a reasonable progression?","e":"Small, consistent progression beats big jumps that hurt technique.","o":["Add 2.5 kg or one rep per set","Double the weight to 100 kg","Replace the exercise with another one","Lower the weight to 40 kg"]},"program-1iuoiw3":{"q":"What is the 'interference effect' in concurrent training?","e":"The effect depends on volume, type of aerobic work and timing. In most general-fitness programs, combining them is recommended.","o":["High aerobic volume may slightly limit gains in strength and power","Aerobic training always directly improves strength and muscle growth","Strength training completely prevents any improvement in cardio-respiratory endurance","There's no mutual effect between aerobic and strength training"]},"program-1jdq2sf":{"q":"What is a recommended, common order in a strength workout?","e":"Heavy compound exercises are done while the trainee is fresh, and isolation exercises come after.","o":["Warm-up → compound → isolation → cool-down","Isolation → warm-up → compound → cool-down","Long static stretches → sprints → strength","Order doesn't matter, any order works equally"]},"program-4cjy75":{"q":"Briefly build a training week for a 35-year-old beginner whose goal is general fitness, 3 days a week.","e":"A good answer includes: non-consecutive days, basic movement patterns, moderate intensity, cardio, and gradual progression.","a":"3 full-body workouts on non-consecutive days (e.g. Sun, Tue, Thu). Each workout: warm-up, a leg exercise (goblet squat), a hip hinge (dumbbell RDL), a push (modified push-ups or a press), a pull (row), core (plank). 2–3 sets of 8–12 reps at RPE 6–7, and finish with 10–15 minutes of moderate cardio. Walking on the other days. Gradual progression every week."},"program-9bpm4p":{"q":"For two weeks a trainee has had declining performance, persistent fatigue and poor sleep. Which factors would you check and which adjustments would you consider?","e":"A good answer addresses load, recovery and sleep, gradual adjustments, and medical referral when needed.","a":"Check training load and frequency, volume and intensity, rest days, sleep, nutrition, stress and changes in routine. Consider a temporary load reduction (deload), recovery and monitoring. If there are unusual or persistent signs – refer for an appropriate medical evaluation."},"trx-1b9hpa2":{"q":"In a TRX row, what usually happens when the body leans further back (closer to horizontal)?","e":"The more horizontal the body is relative to the ground, the more of the body weight 'hangs' on the arms.","o":["The exercise gets harder","The exercise gets easier","No change in load","The load moves to the legs only"]},"trx-q9pl18":{"q":"In TRX, which type of exercises are usually done facing the anchor point?","e":"Facing the anchor = pull (row, biceps curl). Back to the anchor = push (chest press, triceps extension).","o":["Pulling exercises, like rows","Pushing exercises, like chest presses","Leg exercises only","Stretches only"]},"trx-1eljibd":{"q":"In a TRX chest press (back to the anchor), what makes the exercise harder?","e":"Body angle determines how much of the body weight is pushed. Standing upright makes it easier, a bigger lean makes it harder.","o":["Increasing the lean so the body is more horizontal","Standing more upright and closer to the anchor","Shortening the set and reducing the reps","Gripping the straps harder"]},"trx-qd97fo":{"q":"How does reducing the base of support (e.g. standing on one leg) affect a TRX exercise?","e":"A narrower base = less stability = more work for the core and stabilizers.","o":["Increases the stability demand and makes it harder","Makes it much easier because there's less weight to carry","Has no effect on difficulty","Completely eliminates core work"]},"trx-gq2fdz":{"q":"Before a TRX workout it's important to make sure the anchor point is stable and the straps are intact.","e":"True. Checking equipment is part of the instructor's responsibility – before every session."},"trx-1c0chso":{"q":"What indicates proper form in a TRX row?","e":"The core holds a straight line from ankle to shoulder, and the back does the work.","o":["A straight body, shoulder blades back and controlled movement","Sagging hips and pulling with the legs to help the arms","Shoulders rising toward the ears","Slack straps at the end of each rep"]},"trx-q96zpk":{"q":"What is the problem with slack TRX straps during an exercise?","e":"In most exercises the straps are kept taut throughout the movement.","o":["Loss of tension and jerking that leads to uncontrolled movement","They always make the exercise harder and more tiring","No problem as long as you hold the handles","They improve stability and make the core's job easier"]},"trx-19who64":{"q":"With TRX you can also train the lower body and core, not only the upper body.","e":"True. For example: assisted squats, lunges with the rear foot in the strap, pikes, and planks with the feet in the straps."},"trx-129aoib":{"q":"In a TRX pike with the feet in the straps, what mainly works?","e":"Pulling the hips up requires a strong core, and the hands on the floor challenge shoulder stability.","o":["The core and hip flexors, with shoulder stabilization","Only the calf and ankle muscles","Mainly the biceps and forearms","The knee and hip extensors, mainly in the lowering phase"]},"trx-1v0uj4v":{"q":"What characterizes a third-class lever, like the elbow in a biceps curl?","e":"Most levers in the body are third-class: the muscle must exert a large force, but you get fast, wide movement.","o":["The effort is between the axis and the load – speed and range at the expense of force","The load is between the axis and the effort – a large mechanical advantage","The axis is between the effort and the load – like a seesaw, balancing force and speed","It's the most force-efficient lever in the body"]},"trx-lknfnv":{"q":"What affects the torque around a joint?","e":"Torque = force × the perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation to the line of action of the force.","o":["The size of the force, the moment arm and the angle","The size of the force only, regardless of distance","The length of the moment arm only","Only the speed of movement and the duration of the contraction"]},"trx-wodtzp":{"q":"Why are crunches with the arms overhead harder than with the arms by the sides?","e":"Same weight, greater distance from the axis = more torque. A simple way to make an exercise harder without equipment.","o":["The center of mass moves away from the axis and the moment arm increases","Arms overhead add weight to the whole body","The arm muscles do a large part of the work","They're not really harder, it's just a feeling"]},"trx-16ml591":{"q":"Explain how you can make a TRX row easier or harder without changing the strap length.","e":"The key: changing the body angle relative to the ground, plus changing the base of support, tempo and unilateral work.","a":"Change the body angle and foot position: a more upright body makes it easier, a more horizontal body makes it harder. You can also change the base of support (feet together or one leg), add a pause at the top, slow the tempo or do it single-arm."},"trx-k44rkf":{"q":"A 68-year-old trainee is starting with TRX. How would you adapt the load, stability and progression?","e":"Look for: medical screening, easy angles, a wide base, controlled movement, safe entry and exit, and gradual progression.","a":"First make sure there is a health declaration and medical clearance if needed. Start at upright angles with a wide base of support, with simple exercises (row, assisted squat, light chest press), using slow, controlled movement. Pay attention to safe entry and exit from each exercise. Progress gradually: first technique and endurance, then harder angles and a narrower base. Include balance work and monitor sensations and fatigue."},"scenarios-ijmelo":{"q":"A trainee reports a sharp, sudden knee pain during a squat. What is the most appropriate action?","e":"Never continue through sharp pain. A fitness instructor doesn't diagnose – stop and refer to the right professional when needed.","o":["Stop, find out what happened and refer for evaluation as needed","Add weight to test whether the knee is stable","Ask them to continue and see if the pain passes","Switch immediately to jumping exercises to warm up the knee"]},"scenarios-xkn6d3":{"q":"A trainee returns after an injury with a document of restrictions from a medical professional. What is right to do?","e":"Work according to the instructions the trainee received, and don't provide treatment or rehabilitation without proper certification.","o":["Build the training according to the instructions and ask for clarification when needed","Check yourself whether the injury has fully healed, and decide based on that","Ignore the restrictions as long as they feel good","Promise them your training will heal the injury"]},"scenarios-157a1eh":{"q":"A new trainee arrives and says: 'I want to lose weight, fast.' What is the first step?","e":"Without information you can't build a safe, suitable program. A good conversation also builds trust and commitment.","o":["An intake conversation: goals, experience, health declaration and habits","A 6-workout-per-week program from the first week, with no conversation","Recommending daily fasting and a sharp cut in carbs","Recommending weight-loss supplements to speed up results"]},"scenarios-4lkkiz":{"q":"A gym member is doing an exercise dangerously. How should you approach them?","e":"A respectful, professional approach increases the chance they'll accept the correction and feel comfortable asking again.","o":["Approach politely, offer a short correction and demonstrate","Shout at them from a distance so they stop before getting hurt","Ignore it – it's not your responsibility","Film it and post it as an example of what not to do"]},"scenarios-173dpgl":{"q":"A trainee says she's pregnant and wants to keep training. What is right?","e":"Medical guidelines support adapted activity in a normal pregnancy. Adjustments are made by stage, how she feels and the instructions she received.","o":["In a normal pregnancy it's recommended, with her caregiver's approval and adjustments","She must stop all physical activity immediately until after the birth","She can continue exactly the same training, with no change or adjustment","She should switch to a more intense sport to keep fit"]},"scenarios-y9qnra":{"q":"A trainee hasn't come for two weeks and sounds embarrassed. What's a good approach?","e":"Adherence is built on a sense of success and belonging. Small goals and support work better than pressure.","o":["Reach out empathetically, find out what's hard and suggest small goals","Send a message saying they're letting you and themselves down, to motivate them","Give up on them and focus on those who come","Schedule 5 workouts a week for them to 'catch up'"]},"scenarios-o77kd4":{"q":"A group class has 12 participants at different levels. How do you adapt the workout?","e":"That way everyone works at a level that challenges them – without anyone feeling they don't belong.","o":["Give every exercise an easier and a harder version","Match the pace to the strongest participant","Adapt the whole class only to the weakest","Ask those who struggle to sit on the side and watch"]},"scenarios-16dmgg2":{"q":"A trainee complains of muscle soreness two days after their first workout. What is a reasonable explanation?","e":"DOMS is common after new activity. Sharp joint pain, significant swelling or dark urine require medical referral.","o":["Delayed-onset muscle soreness (DOMS), which passes within a few days","A muscle tear, so they must go for a medical check immediately","A sign they're not suited to training and should stop for good","Lactic acid left in the muscle that will stay there for several weeks"]},"scenarios-42fyk5":{"q":"Two days after a very hard workout, a trainee reports extreme muscle pain, swelling and dark urine. What is suspected?","e":"Dark (cola-colored) urine together with unusual pain and swelling are warning signs. This medical condition requires immediate evaluation.","o":["Rhabdomyolysis – refer for urgent medical evaluation","Normal DOMS – keep training as usual tomorrow","Mild dehydration only – drink and keep training","Creatine deficiency – start taking a supplement immediately"]},"scenarios-d4t5xg":{"q":"A 70-year-old trainee wants to improve stability and prevent falls. What is important to include in the program?","e":"Leg strength and balance are among the most important factors in reducing fall risk in older age.","o":["Balance, leg strength and everyday movements","Slow walking only, with no strength exercises","Stretching only, to improve range of motion","Sprints and depth jumps from the first week"]},"scenarios-1048nfw":{"q":"When a trainee shares personal or medical information, it must be kept confidential.","e":"True. Protecting privacy is a basic part of professional ethics and of building trust."},"scenarios-swmca5":{"q":"A trainee insists on lifting too heavy a weight, and their technique falls apart. What is most effective?","e":"When the trainee understands why and sees the difference, they become a partner in the process, not just someone taking orders.","o":["Explain, reduce the weight, show them on video and build a gradual goal","Let them continue with the weight, so they learn from their own mistakes","Add more weight to prove they're not ready yet","Refuse to keep coaching them until they agree to reduce the weight"]},"scenarios-18lnc1x":{"q":"A trainee asks you about using anabolic steroids. What is the professional response?","e":"This is outside your scope of practice and carries real health risks. Focus on what is in scope: training, habits and consistency.","o":["Explain the risks, don't advise on use and refer to a doctor","Recommend a low dose considered 'safe' for beginners, with blood tests","Refer them to someone who sells and knows how to guide use","Tell them that at the right dose there's no real risk"]},"scenarios-ch6o93":{"q":"A new trainee feels dizzy, has chest pressure and unusual shortness of breath during a workout. Describe your sequence of actions.","e":"The key: immediate stop, calling urgent help, continuous supervision and acting within your training.","a":"Stop the workout immediately, sit or lay her down depending on her condition, call for urgent medical help (in Israel: MDA 101), don't leave her alone, bring a defibrillator if one is available, and act according to first-aid training and the dispatcher's instructions until the team arrives."},"scenarios-1t3cre3":{"q":"A beginner with a busy job asks for a program 'like the Instagram influencer's' – 6 workouts a week. What would you do?","e":"Look for: listening, a factual explanation, fitting ability and time, measurable goals and gradual progression.","a":"Listen to the motivation and goal behind the request, gently explain that someone else's program doesn't necessarily fit their ability, time and recovery, and offer a realistic program of 2–3 workouts a week they can stick to. Set measurable goals together, and increase frequency gradually if they're consistent and recovering well."},"scenarios-1606k39":{"q":"A trainee insists on continuing an exercise despite sharp pain, because they want to meet the workout goal. How would you handle the conversation and what would you do?","e":"Safety before goals: stop, don't diagnose, refer as needed and adjust the program afterwards.","a":"I'd explain that sharp pain is a reason to stop, not to push through. I'd stop the exercise, ask how they feel without trying to diagnose, and recommend an evaluation by an appropriate medical professional. We'd adjust the goal after the evaluation and according to the instructions they receive – safety comes before the goal."},"safety-esxz1w":{"q":"Which action is within the scope of practice of a fitness instructor who is not a physiotherapist?","e":"A fitness instructor plans and coaches training. Medical or physiotherapy diagnosis and treatment require a licensed professional.","o":["Adapting training to goals, ability and limitations","Diagnosing a muscle tear by the type of pain","Prescribing medical treatment for chronic back pain","Independently managing medical rehabilitation after surgery"]},"safety-wlquxn":{"q":"A trainee asks you to diagnose a persistent shoulder pain and treat it. What is the professional response?","e":"It's important to clarify your scope of practice and refer for a medical or physiotherapy evaluation. You can keep training other areas without pain.","o":["Explain that you don't diagnose, and recommend an appropriate evaluation","Diagnose the problem from the painful movement and build a rehab program","Offer deep massage as treatment every session","Continue all exercises as long as the pain is tolerable"]},"safety-1aayli2":{"q":"When should you ask a trainee for medical clearance before starting training?","e":"A health screening questionnaire (like a health declaration or PAR-Q) is a basic tool for identifying who needs clearance before starting.","o":["When the health declaration shows a risk sign or medical condition","Never – it's the trainee's responsibility only","Only if the trainee is over 80","Only if the trainee asks for it, or is over 70"]},"safety-hzkox9":{"q":"Equipment – cables, benches and locks – should be checked before use.","e":"True. Faulty equipment is a known risk factor, and checking takes seconds."},"safety-mscl4f":{"q":"Why is it important to use collars (clips) on an Olympic bar?","e":"A plate sliding off one side can suddenly tip the bar and cause injury.","o":["To prevent plates sliding and the bar falling","To make the bar easier to lift","To add a little weight to the bar","It matters only with light weights"]},"safety-10zofik":{"q":"A trainee with diabetes starts shaking, sweating and looks confused mid-workout. What is suspected and what do you do?","e":"Fast-acting carbohydrate like juice or sugar. If they're unconscious or can't swallow – give nothing by mouth and call for help immediately.","o":["Suspected low blood sugar: stop, give fast-acting carbohydrate if conscious, and call for help if there's no improvement","Normal fatigue: continue the workout and let them recover while moving","Dehydration: just give a few glasses of water, sit them in the shade and continue at low intensity","Suspected high blood sugar: increase the intensity to lower the sugar level"]},"safety-1facima":{"q":"Which signs raise suspicion of heat stroke?","e":"Heat stroke is life-threatening: call for help, move to a shaded, cool place and start cooling according to guidelines.","o":["High body temperature, confusion and hot skin – an emergency","Mild chills at the start of training and cold hands","Heavy sweating on a hot day","Muscle soreness the day after training"]},"safety-1b0xjtk":{"q":"What is the first action when someone collapses and is unresponsive?","e":"Rapid recognition, calling for help, CPR and early use of a defibrillator greatly improve survival.","o":["Make sure the scene is safe, check response and breathing, call for help and a defibrillator","Give water, lift the head and wait a few minutes for them to wake up, and only then call for help","Sit them in front of a fan and let them rest until they feel better","Check the pulse for a full minute before any other action"]},"safety-p7wy47":{"q":"A fitness instructor may advise a trainee to stop a medication prescribed by a doctor if they think it hurts performance.","e":"False. Any change in medication is made only with the treating physician. The instructor can suggest the trainee consult their doctor."},"safety-17s80wy":{"q":"When must you stop a workout and refer for a medical check?","e":"These are cardiovascular warning signs. In a severe case – call urgent help immediately.","o":["Chest pain, dizziness, unusual shortness of breath or an irregular heartbeat","When the trainee sweats a lot and feels generally tired at the end of the workout","When the trainee says the exercise is hard","When the heart rate rises during exercise"]},"safety-1tec1rs":{"q":"A trainee asks whether to take a certain supplement. What is right?","e":"Personalizing supplements – especially for people with a medical condition or on medication – is outside a fitness instructor's scope.","o":["Give general information and refer to a doctor or dietitian","Prescribe an exact dose by body weight","Promise the supplement will solve their problem","Sell them the supplement at a discount"]},"safety-bolbzx":{"q":"A trainee asks if they can train with a fever. What is the standard recommendation?","e":"Exercising while sick with a fever strains the body and can be dangerous. The return should be gradual.","o":["Don't train with a fever, and return gradually after recovery","Train harder to 'sweat it out' and recover faster","Do only intense cardio","There's no limitation"]},"safety-mannwx":{"q":"What is a fitness instructor's scope regarding nutrition?","e":"Therapeutic nutrition is the domain of clinical dietitians. The instructor can encourage good habits and refer.","o":["General information about healthy habits, without a therapeutic diet","Building a therapeutic diet for every disease or medical condition","Diagnosing nutritional deficiencies from external signs","Prescribing iron and vitamin supplements to trainees"]},"safety-1h1wjis":{"q":"The gym's air conditioning broke on a very hot day, and you have a class with participants of different ages. How would you handle it?","e":"Look for: risk assessment, lower intensity, drinking, supervising vulnerable people and recognizing warning signs.","a":"Assess the risk and consider canceling or moving to a cooler place. If continuing: reduce intensity and duration, add frequent drink breaks, tell everyone to work at their own pace, pay special attention to older people and those with medical conditions, recognize signs of heat strain (dizziness, nausea, confusion), and stop anyone who feels unwell immediately."},"nutrition-8kqli0":{"q":"How many calories are in one gram of protein or carbohydrate?","e":"Protein and carbohydrate – 4 calories per gram. Fat – 9. Alcohol – 7.","o":["4","9","7","2"]},"nutrition-l1jp8t":{"q":"How many calories are in one gram of fat?","e":"Fat is the most energy-dense macronutrient – more than twice protein and carbohydrate.","o":["9","4","7","2"]},"nutrition-rlaqr7":{"q":"How many calories are in one gram of alcohol?","e":"Alcohol provides energy but almost no other nutritional value.","o":["7","4","9","0"]},"nutrition-63j6oc":{"q":"What is the research-accepted daily protein range for strength trainees who want to build muscle?","e":"This is a range accepted in the literature. Individual needs depend on goal, health status and preferences.","o":["About 1.6–2.2 g per kg","About 0.2–0.4 g per kg","About 4–5 g per kg","No special need for protein"]},"nutrition-1vrtfuy":{"q":"Weight loss requires a calorie deficit over time.","e":"True. It can be reached in different ways; what matters is that the way is healthy and sustainable."},"nutrition-5w25uj":{"q":"What is a 'complete' protein?","e":"Animal sources are usually complete. In plant foods you can combine sources (e.g. legumes and grains).","o":["Protein with all the essential amino acids","Protein eaten as part of a complete, balanced meal","Only protein that comes from protein powder","Protein with no fat at all"]},"nutrition-ij0ow2":{"q":"A meal has 30 g protein, 50 g carbohydrate and 10 g fat. About how many calories does it have?","e":"30×4 + 50×4 + 10×9 = 120 + 200 + 90 = 410 calories.","o":["410","360","500","90"]},"nutrition-1rnl65o":{"q":"What is BMR?","e":"BMR is the largest part of daily energy expenditure for most people.","o":["The energy the body uses at complete rest","Body mass relative to height (BMI)","The energy the body expends in training only","Metabolic rate during maximal exertion"]},"nutrition-152ncr1":{"q":"What is TDEE?","e":"TDEE includes BMR, the energy of exercise and daily movement, and the energy for digesting food.","o":["Total daily expenditure: rest, activity and digestion","The energy the body expends in workouts only","Metabolic rate at complete rest only","The calories recommended to eat to lose weight"]},"nutrition-y9edqj":{"q":"Creatine monohydrate is one of the most researched supplements for improving strength performance.","e":"True. Still – for people with a medical condition, especially kidney-related, the decision should be made with a doctor."},"nutrition-1msclkw":{"q":"When is it right to refer a trainee to a clinical dietitian?","e":"The instructor can give general information, but medical and complex situations require a licensed professional.","o":["With a medical condition, pregnancy or a suspected eating problem","Every time they ask what to eat before training","Never – the instructor handles all nutrition","Only when they want to gain muscle mass"]},"nutrition-oky87j":{"q":"What is the main energy source in intense exercise?","e":"As intensity rises, the body relies more on carbohydrates as a fast energy source.","o":["Carbohydrates (glycogen and glucose)","Stored fat only, even in maximal exertion","Protein from muscle","Ketones from the liver"]},"nutrition-1wgmo0m":{"q":"What is the main role of dietary fiber?","e":"Fiber is found in vegetables, fruits, legumes and whole grains.","o":["Contributing to satiety and digestive function","Providing a large amount of quality protein","Providing fast energy before exercise","Directly building muscle after training"]},"nutrition-1k7t0rh":{"q":"There is a 30-minute 'anabolic window' after training, and if you don't eat protein exactly then the workout is wasted.","e":"False. Total daily protein and its distribution through the day matter much more than exact timing in the minutes after training."},"nutrition-1rsl4cf":{"q":"What is a simple, practical sign of hydration status?","e":"Very dark urine can indicate a lack of fluids. Weighing before and after a long workout helps estimate fluid loss.","o":["Urine color – pale yellow is a good sign","Feeling hungry during the day","Morning resting heart rate","Sweat in training – the more you sweat, the better hydrated you are"]},"nutrition-1b0rebq":{"q":"A trainee with type 2 diabetes who takes medication asks you to build a weight-loss diet. What would you say and do?","e":"Look for: scope of practice, referral to a dietitian and doctor, what is within the instructor's role, and awareness of hypoglycemia risk.","a":"I'd explain that building a diet for a medical condition with medication is outside my scope, and refer them to a clinical dietitian and their doctor. Within my scope: build adapted training, learn the instructions they received, watch for low-blood-sugar signs during training, make sure they have fast-acting carbohydrate available, and encourage general healthy habits. I'd be happy to coordinate with their care team."},"special-1yoidze":{"q":"Strength training during adolescence stunts growth.","e":"False. Adapted, supervised strength training with good technique is considered safe and beneficial for teenagers."},"special-47axqc":{"q":"What is true about strength training for teenagers?","e":"Benefits include stronger bones, better movement skills and a lower risk of sports injuries.","o":["Safe and beneficial with supervision and good technique","Completely forbidden until age 18","Allowed only with maximal weights","Suitable only for boys; girls should stick to cardio"]},"special-1m56tw6":{"q":"Which exercises call for caution during pregnancy, especially from the second trimester?","e":"When lying on the back for long, the uterus may press on large blood vessels. Contact sports and fall risk are dangerous because of the risk of trauma.","o":["Prolonged lying on the back, and risk of falls or contact","Brisk walking on flat ground and gentle slopes","Moderate-intensity stationary cycling","Breathing and pelvic floor exercises"]},"special-rtzzeq":{"q":"A trainee with high blood pressure. What should they be careful about in training?","e":"Aerobic and moderate strength training are usually recommended. Breath-holding and maximal effort raise blood pressure sharply.","o":["Prolonged breath-holding and maximal efforts","Brisk walking at a conversational pace, mainly in the morning","Moderate strength training with continuous breathing","A gradual warm-up before each workout"]},"special-13ux3b2":{"q":"Which type of activity is considered beneficial for bone health, e.g. in people with osteoporosis?","e":"Bone responds to load. At the same time, avoid deep loaded trunk flexion and fall risk, and follow the care provider's instructions.","o":["Resistance training and weight-bearing activity","Swimming only, to reduce load on the bone","Complete rest until the bone gets stronger","Deep trunk flexion with added weight"]},"special-78b91l":{"q":"Which ability is especially important for staying independent in older age?","e":"Getting up from a chair, climbing stairs and preventing falls all depend on leg strength and balance.","o":["Leg strength and power, and balance","Being able to run a full marathon at a steady pace","Extreme flexibility in all joints","Large muscle mass in the arms only"]},"special-6bwdec":{"q":"A trainee takes beta blockers. What does this mean for monitoring training intensity?","e":"Beta blockers slow the heart rate, so normal heart-rate zones don't accurately reflect effort.","o":["Heart rate will be lower than expected – an effort scale is better","Heart rate will be higher than expected – slow down and reduce intensity","The drug has no effect on heart rate during exercise","They must not train while taking it"]},"special-fua8tj":{"q":"A rating of perceived exertion scale (RPE / Borg scale) can be used to monitor intensity when heart rate measurement is unreliable.","e":"True. It's simple, free and always available – and especially suited to people taking medications that affect heart rate."},"special-td535g":{"q":"A trainee with asthma. What is right?","e":"Most people with controlled asthma can train. A gradual warm-up and an inhaler on hand are part of the routine.","o":["An inhaler on hand, a gradual warm-up and stopping at signs of an attack","Avoiding any activity that raises the heart rate, even with an inhaler on hand","Training at maximal intensity to strengthen the lungs","Skipping the warm-up so as not to tire them in advance"]},"special-17d3af8":{"q":"A trainee with significant excess weight is starting to train. What opening activity usually suits them?","e":"Start with activity the joints 'tolerate' well, build small successes and add strength gradually.","o":["Low-impact activity – walking, cycling or water","Long runs on asphalt from the first week","Box jumps to burn more calories","Uphill sprints to build fitness fast"]},"special-16ykryh":{"q":"What is the 'talk test'?","e":"If it's already hard to say more than a few words, the intensity is high. A very simple tool for any population.","o":["You can talk in sentences but not sing – moderate intensity","You can sing easily – a sign the intensity is high and you should slow down","You can't talk at all – a sign the intensity is light","You count breaths per minute and calculate heart rate from them"]},"special-103ucqm":{"q":"A trainee 3 months after giving birth wants to return to jumping and running. What would you consider first?","e":"Look for: medical clearance, pelvic floor and abdominal separation, referral when needed, and gradual progression from strength to high-impact work.","a":"Make sure she has clearance from her care provider to return to activity. Ask about pelvic floor symptoms (leakage, heaviness) and abdominal separation, and refer to a pelvic floor physiotherapist as needed. Build a gradual return: core, glute and leg strength and stability first, then low-impact work, and only then jumping and running. Monitor symptoms, sleep and fatigue."},"psych-11uxf9":{"q":"What is intrinsic motivation?","e":"Intrinsic motivation tends to be more stable over time – so it's worth helping trainees find activities they enjoy.","o":["Doing something for the enjoyment or meaning in it","Doing something for a reward, money or recognition","Doing something because of pressure from others","Doing something out of fear of punishment or criticism"]},"psych-1kj8v3j":{"q":"According to self-determination theory (SDT), which three psychological needs strengthen motivation?","e":"An instructor can support all three: give choice (autonomy), plan successes (competence) and create personal and group connection (relatedness).","o":["Autonomy, competence and relatedness","Money, recognition and a sense of power over others","Fear, guilt and shame","Speed, strength and flexibility"]},"psych-117j3h4":{"q":"What does the SMART goal acronym stand for?","e":"For example: 'reach 10 full push-ups within 8 weeks' instead of 'get stronger'.","o":["Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound","Small, Quick, Brave, Serious and Competitive – against other trainees","Simple, Agreed, Personal, Fun and Social","Weekly, Monthly, Yearly, Personal and Group"]},"psych-r5bucp":{"q":"What is the difference between a process goal and an outcome goal?","e":"'Train 3 times a week' is a process goal. 'Lose 5 kg' is an outcome goal. Combining them works best.","o":["Process = actions under your control; outcome = the final result","No difference – two names for the same type of goal","An outcome goal is always better because it's more measurable","A process goal suits only professional athletes"]},"psych-1m4us7c":{"q":"In a well-known study by Lally and colleagues on habit formation, the median time until a behavior became automatic was about 66 days, with large variation between people.","e":"True. For some participants it took less than three weeks and for others much longer. A single slip doesn't 'reset' the process."},"psych-1y4e5k9":{"q":"Building a new habit takes exactly 21 days for everyone.","e":"False. This is a common myth. The time varies greatly between people and between types of behavior."},"psych-17t3i14":{"q":"What is an 'implementation intention'?","e":"Planning when, where and how significantly increases the chance of doing what you planned.","o":["A 'when X happens – I will do Y' plan","A general decision to start training sometime","An organized list of exercises for the whole week","A fitness test that measures the starting point"]},"psych-wtj0ki":{"q":"According to Albert Bandura, what is the strongest source of self-efficacy?","e":"When a person succeeds themselves, they believe they can do it again. So it's important to build small, early successes for beginners.","o":["Personal mastery experiences","Watching others succeed","Verbal persuasion","Emotional and physiological state"]},"psych-1ffujyw":{"q":"A beginner says: 'I just can't do it.' What will most strengthen their self-efficacy?","e":"Early, documented success ('two weeks ago you did 5, today 8') changes the story they tell themselves.","o":["Tasks they'll succeed at, with gradually increasing difficulty","The hardest exercise right away, so they see they can","Comparing them to strong trainees to motivate them","Telling them it's all in their head and carrying on"]},"psych-1blznnj":{"q":"According to the stages-of-change model, which stage is a person in who is thinking about starting to exercise in the coming months but hasn't started yet?","e":"At this stage the person is undecided. It helps to talk about the benefits and barriers, not to push them straight into an intensive program.","o":["Contemplation","Precontemplation","Action","Maintenance"]},"psych-tnkejy":{"q":"In the stages-of-change model, when is a person considered to be in the 'maintenance' stage?","e":"Even in maintenance there's a risk of slipping, so keep reinforcing habits and plan ahead for hard periods (vacations, busy work).","o":["When the behavior has lasted more than six months","Right after their first workout","When they're just starting to think about change","When they stop training for a while"]},"psych-1u8s58m":{"q":"Which of the following is an open question?","e":"An open question invites the trainee to tell you more and gives you much more information about their motives.","o":["What's most important for you to achieve in training?","You want to lose weight by summer, right?","Have you ever trained in a gym?","Do you have any pain?"]},"psych-vu3m3l":{"q":"What is the purpose of 'reflective listening' in a motivational conversation?","e":"For example: 'It sounds like you want to feel more energetic, but work takes up your whole evening.'","o":["Saying back in your own words what they said, so they feel understood","Repeating their words verbatim to fill time and show attention","Gently showing them exactly where they're wrong","Steering the conversation to a topic more comfortable for both of you"]},"psych-c9i8i2":{"q":"What does the Yerkes–Dodson law say about arousal and performance?","e":"Fine, complex tasks usually need lower arousal than simple strength tasks.","o":["Inverted U: best performance at moderate arousal","The higher the pressure, the better the performance","There's no link between arousal and performance","Good performance is possible only in complete calm"]},"psych-1ekneuh":{"q":"Regular physical activity is associated with improved mood and reduced symptoms of anxiety and depression.","e":"True. This is one of the well-established benefits of physical activity – but it doesn't replace professional treatment when it's needed."},"psych-hl3poc":{"q":"What is positive reinforcement?","e":"Immediate, specific reinforcement ('your back stayed straight on every rep!') works better than a general 'nice'.","o":["Adding something pleasant after a desired behavior","Removing something unpleasant after a desired behavior","Punishing an undesired behavior","Ignoring a behavior so it disappears"]},"psych-13tm7ud":{"q":"Which feedback is more effective when learning a movement?","e":"Too many corrections confuse. Focus on one important correction at a time.","o":["Short, specific feedback on one thing","A general 'nice' after every rep","A list of 7 corrections at once","Silence until the end of the whole set"]},"psych-1e824u":{"q":"A trainee says: 'I missed one workout, so this week is already gone.' What is this thinking pattern called?","e":"It helps to normalize slips: 'One missed workout doesn't cancel the whole week. What's your next session?'","o":["'All-or-nothing' thinking","Positive, realistic thinking","Setting SMART goals","Intrinsic motivation"]},"psych-1ht6xm6":{"q":"A trainee missed a week of training and feels like a failure. What's a good response?","e":"A quick, successful return to routine matters more than 'making up' what was missed.","o":["Normalize it, come back with an easy workout and plan the next one together","Tell them they're not serious and ask them to recommit to a full program","Double their workouts this week to catch up","Suggest they start over only next month"]},"psych-19cybnh":{"q":"What is 'habit stacking'?","e":"The existing habit serves as a fixed 'cue' that reminds you to do the new one.","o":["Attaching a new habit to an existing one","Lots of new habits from day one","Changing all habits at once","A special kind of circuit training"]},"psych-11gel4o":{"q":"What is 'social facilitation'?","e":"So a beginner learning a new movement may prefer a quiet place, while an experienced trainee may 'wake up' in front of an audience.","o":["An audience improves familiar tasks and may impair new ones","The presence of others always impairs performance, in any task","The presence of others doesn't affect performance","Only a coach's presence affects performance"]},"psych-1juppyr":{"q":"Extrinsic motivation, like prizes and competitions, always harms intrinsic motivation.","e":"False. Rewards can help, especially at the start. They may harm when experienced as control and pressure, less so when they provide feedback and recognition."},"psych-1p8otry":{"q":"According to the habit loop model (cue → routine → reward), what helps build a training habit?","e":"The more fixed the cue and the simpler the routine, the easier it becomes automatic.","o":["A fixed cue, a simple routine and an immediate reward","A different time each time, with no planning","A complicated, full program from day one","Waiting until you feel like training"]},"psych-1bsmtx":{"q":"What characterizes a 'growth mindset' as described by Carol Dweck?","e":"In practice it shows in phrases like 'you haven't succeeded – yet' and in focusing on the process.","o":["The belief that abilities develop through effort and learning","The belief that talent is fixed from birth and doesn't change","Avoiding challenges so as not to fail","Constantly comparing yourself to others' achievements"]},"psych-k6pkrb":{"q":"Which compliment contributes more to a trainee's consistency?","e":"Praise for something within the trainee's control reinforces the behavior you want to see again.","o":["'You came three times this week – excellent'","'You were born with really good genetics for training'","'You're just naturally talented'","'You're the best trainee here'"]},"psych-1eh11f":{"q":"Mental imagery of performing a movement can be used as an aid to learning and performance.","e":"True. Athletes use imagery to prepare and practice. It complements physical training – it doesn't replace it."},"psych-iof3vf":{"q":"What characterizes a professional approach when talking with a trainee who hesitates to start?","e":"When the choice is the trainee's, their commitment is higher too.","o":["Listen, ask what matters to them and let them choose","Scare them about the health consequences of inactivity","Give a long lecture on all the benefits","Decide a 5-day program for them"]},"psych-ak5ut":{"q":"A trainee tells you: 'I don't have time to train.' How would you run a motivational conversation with them?","e":"Look for: open questions, reflection, respect for their choice, a small realistic step and planning 'when and where'.","a":"Ask open questions about their routine and what matters to them, reflect the dilemma ('it sounds like you want to, but the days are packed'), find out what has worked for them before, and offer them to choose a small, realistic step – for example two 20-minute workouts or a short home session. Phrase an implementation intention together (when and where), and set a check-in in a week."},"psych-11rf0fi":{"q":"Explain how the three needs of self-determination theory can be applied in a group class.","e":"A good answer gives a practical example for each of the three needs.","a":"Autonomy: give choices between exercise versions or music, and explain why each thing is done. Competence: regressions and progressions for each exercise, specific feedback and tracking progress. Relatedness: call participants by name, partner work, a supportive atmosphere and celebrating group achievements."},"trx-1iaajqe":{"q":"What type of lever is shown in the figure?","e":"Example in the body: nodding the head – the neck joint is the axis, the weight of the head is in front and the neck extensors are behind.","o":["A first-class lever – the axis is between the effort and the load","A second-class lever – the load is between the axis and the effort","A third-class lever – the effort is between the axis and the load","It's not a lever"]},"trx-1iaajqf":{"q":"What type of lever is shown in the figure?","e":"A common example: rising onto the toes – the foot pivots around the toes, body weight is in the middle and the calf pulls on the heel.","o":["A second-class lever – the load is between the axis and the effort","A first-class lever – the axis in the middle, between the load and the effort","A third-class lever – the effort in the middle","A double lever"]},"trx-v1kn2t":{"q":"What type of lever is shown in the figure – the most common type in the body?","e":"The elbow curl is a classic example: the elbow is the axis, the biceps attaches close to it, and the dumbbell is in the hand.","o":["Third class","First class","Second class","There are no levers in the body"]},"trx-1wu6ktl":{"q":"Two trainees hold the same dumbbell. Who has the greater torque around the shoulder (the marked point)?","e":"The moment arm is the horizontal distance from the shoulder to the dumbbell. When the arm is horizontal, that distance is maximal.","o":["Trainee 2 – the arm is horizontal","Trainee 1 – the arm is close to the body","The torque is the same for both","Can't tell without weighing the dumbbell"]},"anatomy-1cbtgjz":{"q":"Which plane does the dashed line in the figure mark?","e":"Viewed from the front, a vertical line in the middle of the body divides it into right and left sides – that's the sagittal plane.","o":["The sagittal plane – divides into right and left","The frontal plane – divides into front and back","The transverse plane – divides into upper and lower","No plane"]},"anatomy-1jw0gzz":{"q":"Which plane does the dashed line in the figure (side view) mark?","e":"Viewed from the side, a vertical line divides the body into front and back parts – that's the frontal plane.","o":["The frontal plane","The sagittal plane","The transverse plane","An oblique plane"]},"anatomy-19ybpcz":{"q":"Which plane does the dashed line in the figure mark?","e":"The transverse (horizontal) plane is the plane of rotational movements.","o":["The transverse plane","The sagittal plane","The frontal plane","The anterior plane"]},"technique-47lyq4":{"q":"What is the main mistake in the squat shown in the figure?","e":"Correct it by reducing the weight, cueing 'knees out in line with the toes', strengthening the gluteus medius, and sometimes a band around the knees as feedback.","o":["The knees cave inward (valgus)","The heels are on the floor","The back is too upright and the chest too open","The feet are shoulder-width apart"]},"technique-34lxpi":{"q":"What is the problem with the plank position in the figure? (The dashed line shows the correct position)","e":"A short, quality plank is better. You can make it easier on the knees or on an incline, and cue 'squeeze glutes and abs'.","o":["The hips sag and the back arches","The head is lifted too high","The elbows aren't under the shoulders","The hips are too high in the air"]},"anatomy-1voayfc":{"q":"Where is the deltoid muscle?","e":"The deltoid has three heads: anterior, middle and posterior – each drives a different arm movement.","o":["Wrapping around the shoulder joint","On the inner thigh","In the lower back","In the calf"]},"anatomy-g3iwzz":{"q":"Which muscles make up the front and side abdominal wall?","e":"Together they stabilize, flex, rotate and side-bend the trunk.","o":["Rectus abdominis, the two obliques and the transversus","Latissimus dorsi, trapezius, rhomboids and erector spinae","Deltoid, pectoralis major and pectoralis minor","Psoas, glutes and adductors"]},"anatomy-1vzjplg":{"q":"What is the main role of the transversus abdominis?","e":"It wraps around the abdomen like a belt and contributes to the intra-abdominal pressure that stabilizes the spine.","o":["Compressing the abdomen and stabilizing the trunk","Forcefully flexing the trunk forward","Rotating the trunk to the sides","Extending the spine"]},"anatomy-16sdgmp":{"q":"Which muscle mainly performs hip abduction?","e":"The gluteus medius also stabilizes the pelvis with every step, in walking and running.","o":["The gluteus medius","The adductors","The hamstrings","The quadriceps"]},"anatomy-ewpan3":{"q":"Weakness of the gluteus medius may show up as...","e":"This is called the 'Trendelenburg sign'. Exercises like lateral band walks and side bridges help strengthen it.","o":["The pelvis dropping on the opposite side when standing on one leg","Difficulty bending the knee while running and climbing stairs","Reduced strength in the leg press","Foot pain while walking"]},"anatomy-lwm2dv":{"q":"Which muscles mainly perform hip adduction?","e":"The adductors are also active in squats and lunges, not only on the adductor machine.","o":["The adductors on the inner thigh","The gluteus medius and minimus on the outer hip","The quadriceps only","The gastrocnemius and soleus"]},"anatomy-2nyrku":{"q":"The upper trapezius elevates the shoulder blades, e.g. in shrugs.","e":"True. The trapezius also has middle and lower parts, which retract and depress the shoulder blades."},"anatomy-jl9whn":{"q":"Which muscle mainly performs ankle dorsiflexion?","e":"It's at the front of the shin and lifts the foot – important in walking so the toes don't catch.","o":["The tibialis anterior","The gastrocnemius","The soleus","The hamstrings"]},"anatomy-1b505nm":{"q":"How many vertebrae are in the lumbar spine?","e":"The spine: 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, followed by the sacrum and coccyx.","o":["5","7","12","3"]},"anatomy-qi38sz":{"q":"How many vertebrae are in the thoracic spine?","e":"Ribs attach to each thoracic vertebra – so this area is less mobile in flexion and more in rotation.","o":["12","7","5","9"]},"anatomy-ey8neo":{"q":"What is the main role of the rhomboids?","e":"They work in rows and in any 'squeeze the shoulder blades' movement.","o":["Retracting the shoulder blades toward the spine","Raising the arm forward overhead","Extending the elbow in pushes","Flexing the neck forward"]},"anatomy-1klytjo":{"q":"The pectoralis major is active in pushing exercises like push-ups and the bench press.","e":"True. It adducts and internally rotates the arm and brings it forward."},"physio-11njj3s":{"q":"What is stroke volume?","e":"Aerobic training increases stroke volume – so the heart can beat more slowly at rest.","o":["The amount of blood the heart ejects with each beat","The number of heartbeats per minute at rest","The amount of air in each breath","The total amount of blood in the body"]},"physio-x38y8":{"q":"Cardiac output equals...","e":"For example: 70 beats × 70 ml ≈ 4.9 liters per minute at rest.","o":["Heart rate × stroke volume","Blood pressure × heart rate","Stroke volume ÷ heart rate","Oxygen uptake × body weight"]},"physio-fi17kk":{"q":"During exercise, blood flow to the active muscles increases.","e":"True. Blood vessels in the muscles dilate and the heart pumps more blood to supply oxygen and fuel."},"physio-d2ku60":{"q":"What is the accepted normal range of resting heart rate in adults?","e":"In people with good aerobic fitness, resting heart rate can be below 60.","o":["About 60–100","About 20–40","About 120–160","About 180–200"]},"physio-rb5c9s":{"q":"Where is most of the body's glycogen stored?","e":"Muscle glycogen is used by the muscle itself. Liver glycogen maintains blood sugar levels.","o":["In skeletal muscle and the liver","In the bones","In subcutaneous fat","Only in the blood"]},"physio-10d1aun":{"q":"What is heat acclimatization?","e":"So at the start of summer, or on a trip to a hot country, it's worth lowering intensity in the first days.","o":["Gradual exposure to heat that improves sweating and heat regulation","A habit of drinking less water when training in the heat to save sweat","A permanent rise in resting body temperature","An adaptation that is fully completed within one hour"]},"physio-cwoz71":{"q":"What is the role of the Golgi tendon organ?","e":"It's a protective mechanism against overload of the muscle and tendon.","o":["A tension sensor in the tendon that inhibits an overly strong contraction","A temperature sensor that regulates sweating in the muscle","An energy store that supplies ATP to the muscle","A structure that produces new muscle fibers"]},"physio-fyuq3e":{"q":"What is the role of the muscle spindle?","e":"A fast stretch of a muscle makes it contract reflexively – exactly what the doctor tests with the hammer on the knee.","o":["A sensor of muscle length that triggers the stretch reflex","A pain sensor in the skin that detects injury","A glycogen store inside the muscle fiber","A small tendon connecting two muscles and transferring force between them"]},"physio-esdsdt":{"q":"What is power?","e":"Power matters in sport and also in older age – e.g. standing up quickly from a chair or catching your balance.","o":["Force × velocity","Maximal force only","Endurance over time","Maximal range of motion"]},"physio-nnqlu4":{"q":"What is the stretch-shortening cycle?","e":"For example: dropping down fast and jumping straight back up. The muscle and tendon 'store' elastic energy and release it.","o":["A fast stretch immediately followed by a contraction – the basis of plyometrics","A long static stretch of the muscle before explosive strength training","A type of active rest between sets","A slow lowering only, with no lifting phase"]},"physio-1jqxizf":{"q":"Adequate sleep is important for recovery and adaptation to training.","e":"True. Lack of sleep impairs performance, concentration and recovery."},"physio-yw8it0":{"q":"What happens to breathing as exertion increases?","e":"The body needs more oxygen and has to remove more carbon dioxide.","o":["Breathing rate and depth increase","Breathing stops","Breathing becomes slower and deeper","No change"]},"technique-1wxc7eq":{"q":"What is especially important in a bent-over barbell row?","e":"If the lower back fatigues before the upper back, you can switch to a chest-supported row.","o":["A neutral back, hinging at the hip and pulling without jerking","Rounding the back to reach further down","Pulling with momentum from the legs to lift more","Lifting the head to the ceiling on every rep"]},"technique-rarqly":{"q":"What is the correct push-up position?","e":"Hands slightly wider than the shoulders and elbows at a moderate angle to the body.","o":["A straight line from head to heels","Hips high in the air like a triangle","Hips sagging toward the floor","Head dropping below the shoulders"]},"technique-x3npvc":{"q":"What is a good regression (easier version) of the push-up?","e":"The higher the hands, the easier the exercise. Lower the height gradually.","o":["Hands on a bench or wall","Feet on a high bench","Extra weight on the back","Clapping between reps"]},"technique-nqcyvq":{"q":"What is a common mistake in dumbbell curls?","e":"If you need momentum to lift it, the weight is too heavy.","o":["Swinging the torso and using momentum","Elbows close to the body","Slow, controlled movement","Full range of motion"]},"technique-1vv4h1r":{"q":"In a kettlebell swing, where does most of the force come from?","e":"The arms just 'hold'. The kettlebell rises as a result of driving the hips forward.","o":["Explosive hip extension (hip hinge)","Lifting with the shoulders","Bending the elbows","A deep knee bend like a squat"]},"technique-qa6n2q":{"q":"Prolonged static stretching (more than about a minute per muscle) right before an explosive effort may slightly reduce performance.","e":"True. Short stretches have almost no effect. A dynamic warm-up is usually recommended before training."},"technique-5m18br":{"q":"Which warm-up is especially suitable before strength training?","e":"Warm-up sets with increasing weight prepare the joints, muscles and nervous system for the specific movement.","o":["A general warm-up, dynamic movements and gradual sets","10 minutes of static stretching per muscle before the first set","No warm-up is needed before strength training","30 minutes of hard running before lifting"]},"technique-ae3891":{"q":"In a hip thrust, what indicates proper form at the top of the movement?","e":"The glutes finish the movement, not the back. Looking forward helps keep the ribs down.","o":["The hip fully extended without arching the back","A big lower-back arch to lift the hips high","Completely straight knees","The head thrown back"]},"technique-1xy0q55":{"q":"What should you be careful about in the leg press?","e":"Go down only to the range where the hips stay against the seat.","o":["Hard knee lockout and lifting the hips off the seat","Feet shoulder-width apart in the middle of the platform","Controlled movement throughout the range","Continuous breathing throughout the set"]},"technique-y4xvuq":{"q":"In a triceps pushdown, what should move?","e":"Fixed elbows = focused triceps work.","o":["Only the forearms, elbows tucked in","The whole body swinging forward","The shoulders going up and down","The elbows moving forward and back"]},"technique-1roui0i":{"q":"For whom should dips be adapted with caution?","e":"Going too deep loads the front of the shoulder. You can start with bench dips or an assisted machine.","o":["Someone with sensitivity in the front of the shoulder","Every beginner, as a first exercise","Someone who wants to strengthen their legs","No one – it suits everyone"]},"technique-d9i7kr":{"q":"Which area is especially challenged in a side plank?","e":"You can make it easier on the knees, and harder by lifting the top leg or with small hip movements.","o":["The obliques and lateral trunk stabilizers","The biceps and forearms, which hold the body weight","The calf and ankle muscles","The neck muscles only"]},"technique-15q2mnp":{"q":"How would you teach a new trainee to squat, step by step?","e":"Look for: progression from an easier version, few cues at a time, focused feedback and progression based on quality.","a":"Start with a short demonstration and two simple cues. Practice sitting to a bench/box (box squat) to teach safe depth, then a goblet squat with a light weight that helps keep the torso upright. Key cues: comfortable stance width, knees in line with the toes, heels on the floor, breathing and bracing. Give feedback on one thing at a time, film if possible, and progress load or depth only when technique is stable."},"program-1d976nz":{"q":"What is the 'training frequency' of a muscle group?","e":"It's usually recommended to train each muscle group at least twice a week.","o":["How many times a week it's trained","How many reps are done in each set","How long each workout lasts","How much weight is lifted in each set"]},"program-17ow4rq":{"q":"What is a typical rest time between sets in hypertrophy training?","e":"Enough rest lets you maintain quality and volume in the following sets.","o":["About 1–3 minutes","About 5 seconds","Always 10 minutes","No rest needed"]},"program-vx73un":{"q":"A good program is tailored to the trainee's goals, ability and available time.","e":"True. The best program is the one the trainee does consistently."},"program-sl4yhm":{"q":"A trainee's squat 1RM is 120 kg. How much is 75%?","e":"120 × 0.75 = 90 kg.","o":["90 kg","75 kg","100 kg","80 kg"]},"program-5o1gzw":{"q":"What is 'pyramid' training?","e":"For example: 12 reps at 40 kg, 10 at 45, 8 at 50.","o":["Increasing the weight and decreasing the reps from set to set","Same weight and reps in all sets","Training only the lowering phase of the movement","Only one exercise per training day"]},"program-1vqrx4o":{"q":"What is a drop set?","e":"An intense, time-saving method, but fatiguing. Use it in moderation.","o":["Continuing immediately with a reduced weight","Resting 5 minutes between sets","Lowering the weight extra slowly","A set with the maximum weight for one rep"]},"program-24ugi0":{"q":"What is an upper/lower split?","e":"A popular split of 4 workouts a week, so each area is trained twice.","o":["Alternating upper-body and lower-body workouts","A workout doing light exercises first and heavy ones after","Leg exercises only","Ab exercises only"]},"program-oh21fu":{"q":"What is the main drawback of taking every set in a workout to complete failure?","e":"Most sets can be close to failure (RIR 1–3) and give a good stimulus with less fatigue.","o":["Accumulated fatigue that hurts recovery and the following sets","It's always dangerous and completely forbidden for every trainee","It's not tiring at all, so it has no drawbacks","It completely prevents any muscle building over time"]},"program-1a672r7":{"q":"How long is typically spent on a general warm-up before training?","e":"After the general warm-up, continue with a warm-up specific to that day's exercises.","o":["About 5–10 minutes","30 seconds","An hour","Not needed"]},"program-111k3rw":{"q":"A trainee wants to train for a 10 km run and also get stronger. What is a reasonable arrangement?","e":"Both goals are possible together. Planning keeps fatigue from one from hurting the quality of the other.","o":["Separate hard runs from leg workouts and plan the weekly load","Run 10 km right before every heavy squat session","Drop strength training completely until after the race to keep the legs fresh","Do running and strength on the same day, every day of the week"]},"program-13tn6yi":{"q":"There's no need to log workouts – it's enough to roughly remember what you did.","e":"False. Logging lets you see progress, plan progressive overload and spot plateaus."},"program-18yzju8":{"q":"An advanced trainee has been stuck at the same bench press weight for two months. What would you check and what would you change?","e":"Look for: checking recovery, volume and frequency, technique, and a planned change in training variables.","a":"Check sleep, nutrition and stress, weekly volume and frequency for the press, whether they go to failure on every set, and technique (scapular stability, bar path, legs). Options: a deload week, changing the rep range or using undulating periodization, adding frequency (2–3 times a week), accessory exercises for the weak point (paused bench, triceps, upper back), and accurate logging."},"trx-7qik7s":{"q":"What should you check on TRX straps before starting an exercise?","e":"Handles at different heights create an asymmetric load and unstable movement.","o":["Appropriate length, handles at the same height and no fraying","That the straps are at maximum length in every exercise for a big range","That one handle is lower than the other for balance","That the straps are slack at the start of the movement"]},"trx-z724gb":{"q":"Which TRX exercise suits a beginner for the lower body?","e":"The straps help with balance and let you learn depth and technique.","o":["A squat holding the straps for support","A single-leg pistol squat","Jumping lunges in the straps","A pike with the feet in the straps, pushing the hips to the ceiling"]},"trx-gqd4pj":{"q":"Which area does the TRX Y-fly mainly train?","e":"An excellent exercise for balancing out pushing exercises and improving posture.","o":["The rear shoulders and scapular stabilizers","The calf and ankle muscles","The lower abs","The biceps and chest, mainly in the closing phase"]},"trx-195cwrn":{"q":"With TRX you can change the intensity in seconds, simply by stepping forward or back.","e":"True. That's what makes TRX very convenient for groups with mixed levels."},"trx-1j22lui":{"q":"According to Newton's second law, force equals...","e":"So lifting the same weight fast requires more force than lifting it slowly.","o":["Mass × acceleration","Mass × velocity","Velocity ÷ time","Weight ÷ height"]},"trx-2r3eca":{"q":"Where, roughly, is the center of gravity of a standing person?","e":"Its position changes when you move the limbs – for example raising the arms moves it up.","o":["Around the pelvis","In the head","In the feet","In the upper chest"]},"trx-1xma4fr":{"q":"Why is it easier to keep balance with the feet apart?","e":"This is the principle behind making stability exercises easier or harder.","o":["The base of support is wider","Body weight is smaller","The muscles don't need to work","It's not true – feet together is more stable"]},"trx-1nn5m9t":{"q":"Why do you keep a weight close to the body when lifting it from the floor?","e":"The further the weight is from the body, the greater the torque on the lower back, even if the weight is the same.","o":["To shorten the moment arm and reduce the load on the back","So the weight itself is lighter to lift and grip","So you can lift faster","It has no effect – it's just for comfort"]},"scenarios-wrrlf":{"q":"A trainee arrives on a hot day without a water bottle. What would you do?","e":"Drinking and recognizing early signs of heat strain are part of basic safety.","o":["Get them water, lower the intensity and add breaks","Tell them to train without water, just at light intensity","Cancel their workout and angrily send them home","Increase the intensity so they sweat and warm up"]},"scenarios-a8xyev":{"q":"A trainee asks: 'How soon will I see results?' What is a professional answer?","e":"Realistic expectations prevent disappointment. It's worth measuring things that improve quickly too – strength, energy, sleep.","o":["It varies; performance improves within weeks, appearance within months","Guaranteed within one week, if you train every day and eat right","Never – at an older age the body no longer changes","Only if you combine supplements with training"]},"scenarios-1gg81di":{"q":"Two trainees are arguing over a machine. What would you do?","e":"A quick, respectful solution keeps a good atmosphere in the gym.","o":["Approach calmly and suggest they alternate sets","Ignore it and let them sort it out","Take a side immediately without hearing both","Close the machine for the rest of the day"]},"scenarios-16w156b":{"q":"A trainee looks very exhausted and says she barely eats before workouts to 'burn more'. What is the right approach?","e":"Genuine concern, a discreet conversation and referral to a professional – without diagnosing or judging.","o":["Talk privately and sensitively and encourage her to see a professional","Recommend an even more restricted diet on training days","Ignore it – nutrition isn't part of an instructor's job","Tell her in front of everyone that she's wrong and it's dangerous"]},"scenarios-1tpmcgi":{"q":"An older participant in a class wants to do exactly what the younger ones do. What is right?","e":"Age is just one data point. Adapt by ability, history and how they feel.","o":["Respect it, offer adapted versions and monitor","Forbid them from taking part in the hard parts","Ask them to sit on the side and watch","Tell them they're too old for this"]},"scenarios-1xaf83l":{"q":"A trainee is filming other trainees in the gym without their permission. What would you do?","e":"Trainees' privacy and sense of safety are part of the staff's responsibility.","o":["Politely ask them to stop and act according to the facility's rules","Film them back so they understand how it feels","Ignore it – it's not the instructor's responsibility","Take their phone by force and delete the photos"]},"scenarios-2rtzie":{"q":"An instructor should come to a session with a plan, but be flexible and change it according to the trainee's condition that day.","e":"True. A tired, sore or stressed trainee needs adjustment – that's a sign of professionalism, not lack of planning."},"scenarios-1snxmhb":{"q":"A trainee asks for exercises to 'lose fat only from the belly'. What would you explain?","e":"'Spot reduction' is a common myth. Explain gently and offer a comprehensive program.","o":["You can't choose where fat comes off – fat loss is general","500 crunches a day will remove belly fat","A sweat belt during training will melt the fat","Doing only ab exercises is enough, with no other change"]},"scenarios-1sjb4hb":{"q":"A trainee feels embarrassed training near others. What can help?","e":"Feeling comfortable at the start greatly increases the chance she'll stick with it.","o":["Quiet hours, a less crowded area and guidance at the start","Putting her in the middle of the room so she gets used to it","Telling her it's her problem and she should get over it","Giving up on her and focusing on other trainees"]},"scenarios-14vc4o4":{"q":"A participant collapses in the middle of a group class. Describe what you would do and how you'd manage the rest of the group.","e":"Look for: safety, checking response and breathing, assigning specific tasks, CPR and defibrillator, and managing the group.","a":"Stop the class, make sure the area is safe and approach the trainee: check response and breathing. Assign clear tasks to specific participants: 'You – call emergency services (101 in Israel)', 'You – bring the defibrillator'. If they're not breathing normally – start CPR and use the defibrillator as instructed. Ask the rest of the group to clear the space and stay calm, open a path for the medical team, and report to management after the event."},"safety-crmrkl":{"q":"What is the emergency number for Magen David Adom (ambulance) in Israel?","e":"100 – police, 101 – MDA (ambulance), 102 – fire and rescue.","o":["101","100","102","103"]},"safety-1rnk7cl":{"q":"What is an AED?","e":"A defibrillator can restore a normal heart rhythm in cardiac arrest, and using it early saves lives.","o":["An automated external defibrillator","A device for measuring blood pressure and heart rate","A manual resuscitation bag","A portable glucose meter"]},"safety-et7wb":{"q":"An automated external defibrillator (AED) gives voice instructions and is meant to be used even by people without medical training.","e":"True. The device analyzes the heart rhythm itself and shocks only when needed. It's best to get to know it in a first-aid course."},"safety-nbc11l":{"q":"What is the recommended compression rate in adult CPR?","e":"Fast, deep and continuous compressions, with as few interruptions as possible.","o":["100–120 per minute","40–60 per minute","150–180 per minute","10 per minute"]},"safety-dahz53":{"q":"What is the recommended compression depth in adult CPR?","e":"And let the chest recoil fully between compressions.","o":["About 5–6 cm","About 1 cm","About 10 cm","It doesn't matter"]},"safety-3yfsn6":{"q":"A trainee sprained an ankle and there's swelling. What is right to do?","e":"If they can't bear weight, there's deformity or very severe pain – refer for a medical check.","o":["Stop, rest, cool, elevate and refer for a check","Keep running slowly to 'warm up' the ankle","Massage the area hard to spread the swelling","Apply strong heat immediately to speed up healing"]},"safety-yzsqul":{"q":"What is the right way to lower a heavy dumbbell from the rack to the floor?","e":"The same safe-lifting principles apply when arranging equipment.","o":["Bending the hips and knees, close to the body","With a rounded back and straight legs, to save time","With one hand, far from the body","Dropping it from height"]},"safety-dpowt1":{"q":"A trainee complains of a sudden, very severe headache during exertion. What do you do?","e":"A sudden headache of unusual severity is a warning sign that requires immediate evaluation.","o":["Stop immediately and refer for urgent medical evaluation","Give them a drink and return them to training at low intensity","Increase the intensity","Ignore it"]},"safety-g6411c":{"q":"It's important to keep order and not leave dumbbells and plates on the floor in walkways.","e":"True. Scattered equipment is a common cause of trips and injuries."},"safety-1qujzgl":{"q":"A trainee took a blow to the head and feels confused and dizzy. What is right?","e":"Confusion and dizziness after a blow to the head raise suspicion of a concussion. Return to activity only per medical guidance.","o":["Stop, don't return to training today and refer for a check","Continue if they feel fine after a minute of rest","Give them a painkiller and continue at low intensity","Ask them to do a squat to test their balance"]},"safety-1e33ot8":{"q":"What should a basic gym safety procedure include?","e":"Look for: health screening, equipment, order, first aid and defibrillator, emergency procedure and documentation.","a":"A health declaration for every new trainee, routine equipment checks and maintenance, order and clear walkways, an accessible first-aid kit and defibrillator with staff who know how to use them, visible emergency numbers, an emergency procedure (who calls, who brings the AED, who guides the medical team), signs on correct machine use, and documentation of unusual incidents."},"nutrition-1svh4i4":{"q":"Which vitamins are fat-soluble?","e":"Fat-soluble vitamins are stored in the body. B vitamins and C are water-soluble.","o":["A, D, E, K","B and C","Vitamin C only","All vitamins"]},"nutrition-oak7lf":{"q":"What is a good source of plant protein?","e":"Combining legumes with grains over the day gives a complete amino acid profile.","o":["Legumes and tofu","Cucumber and lettuce","Apple and banana","Olive oil and avocado"]},"nutrition-1lfenrv":{"q":"You can build muscle on a well-planned vegetarian or vegan diet too.","e":"True. It's important to get enough protein and a variety of sources."},"nutrition-u64a0v":{"q":"A trainee weighs 80 kg and aims for 1.8 g of protein per kg. How many grams of protein is that per day?","e":"80 × 1.8 = 144 g, ideally split across several meals.","o":["144 g","80 g","180 g","100 g"]},"nutrition-51twd1":{"q":"What is a general recommendation about eating before training?","e":"There are big differences between people. Experiment in regular training, not before a competition.","o":["Carbohydrate and protein about one to three hours before","A heavy, fatty meal right before, so there's enough fuel","A 12-hour fast before every workout","Only a cup of coffee, no food at all"]},"nutrition-124ipu6":{"q":"In most regular workouts of up to about an hour, water is the appropriate drink.","e":"True. Sports drinks are mainly meant for long efforts or heavy sweating."},"nutrition-lq5fwq":{"q":"When might a drink with electrolytes and carbohydrates help?","e":"In long efforts the body loses salts and uses carbohydrates, and replacing them helps performance.","o":["In prolonged exercise over about an hour, or in the heat","On every 10-minute walk","Only before bed at night","Never – water is always enough"]},"nutrition-11opqma":{"q":"What is the 'glycemic index'?","e":"The actual effect also depends on the amount of food and what is eaten with it.","o":["How fast blood sugar rises after a food","A measure of the amount and quality of protein in food","A measure of body fat percentage","A measure of the water content of food"]},"nutrition-6rumoy":{"q":"What is true about caffeine and performance?","e":"Caffeine late in the day may hurt sleep – and sleep matters for recovery.","o":["It improves performance in many people, but sensitivity varies","It's banned for every athlete at any dose","It has no effect on performance","The more you take, the better the performance"]},"nutrition-xvhdbc":{"q":"Which nutrients are especially important for bone health?","e":"Together with weight-bearing activity and strength training.","o":["Calcium and vitamin D","Vitamin C only","Caffeine","Sugar"]},"special-fbpxcl":{"q":"What suits training children before puberty?","e":"The goal: enjoyment, physical literacy and good lifelong habits.","o":["Play, a variety of movements and light, supervised strength work","A program identical to an adult trainee's","Long runs only, with no strength work","Maximal lifts to build strength early"]},"special-1a75nat":{"q":"A trainee with chronic low-back pain has been cleared to train. What is usually recommended?","e":"In most cases adapted activity is better than rest. Work in line with the instructions they received.","o":["Gradual, adapted movement, avoiding what makes it worse","Complete rest until the pain is gone","Heavy exercises from day one","Ignoring the pain completely and carrying on"]},"special-yjqq6l":{"q":"People aged 80 and over can also benefit from adapted strength training.","e":"True. Strength training in older age improves strength, function and independence."},"special-osd145":{"q":"What is sarcopenia?","e":"Strength training and adequate protein intake are the main tools to slow it.","o":["Loss of muscle and strength with age","Loss of bone density with age","Chronic inflammation of the tendons","Wear of the joint cartilage"]},"special-z4zofz":{"q":"A trainee in menopause asks what type of training is especially important for her. What is right?","e":"During this period bone density declines, and resistance training helps preserve it.","o":["Strength and weight-bearing activity, alongside aerobic work","Stretching only, to keep flexible","Swimming only, so as not to load the bone","Stop training until this period passes"]},"special-3xlt9f":{"q":"A trainee with type 1 diabetes arrives to train. What is important to make sure of?","e":"The instructor doesn't manage the treatment, but knows the signs of low blood sugar and how to respond.","o":["That they follow their own instructions and have fast-acting carbohydrate available","That they haven't eaten all day before training, so their sugar is low","That they skipped the insulin dose before training","Nothing special – they can train like everyone"]},"special-w7k0gt":{"q":"In a normal pregnancy, the 'talk test' is a simple tool for monitoring training intensity.","e":"True. If she can hold a conversation, the intensity is usually appropriate. Personal instructions from her care provider come first."},"special-186ev76":{"q":"A trainee after knee replacement surgery wants to return to training. What is right?","e":"After rehab is complete, the instructor continues within the framework set by the care team.","o":["Follow the surgeon and physiotherapist and coordinate with them","Return to jumping immediately to check the new knee works","Avoid moving the knee forever","Build a rehab program for them yourself"]},"special-1u9l6z1":{"q":"What is an important principle when working with people with disabilities?","e":"The person is the expert on their own body. Cooperation leads to safer, more enjoyable training.","o":["Ask what suits them and focus on abilities","Decide for them what they can do","Avoid training so as not to endanger them","Give them exactly what everyone else does"]},"special-qh75hx":{"q":"List principles for building a group class for older adults (65+).","e":"Look for: health screening, strength and balance, adaptations, intensity monitoring, safety and the social aspect.","a":"Health declaration and clearances as needed, a long, gradual warm-up, strength exercises for the legs and upper body using everyday movements (rising from a chair, lifting, pushing), balance work with support available, easy and hard versions of every exercise, a comfortable pace and drink breaks, monitoring with the talk test or an effort scale, an emphasis on safety (stable chairs, non-slip floor), and plenty of social atmosphere and positive feedback."},"anatomy-dsv4tf":{"q":"Which muscle lies under the biceps and flexes the elbow in any forearm position?","e":"The brachialis attaches to the ulna, so it flexes the elbow regardless of forearm rotation.","o":["The brachialis","The triceps","The anterior deltoid","The coracobrachialis"]},"anatomy-oh44nz":{"q":"With which grip is the brachioradialis especially active in elbow flexion?","e":"In a hammer curl the brachioradialis is in a good mechanical position and works more.","o":["A neutral grip ('hammer' curl)","A very wide grip on the bar","A grip with the thumb under the bar","Grip has no effect"]},"anatomy-upgzkm":{"q":"What is the main action of the anterior deltoid?","e":"The anterior deltoid works in front raises and all pressing exercises.","o":["Raising the arm forward (shoulder flexion)","Extending the elbow in overhead presses","Retracting the shoulder blades","Extending the shoulder backward"]},"anatomy-43rdnm":{"q":"What is the main action of the posterior deltoid?","e":"The rear deltoid works in exercises like reverse flyes, face pulls and wide rows.","o":["Moving the arm backward in the horizontal plane","Raising the arm forward overhead","Flexing the elbow with a neutral grip","Shrugging the shoulders toward the ears"]},"anatomy-1i3psx":{"q":"Which of the following muscles belongs to the quadriceps group?","e":"The quadriceps include the rectus femoris and the three vasti: lateralis, medialis and intermedius.","o":["Rectus femoris","Semitendinosus","Gracilis","Sartorius"]},"anatomy-fcoisj":{"q":"Which head of the quadriceps also crosses the hip joint?","e":"The rectus femoris is a two-joint muscle: it extends the knee and also flexes the hip.","o":["Rectus femoris","Vastus lateralis","Vastus medialis","Vastus intermedius"]},"anatomy-z5d4ss":{"q":"Which muscle group is the main extensor of the spine?","e":"The erector spinae along the spine hold it upright and work hard in the deadlift and RDL.","o":["The erector spinae","The rectus abdominis","The psoas","The chest muscles"]},"anatomy-1y8i1js":{"q":"Which part of the trapezius depresses the shoulder blades?","e":"The lower trapezius depresses the shoulder blades and helps rotate them upward – important for stability in overhead lifts.","o":["The lower trapezius","The upper trapezius","The levator scapulae","The posterior deltoid"]},"anatomy-12ovcny":{"q":"What does the levator scapulae do?","e":"Tension in this muscle is common in people who sit a lot at a computer.","o":["Elevates the scapula and side-bends the neck","Depresses and stabilizes the scapula","Extends the elbow in pushes","Flexes the hip when walking"]},"anatomy-1cdju98":{"q":"What is the thigh bone – the longest bone in the body – called?","e":"The femur connects the hip joint to the knee joint.","o":["Femur","Tibia","Fibula","Humerus"]},"anatomy-aluvyz":{"q":"Which lower-leg bone carries most of the body weight?","e":"The tibia is thick and strong; the fibula is thinner and serves mainly for muscle attachment and ankle stability.","o":["The tibia","The fibula","The patella","The heel bone"]},"anatomy-1uzv2m1":{"q":"The patella is a small bone that sits inside the quadriceps tendon.","e":"True. The patella improves the quadriceps' mechanical advantage in knee extension."},"anatomy-qhxtgi":{"q":"What is the 'anatomical position'?","e":"It's the reference point from which directions and movements of the body are described.","o":["Standing upright, palms facing forward","Lying on the back, arms out to the sides","Sitting upright, hands on the knees","Standing upright, arms raised"]},"anatomy-1opi56":{"q":"What does the term 'proximal' mean?","e":"For example: the elbow is proximal to the wrist. The opposite is 'distal'.","o":["Closer to the center of the body","Further from the center of the body","On the back side of the body","Close to the surface of the skin"]},"anatomy-19sbz2s":{"q":"What does the term 'medial' mean?","e":"The opposite is 'lateral' – further from the midline. For example: the big toe is on the medial side of the foot.","o":["Closer to the midline of the body","Further from the midline","Closer to the head","Closer to the feet"]},"anatomy-1a7083e":{"q":"Which movement is ankle plantar flexion?","e":"The gastrocnemius and soleus perform this movement – e.g. in calf raises and pushing off when running.","o":["Rising onto the toes","Pulling the toes toward the shin","Turning the foot inward","Bending the knee backward"]},"anatomy-1208ksj":{"q":"In which exercise does the upper (clavicular) part of the chest work especially?","e":"On an incline the arm moves slightly more upward, and the upper chest works more.","o":["Incline bench press","Decline bench press","Lat pulldown","Cable row"]},"anatomy-1jjt318":{"q":"What is the main breathing muscle?","e":"The diaphragm descends on inhalation and increases chest volume. It's also part of the core stabilization system.","o":["The diaphragm","The rectus abdominis","The trapezius","The pectoralis major"]},"anatomy-1t4utfp":{"q":"The pelvic floor muscles support the pelvic organs and take part in core stabilization.","e":"True. They work together with the diaphragm and deep abdominal muscles to create intra-abdominal pressure."},"anatomy-1e635to":{"q":"Which muscle group assists the gluteus maximus in hip extension?","e":"In the deadlift, hip thrust and bridge, the glutes and hamstrings work together in hip extension.","o":["The hamstrings","The quadriceps","The iliopsoas","The tibialis anterior"]},"physio-5wcuoc":{"q":"What is metabolism?","e":"Metabolism includes both breakdown (catabolism) and building (anabolism).","o":["All the processes that break down and build substances and produce energy","Only the digestion of food in the digestive tract, from mouth to intestine","Resting heart and breathing rate","The amount of muscle relative to fat in the body"]},"physio-97rbs8":{"q":"Roughly how long does it take to refill muscle glycogen stores after long exercise that depleted them, with a carbohydrate-rich diet?","e":"This is one reason to plan rest or light training after an especially long endurance session.","o":["About 24–48 hours","About 5 minutes","About two weeks","About a month"]},"physio-1duy5n3":{"q":"In prolonged, moderate-intensity aerobic activity, fat is a significant energy source.","e":"True. As intensity rises, the body relies more on carbohydrates."},"physio-1htukgf":{"q":"What happens to systolic blood pressure during aerobic activity?","e":"The heart pumps more blood per minute, so systolic pressure rises. Diastolic usually stays relatively stable.","o":["It rises with intensity","It drops sharply","It doesn't change at all","It drops and then disappears"]},"physio-icxz12":{"q":"What is the role of the mitochondria in the cell?","e":"Mitochondria are called the 'power plants' of the cell.","o":["Aerobic energy production","Storing fat only","Transmitting nerve signals","Contracting muscle fibers"]},"physio-1j5r0d2":{"q":"Which adaptation is typical of aerobic endurance training?","e":"The muscle becomes more efficient at receiving and using oxygen – and that's what improves endurance.","o":["More mitochondria and capillaries in the muscle","A decrease in total blood volume","A decrease in the heart's stroke volume","Turning all fibers into fast-twitch fibers"]},"physio-11q8doj":{"q":"What is true about lactate (lactic acid)?","e":"Lactate is recycled as an energy source. The idea that it's 'just waste' turned out to be inaccurate.","o":["It's used as fuel by the heart and other muscles","Toxic waste with no use at all","It stays in the muscle for several days after training","It's produced only at rest, not during exercise"]},"physio-1wot4g0":{"q":"What is 'heart rate reserve' in the Karvonen method?","e":"Karvonen: target HR = resting HR + intensity % × heart rate reserve.","o":["Maximal heart rate minus resting heart rate","Resting heart rate times 2","Maximal heart rate plus resting heart rate","The heart rate after one minute of rest"]},"physio-mbjqeo":{"q":"A 30-year-old trainee with a resting heart rate of 60. Using the Karvonen method, what is the target heart rate for 60% intensity?","e":"Max HR: 220−30=190. Reserve: 190−60=130. 60%×130=78, plus 60 = 138.","o":["138","114","150","126"]},"physio-1t8clid":{"q":"Under which conditions is sweating less effective at cooling the body?","e":"Cooling happens when sweat evaporates. In high humidity evaporation is slow, and the body heats up faster.","o":["High humidity","Dry, cool air","A light breeze","Shade"]},"physio-1p6fuxq":{"q":"Which of the following is an early sign of dehydration?","e":"It's worth drinking throughout the day, not only when you feel very thirsty.","o":["Thirst and dark urine","Cold hands only","Hiccups","Hunger"]},"physio-9ahvj3":{"q":"Within a few weeks, strength training turns most slow-twitch fibers into fast-twitch fibers.","e":"False. There are some shifts within fast-twitch subtypes, but the basic split between slow and fast fibers is fairly stable."},"physio-ynnk63":{"q":"What is the role of the hormone insulin?","e":"Insulin lowers blood sugar and promotes energy storage.","o":["Moving glucose from the blood into the cells","Breaking down muscle into amino acids","Raising the heart rate during exercise","Regulating sweating in the heat"]},"physio-12x5qso":{"q":"What usually happens to insulin sensitivity with regular physical activity?","e":"This is one reason physical activity is recommended for preventing and treating type 2 diabetes.","o":["It increases – the cells respond better","It decreases – the cells respond less","It's not affected by activity at all","It disappears completely after a few weeks"]},"physio-1jsonev":{"q":"What is cortisol?","e":"Cortisol rises with training and stress. Enough sleep and rest help keep it balanced.","o":["A stress hormone that mobilizes energy","A vitamin that helps build bone","A type of fast-twitch muscle fiber","An enzyme that breaks down fats in the intestine"]},"physio-s0v900":{"q":"Warming up raises muscle temperature and improves nerve conduction speed.","e":"True. So a warm muscle responds faster and produces force more efficiently."},"physio-1ii45ar":{"q":"What is the main role of capillaries?","e":"Endurance training increases capillary density in the muscle, improving oxygen delivery.","o":["Exchanging oxygen and substances between the blood and the tissues","Carrying blood from the heart to the organs at high pressure and speed","Storing blood for times of exertion","Producing red blood cells"]},"physio-tip8ee":{"q":"Which type of contraction usually causes stronger delayed-onset muscle soreness (DOMS)?","e":"For example a long downhill hike, or slowly lowering a weight.","o":["Eccentric – the muscle lengthens under load","Isometric – the muscle doesn't change length","Concentric – the muscle shortens under load","A light static stretch at the end of training"]},"physio-tg3s4j":{"q":"Which exercise mainly develops explosive strength (power)?","e":"Power is built with fast, vigorous movements – jumps, throws, Olympic lifts.","o":["Box jump","Static plank","Slow walking","Hamstring stretch"]},"technique-hjhwxg":{"q":"In a barbell bench press, where does the bar usually come down to?","e":"Lowering to the middle–lower chest keeps a shoulder-friendly bar path.","o":["The middle–lower chest","The neck","The belly","It doesn't go below shoulder height"]},"technique-1nfiida":{"q":"What is the role of the legs in the bench press?","e":"Feet planted firmly on the floor help keep the upper back stable and generate power.","o":["Stabilizing and pushing into the floor","They have no role at all","Lifting the hips off the bench","Placing them on the bench"]},"technique-12vb33p":{"q":"In a 'high bar' squat, where does the bar rest?","e":"In a low-bar squat the bar sits slightly lower – on the rear delts.","o":["On the upper trapezius","On the neck","Below the rear delts, on the shoulder blades","On the chest"]},"technique-ppj2no":{"q":"What characterizes a low-bar squat compared with a high-bar squat?","e":"That's why many powerlifters use it – it allows lifting a bit more weight.","o":["The torso leans forward more and there's more hip work","The torso is completely upright and there's almost no hip work","There's no difference between the two versions","The knees travel forward much more"]},"technique-11ghzmb":{"q":"In the deadlift starting position, where is the bar?","e":"A bar over mid-foot keeps a straight, efficient pulling line.","o":["Over mid-foot, close to the shins","Over the toes, away from the body to make room for the knees","Behind the heels","At the knees"]},"technique-1dbo345":{"q":"In a pull-up, which grip emphasizes the biceps more?","e":"With an underhand grip the forearm is supinated – and the biceps works more.","o":["Underhand grip (chin-up)","A very wide grip","A narrow overhand grip","No difference"]},"technique-1mtb7cj":{"q":"What is a good pull-up regression for someone who can't do a single rep?","e":"Slow lowering (negatives) and partial assistance build strength in exactly that movement.","o":["Band-assisted pull-ups and slow negatives","Keep trying full pull-ups until you succeed","Dumbbell shoulder press","Biceps curls only"]},"technique-auobqq":{"q":"What is usually true about a reverse lunge compared with a forward lunge?","e":"So the reverse lunge is a good choice for learning and for trainees with sensitive knees.","o":["The reverse lunge is easier to control and loads the knee less","The reverse lunge is more dangerous for the front knee","There's no difference between the two versions","The reverse lunge suits only advanced trainees"]},"technique-128fwje":{"q":"In a Bulgarian split squat, where is the rear foot?","e":"It's a single-leg exercise that challenges strength and balance.","o":["On a bench behind","On the floor, next to the front foot","In the air","On a Swiss ball"]},"technique-10us43w":{"q":"What is a common mistake in the seated cable row?","e":"If you need momentum, the weight is too heavy. The torso stays stable and the arms and back do the work.","o":["Swinging the torso to move the weight","An upright back throughout the movement","Pulling the handle toward the belly","Squeezing the shoulder blades at the end of the movement"]},"technique-14aqff4":{"q":"In a dumbbell fly, how should the elbows be?","e":"A fixed angle makes the exercise a chest isolation and protects the elbows.","o":["Slightly bent and at a fixed angle","Fully locked throughout the movement","Bent to 90 degrees and changing","The angle doesn't matter at all"]},"technique-j1d9ic":{"q":"In a lateral raise, up to what height do you usually lift?","e":"That's the range where the middle deltoid works the most, comfortably for the joint.","o":["Up to about shoulder height","Above the head","Up to the waist","Until the arms touch each other"]},"technique-1ejfx97":{"q":"In the leg extension machine, what is important about the machine setup?","e":"Aligning the axis prevents unwanted load on the knee and allows smooth movement.","o":["The machine's axis in line with the knee","The seat as far back as possible","The pad resting on the knee itself","The back away from the backrest"]},"technique-hfihi3":{"q":"What does 'bracing' the core before a heavy rep mean?","e":"Bracing creates a stable 'column' around the spine and allows safe force transfer.","o":["A deep breath and stiffening the trunk muscles around the abdomen","Breathing air into the chest only and relaxing the belly","Sucking the belly in hard without breathing","Arching the back"]},"technique-1ou3iah":{"q":"In crunches you should pull the head with your hands to come up higher.","e":"False. Pulling the head loads the neck. The hands only support lightly, and the abs do the movement."},"technique-1b5ymeq":{"q":"What is the 'dead bug' exercise?","e":"An excellent exercise for learning core control without loading the back.","o":["Lying on your back: lower the opposite arm and leg, back pressed down","A slow push-up with a two-second pause at the bottom","Hopping on one leg with a soft landing","A lower-back stretch lying on the stomach"]},"technique-a3378h":{"q":"What is the 'bird dog' exercise?","e":"It trains the trunk and pelvic stabilizers and also suits people with sensitive backs.","o":["On all fours: extend the opposite arm and leg","Jumping in place with high knees and arm swings","A pull-up with the legs raised in front","Dumbbell biceps curls"]},"technique-z7v3d4":{"q":"What is the main challenge in a farmer's walk?","e":"An excellent functional exercise – like carrying heavy shopping bags the right way.","o":["Grip, trunk stability and walking tall","Hamstring flexibility","Jumping as high as possible","Sprinting speed over a short distance"]},"technique-1o0ns7n":{"q":"What is the difference between a pronated and a supinated grip?","e":"For example: a regular pull-up uses a pronated grip, a chin-up a supinated grip.","o":["Pronated: palms facing down; supinated: facing up","No difference – two names for exactly the same grip on different machines","Pronated is a wide grip, and supinated is narrow","Supinated is a fingers-only grip"]},"technique-1i6v7vm":{"q":"In the leg press, where should the feet be placed to emphasize the glutes and hamstrings?","e":"A high position increases hip flexion and decreases knee flexion. A low position emphasizes the quadriceps.","o":["High on the platform","Very low on the platform","Together at the bottom","It makes no difference"]},"technique-7ihmvv":{"q":"What is 'full range of motion' in an exercise?","e":"A full, controlled range contributes to strength, flexibility and muscle building.","o":["The whole range the joint allows, under control","Moving as fast as possible","Half the range, to keep tension","Moving with whole-body momentum"]},"program-10legmw":{"q":"How many sets per week per muscle group are considered a common, effective range for hypertrophy in most trainees?","e":"Beginners can progress well with less, and advanced trainees sometimes need more – depending on recovery.","o":["About 10–20 sets","1–2 sets","50–60 sets","There's no link between sets and growth"]},"program-11td4f":{"q":"What is usually a 'microcycle' in training planning?","e":"Microcycle (usually a week) → mesocycle (several weeks) → macrocycle (months to a year).","o":["A week of training","One day","A whole year","Half a year"]},"program-18g9xbc":{"q":"What is a 'mesocycle'?","e":"For example: 4–6 weeks of strength building, followed by a deload week.","o":["A block of several weeks with a goal","One single workout","One set within a workout","A whole training year with several goals"]},"program-hpt8k6":{"q":"What does AMRAP mean in training?","e":"As Many Reps/Rounds As Possible – a common method in functional training.","o":["As many reps or rounds as possible in a given time","Long, full rest between all sets","Only one set with the maximum weight","Light, continuous aerobic training for a long time"]},"program-1fbo5s7":{"q":"What does EMOM mean?","e":"Every Minute On the Minute – keeps a steady pace and built-in rest.","o":["A task at the start of every minute, resting for the remainder","A full minute of rest after each set","A workout that lasts only one minute","A one-minute walk between every two exercises"]},"program-1bi0r9v":{"q":"What characterizes muscular endurance training?","e":"Usually in the range of 15 reps and up.","o":["Many reps, moderate load and short rest","One rep at maximum load each set, with long rest","5 minutes of rest between all sets","Aerobic training only, no weights"]},"program-1tsqu87":{"q":"A person who has never run wants to start running. What usually suits them?","e":"Gradual progression lets muscles, tendons and bones adapt and reduces injuries.","o":["Alternating walking and running, with running time increasing gradually","A 10 km run in the very first session","Running every day until it hurts","Only short sprints, to build speed before increasing distance"]},"program-o47c3s":{"q":"What is the accepted (non-binding) rule of thumb for increasing weekly running volume?","e":"It's a rule of thumb, not a precise scientific law, but the idea – gradual increase – is very important.","o":["A gradual increase, up to about 10% per week","Double the volume every week until reaching the goal","Triple the volume at once","Increase only when something hurts"]},"program-ztcd0z":{"q":"What is 'tapering'?","e":"Intensity is kept and volume is reduced, so fatigue drops and fitness is preserved.","o":["Reducing volume before a competition to arrive fresh","Increasing training right before a competition to arrive at a peak","A complete two-month break from training","Training fasted before competition day"]},"program-186u73e":{"q":"What is 'active rest'?","e":"It helps blood flow and well-being without adding load.","o":["Light activity on a rest day","A day of especially intense training","A long nap during the day","Short, maximal strength training"]},"program-19cmnw5":{"q":"A trainee has only 30 minutes, 3 times a week. What is most efficient?","e":"Compound exercises work many muscles in little time, and supersets save rest time.","o":["Full body with compound exercises","Arm isolation exercises only","Only stretching every session","A separate day for each small muscle"]},"program-1jplprr":{"q":"At roughly what percentage of 1RM can you do about 10 reps?","e":"It's an estimate – there are differences between people and between exercises.","o":["About 75%","About 95%","About 50%","About 90%"]},"program-ymf1yj":{"q":"You can build muscle with relatively light loads too (about 30%–40% of 1RM), if the sets get close to failure.","e":"True. Studies show similar growth across a wide range of loads – what matters is enough effort and volume."},"program-1wbo40m":{"q":"What does RIR 0 mean?","e":"RIR = Reps In Reserve. RIR 2 means about two reps were left.","o":["Failure – no more proper reps possible","10 more reps left before failure","0 seconds of rest","A very easy warm-up set"]},"program-1kt9r3i":{"q":"A trainee whose main goal is strength wants to add cardio in the same session. What order is usually recommended?","e":"Start with what matters most, while the body is fresh.","o":["Strength first, cardio after","Hard cardio first, strength after","In between the sets","It doesn't matter at all"]},"program-nrm8ac":{"q":"What is a PPL split?","e":"A popular split for trainees who come 3–6 times a week.","o":["Push, pull and legs on separate days","Full body in every session","Cardio only, three different types","One long workout per week"]},"program-1jsn3lu":{"q":"A trainee improved very quickly in the first months, and now progress has slowed. What is the reasonable explanation?","e":"This is the natural phenomenon of diminishing returns – which is why advanced trainees need periodization and load planning.","o":["The more you progress, the less room there is to improve, and planning is needed","They've probably stopped doing the exercises correctly and are compensating with momentum","They need to change all the exercises every week","It's a clear sign of a developing injury"]},"program-1abpbhd":{"q":"What is the purpose of a fitness test at the start?","e":"Repeating the test after a few weeks shows the trainee their improvement – which also boosts motivation.","o":["A starting point, tailoring the program and measuring progress","Showing the trainee how weak they still are so they try harder","Choosing suitable equipment and clothes for them","It has no real purpose beyond ritual"]},"program-1rccr4j":{"q":"What usually suits a beginner?","e":"Repeating basic movement patterns builds technique and strength faster.","o":["A few basic exercises that repeat","Lots of new exercises every workout","Isolation machines only","A professional athlete's program"]},"program-1ix5l2i":{"q":"What is the 'rest-pause' method?","e":"An intense method that adds volume in a short time. Suits experienced trainees, in moderation.","o":["A set close to failure, a short rest and a few more reps","10 minutes of rest between every two sets to fully recover","A one-second pause at the bottom of every rep","Light walking between exercises instead of rest"]},"trx-vsa5wr":{"q":"How do the straps help in a TRX squat?","e":"That's why the TRX squat suits learning the movement and older populations.","o":["They take off some of the load and help with balance","They add weight and make the exercise harder","They completely eliminate leg work","They have no effect on the exercise"]},"trx-19p8sbk":{"q":"In a TRX biceps curl (facing the anchor), what makes the exercise harder?","e":"Like the row – body angle determines the load.","o":["Feet forward – the body leans back more","Standing more upright and closer to the anchor point","Gripping the straps harder","Doing the reps faster"]},"trx-agj96b":{"q":"In which stance is a TRX triceps extension usually performed?","e":"The extension is a pushing movement – so the back is to the anchor.","o":["Back to the anchor, leaning forward","Facing the anchor, leaning back","Sitting on the floor","Lying on the back"]},"trx-12zj6nb":{"q":"What is a TRX 'atomic push-up'?","e":"An advanced exercise combining chest, shoulders and core.","o":["A push-up with the feet in the straps and a knee tuck","A push-up with only the hands in the straps","Jumping in place holding both straps and bracing the abs on each landing","A side plank with the top leg in the strap"]},"trx-ayxuet":{"q":"How does a plank change when the feet are in the straps?","e":"An unstable base demands more work from the core and shoulder muscles.","o":["Harder because of the instability","Easier because the feet are supported","No difference in difficulty","It trains only the arms"]},"trx-945dz2":{"q":"In a TRX hamstring curl (heels in the straps, lying on the back), which muscles mainly work?","e":"The exercise combines hip extension (lifting the hips) and knee flexion.","o":["The hamstrings and glutes","The chest and shoulders","The quadriceps only","The biceps"]},"trx-qdc6f7":{"q":"What is the risk when a trainee 'hangs' on their shoulders in the straps without control?","e":"Keep the shoulder blades active and the body tight throughout the exercise.","o":["Passive load on the shoulder and its ligaments","There's no risk with straps","It improves shoulder stability","It strengthens the shoulder faster"]},"trx-1whgv4u":{"q":"What is the difference between velocity and acceleration?","e":"To accelerate a weight you need force – so explosive movements require a lot of force.","o":["Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity","No difference","Velocity is the rate of change of acceleration over time","Acceleration exists only when going down"]},"trx-1jqwid7":{"q":"What does Newton's third law say?","e":"When you push the floor in a jump, the floor 'pushes' you up.","o":["For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction","A body stays at rest forever, even when a force acts on it","Force equals mass times velocity","Weight equals body volume"]},"trx-ho22w0":{"q":"What is 'ground reaction force'?","e":"In running and landing from a jump this force can reach several times body weight.","o":["The force the ground returns to the body when it pushes against it","Earth's gravity acting on the body during landing","The friction between the shoe and the floor","The force the muscle produces while contracting"]},"trx-twnqd3":{"q":"What does Newton's first law (the law of inertia) say?","e":"So force is needed both to start moving a weight and to stop it.","o":["A body continues in its state unless a force acts on it","Every body stops by itself after a while, even with no force","Force equals mass times acceleration","For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction"]},"trx-jo3tuv":{"q":"In a standing dumbbell curl, at what elbow angle is the torque greatest?","e":"When the forearm is horizontal, the horizontal distance between the elbow and the dumbbell is maximal.","o":["When the forearm is horizontal (about 90°)","When the arm is straight down","When the dumbbell is at the shoulder","The same throughout the range"]},"trx-ypuc3k":{"q":"What distinguishes a cable curl from a free-weight curl?","e":"With a free weight, resistance is always downward. With a cable it's in the cable's direction, so you can change where the exercise is hardest.","o":["Tension through more of the range, depending on the cable's direction","The cable is always lighter than a free weight","There's no difference between them","The cable has no resistance at all in part of the range"]},"trx-hx0k4f":{"q":"Why does a second-class lever give a mechanical advantage?","e":"This lets you move a large load with relatively little force – like a wheelbarrow.","o":["The effort arm is longer than the load arm","The load arm is longer than the effort arm","The axis is always in the middle","It has no mechanical advantage"]},"trx-172x14n":{"q":"In a squat, what happens to the torque on the lower back when the torso leans further forward?","e":"The more horizontal the torso, the greater the horizontal distance between the bar and the hips and lower back.","o":["It increases","It decreases","It doesn't change","It moves to the knees only"]},"trx-1lt1f62":{"q":"A 5 kg dumbbell creates the same torque on the shoulder at every arm angle.","e":"False. The torque depends on the horizontal distance from the shoulder – and it changes with the arm angle."},"trx-kw6ppu":{"q":"What is 'dynamic stability'?","e":"For example: keeping a stable knee when landing from a jump.","o":["Control and stability during movement","Standing without any movement","Extreme flexibility in a joint","Maximal force in one rep"]},"trx-70qn3i":{"q":"What is a 'closed kinetic chain' exercise?","e":"In a closed chain several joints work together, and the movement resembles everyday movements more.","o":["An exercise where the distal limb is fixed – like a squat or a push-up","An exercise where the limb is free to move – like the leg extension machine","An exercise done lying down only","An aerobic exercise"]},"trx-17ute1k":{"q":"Which of the following is an open kinetic chain exercise?","e":"In an open chain the distal limb is free to move – suitable for isolating a specific muscle.","o":["Leg extension machine","Squat","Dumbbell forward lunge","Push-up"]},"scenarios-2hb8yy":{"q":"A trainee arrives after a sleepless night and feels dizzy. What is right?","e":"Extreme fatigue increases injury risk. Persistent dizziness is a reason to stop.","o":["Ask questions, greatly reduce intensity or postpone","Give them a hard workout to 'wake them up'","Ignore it and continue with the regular program","Give them an energy drink and carry on as usual"]},"scenarios-u8zzvd":{"q":"A trainee wants to prepare for a military selection test in two months. What is the first step?","e":"The principle of specificity: train exactly for what is tested – and gradually, to arrive healthy.","o":["Find out what the test includes and build a graded program","Long runs every day until the test, to arrive at a peak","Recommend endurance-boosting supplements","Train only arms and shoulders for push-ups"]},"scenarios-1pvrdm2":{"q":"A new trainee doesn't know how to use a machine. What is right?","e":"Correct seat setup and range are part of safety.","o":["Demonstrate, explain and let them try with a light load","Tell them to find a tutorial video online and come back when ready","Choose a heavy weight for them so they learn fast","Ignore it – they'll figure it out"]},"scenarios-17of4lp":{"q":"A trainee says she doesn't want strength training because she 'doesn't want to bulk up'. What is a professional answer?","e":"Explain kindly, respect her goal and adapt the program.","o":["Explain that building muscle is slow and that strength training benefits her","Agree and give her cardio only, with no weights at all","Tell her she's wrong, without explaining why","Give her only 1 kg weights, forever"]},"scenarios-1l9ex9w":{"q":"A trainee is shy about asking questions. How do you help them?","e":"A trainee who feels comfortable asking learns faster and more safely.","o":["Create an open atmosphere, ask open questions and encourage them","Wait for them to ask on their own, so as not to pressure them","Tell them there are no stupid questions and move on","Quiz them in front of everyone"]},"scenarios-179xlfk":{"q":"Some participants arrive late to class. What is right?","e":"Warming up is part of safety – even for latecomers.","o":["A short, adapted warm-up before they join","Have them join the intense part straight away so they don't miss more","Don't let them take part in the class","Start the whole class over from the beginning"]},"scenarios-1f31a7c":{"q":"A trainee trains the same muscle groups every day and feels tired and stuck. What would you explain?","e":"Adaptation happens during recovery – not during the workout itself.","o":["Muscles need recovery time, and we'll split the workouts","They need to train even more to break through","It's happening because of a lack of the right supplements","They should stop training completely for a few months"]},"scenarios-fvf4kw":{"q":"A trainee is fasting (e.g. during Ramadan) and wants to keep training. What suits them?","e":"Adjusting the time, intensity and drinking keeps it safe.","o":["Lower the intensity and train close to eating hours","Train at maximal intensity in the afternoon, when the body is awake","Stop training completely for the whole month","Ignore the fast and train exactly as usual"]},"scenarios-k3jte3":{"q":"A trainee has mild back pain that gets worse with forward bending. What is right?","e":"Don't diagnose – adapt and refer as needed.","o":["Avoid what makes it worse, choose alternatives and refer if it persists","Add loaded trunk flexion to strengthen the back and relieve the pain","Ignore the pain and continue the program as usual","Diagnose a herniated disc and build a rehab program for it"]},"scenarios-ppvb4b":{"q":"A trainee leaves dumbbells on the floor and doesn't put them back. What would you do?","e":"Order in the gym prevents trips and injuries.","o":["Politely remind them of the gym's rules and safety","Ignore it – it's not your responsibility","Shout at them in front of all the trainees","Put them back yourself every time without saying anything"]},"scenarios-efb20r":{"q":"A trainee says training is boring. What can help?","e":"Enjoyment is one of the strongest predictors of adherence.","o":["Vary the exercises, add challenges and goals","Give up on them and focus on other trainees","Tell them training is supposed to be boring","Continue exactly the same program with no change"]},"scenarios-1m80ej8":{"q":"A trainee asks you to build a program for his 14-year-old brother. What is right?","e":"Teenagers can train safely – with adaptation, supervision and an emphasis on technique.","o":["Age-appropriate training, supervised and with parental consent","Give him exactly his older brother's program","Refuse – teenagers aren't allowed to train in a gym","Give him mainly heavy lifts to build strength"]},"scenarios-1efuy9v":{"q":"A trainee arrives at the gym in flip-flops. What is right?","e":"A dumbbell falling on a bare foot can cause a serious injury.","o":["Ask for closed, stable shoes","It's perfectly fine, no problem","Let them train barefoot","Ignore it and carry on with the workout"]},"scenarios-duthdo":{"q":"In the middle of a class a participant looks pale, has a cold sweat and is breathing heavily. What is right?","e":"A cold sweat and pallor are not normal signs of exertion.","o":["Stop, sit them down, monitor and call for help if there's no improvement","Tell them to continue slowly until the end of class and drink plenty of water","Let them go outside alone to get some air","Ignore it – it's normal in the middle of an intense class"]},"scenarios-1g4jf0l":{"q":"A trainee says: 'No pain, no gain.' What would you explain?","e":"Telling the 'burn' in a muscle from warning pain is an important skill for a trainee.","o":["Effort is part of training, but sharp pain is a signal to stop","They're completely right – without sharp pain there's no progress","You must not feel any effort or discomfort in training","Sharp joint pain means the training is really working"]},"scenarios-ktvop6":{"q":"A trainee wants to gain muscle mass. What is the professional approach?","e":"The instructor is responsible for training and gives general information; a personal meal plan is the dietitian's domain.","o":["Consistent strength training with progressive overload, good sleep and general nutrition guidance","Supplements only – they're enough to build muscle even without regular strength training","Cardio only, several times a day, at high intensity","Eat as much as possible, regardless of food type or training"]},"scenarios-npc1cf":{"q":"A trainee returns after a three-month break and wants to lift their previous weights right away. What is right?","e":"'Muscle memory' helps you come back fast – but tendons and joints need time to adapt.","o":["Start with a reduced load and progress gradually","Start straight from the weight they lifted before the break","Start from zero, like a completely new trainee","Add weight to 'make up' for what they lost"]},"scenarios-56n3i0":{"q":"A trainee asks you to estimate her body fat percentage by eye. What is right?","e":"Simple measures repeated over time are better than one inaccurate number.","o":["Explain it's not accurate and suggest circumferences, photos or strength","Give her an exact number based on appearance, and update it every month","Tell her body fat percentage isn't an important topic","Compare her to other trainees in the gym"]},"scenarios-1xdlpx":{"q":"A trainee is angry and shouting at another instructor in the middle of the gym. What is right?","e":"Calming down and moving to a private conversation prevents escalation.","o":["Stay calm, listen and suggest continuing to the side","Shout back to show authority","Ignore it, let them finish on their own, then report to management","Ask all the trainees to leave the gym"]},"scenarios-7a2tmq":{"q":"A trainee asks you to promise a 10 kg weight loss in a month. What would you answer?","e":"Realistic, safe expectations are the basis of long-term success.","o":["Explain that you can't promise, and build a realistic goal","Promise – with enough motivation it's possible","Recommend a one-week water fast","Tell them to give up because it won't work"]},"safety-pj759w":{"q":"A trainee is choking – they can't speak, breathe or cough. What do you do?","e":"When a person is coughing effectively – encourage them to keep coughing. When they can't – act immediately.","o":["Call for help and give back blows and abdominal thrusts","Give them water to wash down what's stuck","Wait a few minutes for it to pass on its own","Lay them on their back and wait for help"]},"safety-wkl7t7":{"q":"A trainee took a blow and has a nosebleed. What is right?","e":"Tilting the head back can lead to swallowing blood. Bleeding that doesn't stop – refer for a check.","o":["Sit them down, lean forward and pinch the nose","Tilt the head back and hold","Lay them on their back with the legs up","Ask them to jump to stop the bleeding"]},"safety-1c6uhn5":{"q":"A trainee got a minor cut from equipment. What do you do?","e":"A deep wound or bleeding that won't stop – refer for medical treatment.","o":["Gloves, rinse and a clean dressing","Ignore it and continue training","Apply oil to soothe the skin","Cover it with a paper towel and carry on"]},"safety-1umw98":{"q":"What raises suspicion of a fracture?","e":"Don't try to straighten it. Immobilize in the position found and refer for a check.","o":["Deformity, severe pain, swelling and difficulty using it","Mild muscle soreness the next day","Heavy sweating during exertion","General fatigue at the end of training and mild pain that passes with rest"]},"safety-185qj74":{"q":"A trainee fainted briefly and came round. What is right?","e":"Fainting during exertion requires evaluation – it may indicate a heart problem.","o":["Lay them down, raise the legs and refer for evaluation","Return them to training if they feel fine","Let them drive home right away and rest","Ignore it – it sometimes happens during exertion"]},"safety-qdkvez":{"q":"Which signs raise suspicion of a heart attack?","e":"Suspected heart attack – stop activity and call for help immediately (101 in Israel).","o":["Chest pain or pressure, shortness of breath and sweating","Knee pain when climbing stairs","Muscle soreness the day after training","Strong thirst at the end of a long workout"]},"safety-1wt3uz5":{"q":"What helps identify a stroke (FAST)?","e":"In a stroke every minute counts – call for help immediately.","o":["Facial droop, arm weakness and slurred speech","Leg pain after a long run","A fast heart rate during intense exercise with mild breathlessness","Heavy sweating on an especially hot day"]},"safety-b3q9dr":{"q":"A trainee gets a muscle cramp in the middle of training. What do you do?","e":"A recurrent or severe cramp – check fatigue, fluids and electrolytes.","o":["Stop, stretch gently and drink","Continue at full intensity until it passes","Massage hard until it hurts","Put ice on the whole body immediately"]},"safety-13ln98e":{"q":"What is the safest way to do a heavy squat without a spotter?","e":"Safety bars let you 'dump' a failed rep safely.","o":["In a rack with safety bars at the right height","In an open area with no equipment","With eyes closed for concentration","Only with a tight lifting belt and knee sleeves"]},"safety-4f160e":{"q":"Which shoes are suitable for training in the gym?","e":"Stable shoes protect the feet and improve stability.","o":["Closed and stable","Flip-flops","High heels","Socks only"]},"safety-1tnqb87":{"q":"It's acceptable to leave a new trainee to train alone on the equipment before they've been instructed.","e":"False. Initial instruction on the equipment is a basic part of safety."},"safety-1fdo7pl":{"q":"A machine in the gym looks faulty. What do you do?","e":"Using a faulty machine can cause a serious injury.","o":["Stop using it, mark it and report it","Continue carefully","Fix it yourself with whatever is around","Ignore it"]},"safety-1svtfw1":{"q":"What matters for hygiene in a gym?","e":"Hygiene reduces the spread of infections between trainees.","o":["Wiping down equipment and using a towel","Not drinking water during training","Training without shoes","Sharing water bottles with others"]},"safety-1ssby2":{"q":"A runner complains of a localized pain in the shin that gets worse with every run. What is right?","e":"Localized bone pain that keeps getting worse can indicate a stress fracture – don't ignore it.","o":["Stop and refer for a check – suspected stress fracture","Increase running volume to strengthen the bone","Run through the pain, it will pass with time","Just change shoes and carry on as usual"]},"safety-1vajc04":{"q":"A trainee fell from a machine and a head or neck injury is suspected. What is right?","e":"Moving them incorrectly may worsen a spinal injury.","o":["Don't move them, stabilize the head and call for help","Sit them up immediately to check if they're OK","Ask them to get up and walk a few steps","Give them water and continue the workout"]},"safety-1tx97eh":{"q":"What are the signs of heat exhaustion (as opposed to heat stroke)?","e":"In heat exhaustion: move to shade, give fluids, cool and monitor. Confusion – suspect heat stroke, an emergency.","o":["Heavy sweating, weakness and dizziness – fully conscious","Very high temperature with confusion and brief loss of consciousness","Strong shivering from cold and slowed speech","Muscle soreness the day after training"]},"safety-19bsjk9":{"q":"During CPR, if there is a defibrillator on site, use it as early as possible.","e":"True. Early defibrillation greatly improves the chance of survival."},"safety-11ti87l":{"q":"A trainee after heart surgery wants to start training with you. What is required?","e":"Work within the framework set by the care team.","o":["Clearance and instructions from the care team, and a gradual start","Nothing special – they can start like any trainee","Wait a whole year regardless of the instructions they received","Intense training to strengthen the heart as fast as possible"]},"safety-1iupb3s":{"q":"What do you do with the information from a trainee's health declaration?","e":"Medical information is sensitive – protect it.","o":["Keep it confidential and in a secure place","Pin it on the notice board so all instructors know","Share it in the WhatsApp group","Throw it away after a day"]},"safety-3xfp71":{"q":"A pregnant trainee feels abdominal pain or notices bleeding mid-workout. What do you do?","e":"Abdominal pain, bleeding, dizziness or fluid leakage in pregnancy – a reason to stop and refer immediately.","o":["Stop immediately and refer for urgent medical evaluation","Continue at low intensity","Let her rest five minutes, drink water and carry on carefully","Ignore it"]},"nutrition-pznukx":{"q":"What is the difference between simple and complex carbohydrates?","e":"Complex sources like whole grains and legumes also provide fiber, vitamins and minerals.","o":["Simple ones break down fast; complex ones more slowly","No difference in breakdown rate or values","Simple ones contain much more protein","Complex ones are found only in meat and fish"]},"nutrition-4msrma":{"q":"Which food is especially rich in dietary fiber?","e":"Fiber is found only in plant foods.","o":["Lentils and whole grains","Chicken breast and eggs","Olive oil","Yellow cheese"]},"nutrition-1fekca5":{"q":"What is the main role of iron in the body?","e":"Iron deficiency may cause fatigue and reduced endurance performance.","o":["Part of hemoglobin, which carries oxygen","Building strong bones and teeth","Regulating blood sugar","Digesting fats in the intestine"]},"nutrition-3u5ncy":{"q":"Who is at higher risk of iron deficiency?","e":"Diagnosis and treatment of a deficiency belong with a doctor, not guesswork.","o":["Women of childbearing age, vegans and endurance runners","Men who eat red meat several times a week","Anyone who does strength training","No one – iron deficiency is very rare"]},"nutrition-1ummzhw":{"q":"Which vitamin is produced in the skin with sun exposure?","e":"Vitamin D is important for calcium absorption and bone health.","o":["Vitamin D","Vitamin C","Vitamin B12","Vitamin K"]},"nutrition-1pll9l0":{"q":"Which vitamin is found almost only in animal foods, so vegans usually need it as a supplement?","e":"This is one of the important points in planning a vegan diet – together with the medical team.","o":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Vitamin A","Vitamin E"]},"nutrition-1rtvd7q":{"q":"What does 'empty calories' mean?","e":"For example sweetened drinks and candy.","o":["Energy with almost no vitamins, minerals or fiber","Food with no calories at all, so it doesn't affect weight","Water, tea and coffee without sugar","Leafy greens like lettuce and spinach"]},"nutrition-l19wz6":{"q":"Which of the following foods has the most protein per 100 g?","e":"Cooked chicken breast: about 31 g per 100 g. Egg: about 12.6. Yogurt: about 3.5. Rice: about 2.7.","o":["Cooked chicken breast","Egg","Cooked rice","3% yogurt"]},"nutrition-5cprtz":{"q":"About how many grams of protein are in one large egg?","e":"An egg of about 50 g contains about 6 g of protein.","o":["About 6 g","About 20 g","About 1 g","About 12 g"]},"nutrition-uqua4p":{"q":"What is true about trans fat?","e":"Industrial trans fat is found mainly in certain processed products.","o":["It's best avoided – it harms heart health","It's the healthiest fat for the body","It's found mainly in vegetables and fruit","It's essential for building muscle and recovery"]},"nutrition-1jtomxb":{"q":"Which fats are considered healthier?","e":"The general recommendation: prefer unsaturated fats over saturated and trans fats.","o":["Unsaturated – olive oil, nuts and fish","Trans fat from processed products","Saturated fat only, from red meat, butter and cream","No difference – all fats are the same"]},"nutrition-1io1u72":{"q":"Where are omega-3 fatty acids found?","e":"Omega-3s are essential fatty acids – the body doesn't produce them, so they must come from food.","o":["Oily fish, walnuts and flaxseed","White sugar and candy","White bread, white rice and whole-wheat pastries","Sweetened drinks"]},"nutrition-ujyays":{"q":"Roughly how many calories is a kilogram of body fat 'worth', by the common rough estimate?","e":"It's a rough estimate. In practice the body adapts, and weight loss isn't linear.","o":["About 7,700","About 1,000","About 500","About 20,000"]},"nutrition-1debixo":{"q":"A meal after 8 pm is more fattening than exactly the same meal at another time, regardless of total calories.","e":"False. What mainly matters is total calories over time. For some people late eating hurts their sleep – which is a different consideration."},"nutrition-4f14yo":{"q":"A glass of natural orange juice contains a similar amount of sugar to a glass of cola.","e":"True. Juice has about 8–9 g of sugar per 100 ml and cola about 10–11 g – a similar amount. Whole fruit is better – it has fiber and keeps you fuller."},"nutrition-mfk9ti":{"q":"What is true about a product labeled 'low fat'?","e":"Sometimes sugar is added to make up for the taste.","o":["Not necessarily low in calories – check the label","Always diet-friendly and suitable for weight loss, because it has less fat","It has no sugar at all","You can eat it without limit"]},"nutrition-116lxyb":{"q":"Per what amount are nutritional values usually shown on food labels in Israel?","e":"Sometimes a per-serving value appears too – check how many grams a serving is.","o":["Per 100 g or per 100 ml","Per unit only","Per kg","Per whole package only, regardless of the product's weight"]},"nutrition-ctee1c":{"q":"What does a red symbol on a food product in Israel indicate?","e":"The labeling is meant to help consumers choose healthier products.","o":["High in sugar, sodium or saturated fat","That the product is organic and naturally grown","That the product is strictly kosher","That the product is especially rich in protein"]},"nutrition-1ql60z4":{"q":"What is the role of sodium in the body?","e":"Most dietary sodium comes from processed food, not the salt shaker.","o":["Fluid balance and nerve conduction; excess raises blood pressure","Building muscle, recovering after training and increasing maximal strength","Carrying oxygen in the blood to the tissues","Producing vitamin D in the skin"]},"nutrition-1eo34k9":{"q":"What usually suits as a snack 30–60 minutes before training?","e":"Large amounts of fat and fiber digest slowly and may weigh you down in training.","o":["An easily digested carbohydrate, like a banana","A fatty, heavy meal","A big handful of nuts","A large, fiber-rich cabbage salad"]},"nutrition-lxydce":{"q":"About how many calories are in a tablespoon of olive oil?","e":"A tablespoon of about 14 g of oil – about 120 calories. Oil is healthy, but very energy-dense.","o":["About 120","About 20","About 400","About 5"]},"special-vloieh":{"q":"How much physical activity does the World Health Organization recommend for children and adolescents?","e":"Including muscle- and bone-strengthening activity at least 3 days a week.","o":["An average of 60 minutes a day","10 minutes a week","Two hours a month","There's no recommendation for children"]},"special-6iwcvk":{"q":"What matters when training teenagers who play competitive sport?","e":"Specializing too early and overload increase the risk of injury and burnout.","o":["Preventing overload, movement variety and enough rest","Training every day in the same sport with no rest, to reach a high level","Training like professional adults","Avoiding strength training"]},"special-ebm0tb":{"q":"What is the main benefit of physical activity for people with type 2 diabetes?","e":"Activity is an important part of treatment – coordinated with the care team.","o":["Improved insulin sensitivity and blood sugar control","A complete cure of the diabetes within a few months","There's no special benefit in diabetes","Ending the need for any medication"]},"special-zku2ws":{"q":"A trainee with knee osteoarthritis has been cleared to train. What is usually recommended?","e":"Strong muscles around the joint and adapted movement usually reduce pain and improve function.","o":["Strengthening around the knee and low-impact activity","Intense jumping to strengthen the cartilage and improve flexibility","Resting the knee completely forever","Long runs down steep descents"]},"special-j67a2n":{"q":"How does regular aerobic training affect high blood pressure?","e":"Regular activity is part of the lifestyle treatment recommendations – coordinated medically.","o":["It can help lower blood pressure","It always raises it permanently, even in healthy people","It has no effect","It's completely forbidden"]},"special-hnnb4r":{"q":"A pregnant trainee wants to keep doing strength training. What is right?","e":"Avoid prolonged breath-holding, prolonged lying on the back from the second trimester, and fall risk.","o":["Usually possible, with clearance, moderate loads and adjustments","She must never lift weights during pregnancy","She can continue with maximal loads as before the pregnancy","Only ab exercises are allowed, to strengthen the core"]},"special-ub28l3":{"q":"Which simple test checks leg strength in older adults?","e":"An easy test that reflects everyday functional ability.","o":["30-second chair stand","A 2,000 m run","A barbell back squat","A standing vertical jump"]},"special-1llcb9b":{"q":"What does the 'Timed Up and Go' test check?","e":"A longer time than expected may indicate an increased fall risk.","o":["Mobility and balance: standing up, walking and returning","Maximal arm and grip strength","Cardio-respiratory endurance in a long 6-minute walk","Back and hip flexibility"]},"special-1wcsbgo":{"q":"What suits working with a trainee with significant excess weight in exercises that require getting down to the floor?","e":"Comfort and respect encourage consistency.","o":["Adapt the positions – standing, on a bench or against a wall","Make them get down to the floor in every exercise so they get used to it fast","Drop core exercises completely","Ignore their discomfort"]},"special-19n08bn":{"q":"A trainee with controlled epilepsy wants to train. What is right?","e":"An open conversation with the trainee about the instructions they received is the basis.","o":["Usually they can; know the seizure procedure and be careful with heights and water","They must not train in any way","Only light cardio is allowed, with no weights and no machines","Only when a doctor is with them every session"]},"special-14zm4cf":{"q":"A trainee has a seizure. What do you do?","e":"A seizure lasting more than a few minutes, an injury or a first seizure – call for help.","o":["Protect the head, clear objects away and put nothing in the mouth","Hold them down hard to stop the seizure and prevent injury","Put an object in the mouth so they don't swallow their tongue","Pour cold water on them to wake them"]},"special-oa6bb":{"q":"A trainee with stable heart disease and medical clearance. How do you monitor training intensity?","e":"Work within the framework set by the care team.","o":["According to the instructions, with an effort scale and gradual increases","By how they feel, with no monitoring and no effort scale","Always at maximal intensity to strengthen the heart","No monitoring – the medical clearance is enough"]},"special-1p6mwet":{"q":"In people with osteoporosis, which movements require special caution?","e":"Such movements load the vertebrae. Prefer a neutral spine and balance work.","o":["Loaded forward bending and forceful twisting","Walking at a comfortable pace on flat ground and climbing stairs","Balance exercises with support","A light machine leg press"]},"special-xlh8ht":{"q":"Which exercises usually suit as a start for a trainee with low-back pain who has been cleared?","e":"Start with comfortable movement and progress gradually according to the response.","o":["Walking and stabilization exercises – bird dog and bridge","Heavy deadlifts from the first day","Fast crunches, many reps, to strengthen the back quickly","Depth jumps from a high box"]},"special-uuv3dv":{"q":"What is true about the sense of thirst in older adults?","e":"Older adults are at increased risk of dehydration – especially in the heat.","o":["It tends to decline – they need reminders to drink","It's stronger than in young people","It doesn't change with age at all","There's no need to drink during training"]},"special-2l2pbc":{"q":"People with controlled asthma can usually take part in full physical activity.","e":"True. Many top athletes live with asthma."},"special-k8n522":{"q":"Which activity usually suits the first weeks after giving birth, with the care provider's approval?","e":"Return to activity gradually, with an emphasis on the pelvic floor and core.","o":["Walking, breathing and pelvic floor exercises","Light jumping and running to strengthen the pelvic floor","Heavy lifting","Intense crunches"]},"special-l1uj7y":{"q":"What is 'diastasis recti' (abdominal separation)?","e":"If it's suspected, refer to a physiotherapist who specializes in it.","o":["An increased gap between the two halves of the rectus abdominis","A tear in the back of the thigh muscle","A small fracture in one of the ribs","Chronic back pain that appears after birth because of breastfeeding"]},"special-1qyhst2":{"q":"What helps when working with a trainee with a hearing impairment?","e":"Ask the trainee what works for them – they're the expert on their own needs.","o":["Demonstration, eye contact and written instructions","Only speaking very loudly","Avoiding instruction so as not to embarrass them","Letting them train alone"]},"special-1g7ppiy":{"q":"Why is strength training important for women in menopause?","e":"During this period bone density declines faster, and resistance training helps slow it.","o":["To maintain muscle and bone density","It's not important at this age","Only for weight loss and fat burning","To avoid cardio"]},"psych-1hdqd3i":{"q":"What is the 'Pygmalion effect' in training?","e":"The instructor's attitude affects the trainee's belief in their own ability.","o":["The coach's supportive expectations improve performance","A trainee succeeds only if they're born talented","The coach's expectations don't affect performance","Harsh criticism always improves performance"]},"psych-v88uqg":{"q":"What is 'observational learning' (modeling)?","e":"A good, clear demonstration is one of an instructor's strongest tools.","o":["Learning by watching a demonstration","Learning with numbers only","Learning with eyes closed","Working in front of a mirror only"]},"psych-15n4hpk":{"q":"What is the difference between intrinsic and extrinsic feedback?","e":"The goal is for the trainee to learn to recognize on their own when the movement is good.","o":["Intrinsic – from the body's own sensations; extrinsic – from a coach or video","No difference – both come from the same source","Intrinsic is given only by the instructor; extrinsic comes from the muscles themselves","Extrinsic is only the feeling in the muscles"]},"psych-19yu30f":{"q":"What is an 'external focus of attention' cue?","e":"Many studies show an external focus often improves performance and motor learning.","o":["A cue focused on an outcome in the environment – e.g. 'push the floor away'","A cue about a specific muscle – e.g. 'squeeze your quads on every rep'","Silence","A cue about breathing only"]},"psych-1xpvz6l":{"q":"Why is it worth finding out a trainee's personal 'why'?","e":"For example: 'I want to be able to play with my kids without getting tired.'","o":["A deep personal motive strengthens consistency","Because it's polite to ask at the start","Because it's required by law","It has no special importance"]},"psych-meo8kh":{"q":"What helps cope with stress before a fitness test or competition?","e":"A familiar routine creates a sense of control and reduces stress.","o":["Calm breathing, a fixed routine and focusing on the process","Thinking only about the final result you want","Not sleeping the night before to stay sharp","Changing your whole routine on the test day itself"]},"psych-1f9dh4j":{"q":"What is diaphragmatic breathing and how does it help?","e":"A simple tool for reducing tension before exertion or at the end of a workout.","o":["Slow, deep breathing into the belly that calms","Fast, shallow breathing into the chest","Holding the breath for a long time before exertion","Breathing through the mouth only"]},"psych-1g981sc":{"q":"What is 'positive self-talk'?","e":"For example: 'One more rep, I can do it.'","o":["Encouraging phrases you say to yourself during effort","Talking with the instructor about the program and progress","Harsh self-criticism to improve","Complete silence during training"]},"psych-1y7pgh0":{"q":"How does sharing a goal with a supportive person usually affect consistency?","e":"A training partner or an interested friend adds accountability and enjoyment.","o":["It can strengthen commitment and support","It always hurts","No effect","It replaces the need to train"]},"psych-obkapg":{"q":"What characterizes burnout in a trainee?","e":"Variety, rest and bringing back enjoyment help deal with burnout.","o":["Emotional exhaustion and reduced enjoyment","Rapid improvement in performance","Increased joy and energy","Better sleep than usual"]},"psych-f2khpj":{"q":"Which sign may indicate training overload?","e":"In such a case, check loads, recovery and sleep – and consider a deload.","o":["Lower motivation, irritability and poor sleep","Consistent improvement from workout to workout","Normal appetite and good energy","Feeling fresh in the morning"]},"psych-1v6tbn9":{"q":"What is 'ambivalence' in a motivational conversation?","e":"It's a natural state. The instructor's role is to help the trainee explore both sides.","o":["Mixed feelings – wanting to change and also being afraid","Complete resistance to any change","A final, determined decision to change, with no hesitation","Complete lack of interest in the topic"]},"psych-wghfuu":{"q":"What is 'change talk'?","e":"For example: 'I'd like to feel stronger.' Reinforce such statements with reflection.","o":["The trainee's statements expressing desire or reasons for change","The instructor's cues during training to change technique","Criticism the instructor gives the trainee","Talk about topics unrelated to training"]},"psych-n39ol5":{"q":"What does the OARS acronym in motivational interviewing stand for?","e":"Open questions, Affirmations, Reflections, Summaries.","o":["Open questions, Affirmations, Reflections and Summaries","Objective, Action, Result, Success","Training, rest, nutrition, sleep, focus and consistency","Question, answer, question, answer"]},"psych-1hywa4k":{"q":"What is the 'fresh start effect'?","e":"An instructor can use such 'beginnings' to help a trainee start or come back.","o":["'New beginnings' in time increase willingness to change","Real change is possible only in January","Habits are built only in the morning","Time has no effect on change"]},"psych-5pifp6":{"q":"Constantly comparing yourself to others on social media always increases motivation to train.","e":"False. For many people it hurts self-image and motivation. Comparing yourself to who you were a month ago is better."},"psych-1e2iw3m":{"q":"A trainee can't perform an exercise and gets discouraged. What is better to do?","e":"Partial success builds self-efficacy and motivation to continue.","o":["Break it into steps and let them succeed at part of it","Tell them they're not built for this exercise","Keep asking for the full exercise","Switch to a harder exercise to challenge them"]},"psych-anmvsp":{"q":"What is 'graded goal setting'?","e":"Each sub-goal achieved strengthens the sense of progress.","o":["Breaking a big goal into sub-goals","One huge goal, with no steps","Changing the goal every day","Giving up on goals to reduce pressure"]},"psych-8wpolg":{"q":"What characterizes a 'mastery climate' in group training?","e":"In such a climate participants tend to enjoy more and stick with it more.","o":["Recognition for personal improvement and effort","Public ranking of all participants","Praise only for whoever wins","Ignoring the weaker participants"]},"psych-m228xc":{"q":"How does music usually affect training?","e":"Music whose tempo matches the movement works especially well in aerobic training.","o":["It reduces perceived effort and increases enjoyment","It always hurts concentration and performance, especially in strength training","It has no effect on training","It's forbidden in professional training"]},"anatomy-1xxc6xg":{"q":"Why is it harder to contract the hamstrings strongly when the hip is deeply flexed and the knee fully bent?","e":"A two-joint muscle that shortens across both joints at once loses some of its ability to produce force.","o":["Active insufficiency – it's shortened across both joints","Because the hamstrings don't cross the knee joint at all","Because the quadriceps block the movement during contraction","Because the hamstrings have no fast-twitch fibers at all"]},"anatomy-1xmtk5i":{"q":"Which muscle is the main stabilizer of the scapula against the rib cage, with weakness linked to a 'winged scapula'?","e":"The serratus anterior holds the scapula against the rib cage and rotates it upward.","o":["The serratus anterior","The middle deltoid","The latissimus dorsi","The triceps"]},"anatomy-qv18hi":{"q":"Which muscles form the shoulder's rotator cuff?","e":"The four muscles stabilize the head of the humerus in the socket.","o":["Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis","Anterior deltoid, posterior deltoid, upper trapezius, latissimus dorsi and pectoralis major","Biceps, triceps, brachialis and coracobrachialis","Rhomboids, levator scapulae, serratus and trapezius"]},"anatomy-1p5pxid":{"q":"In which movement is the subscapularis especially active?","e":"It's the only rotator cuff muscle that rotates the shoulder inward.","o":["Internal rotation of the shoulder","External rotation of the shoulder","Abduction overhead","Elbow extension"]},"anatomy-uerht0":{"q":"Why does the soleus work more in a seated calf raise (knees bent) than standing?","e":"When the knee is bent the gastrocnemius 'slackens', and the soleus – which crosses only the ankle – carries most of the work.","o":["The gastrocnemius crosses the knee and is shortened when it bends","The soleus crosses the knee and is stretched when seated","When seated the load on the ankle is heavier","There's no difference in muscle work between the two"]},"anatomy-123erk3":{"q":"What is the difference between the glenohumeral joint and the scapulothoracic joint?","e":"Raising the arm fully overhead requires both to work together – 'scapulohumeral rhythm'.","o":["Glenohumeral: the head of the humerus in the shoulder-blade socket; scapulothoracic: the scapula moving on the rib cage","They're two different names for exactly the same shoulder joint","The glenohumeral is the elbow joint, and the scapulothoracic is the shoulder joint","The scapulothoracic is the connection between the collarbone and the sternum"]},"physio-kgu9kk":{"q":"What is the size principle (Henneman) in motor unit recruitment?","e":"So sets that get close to failure recruit the large units too – even with a moderate load.","o":["Small, slow units first; large ones as effort increases","Large, fast units are always recruited first, at any intensity","All units are recruited together in every effort","Recruitment is random and changes from rep to rep"]},"technique-1g6ltz3":{"q":"In the deadlift, what does 'pull the slack out of the bar' mean before the lift?","e":"That way the body enters the lift tight and stable, without a 'jerk' that rounds the back.","o":["Creating tension in the body and bar before the weight leaves the floor","Yanking the bar off the floor quickly to get past the sticking point","Relaxing the back for a moment before starting the pull","Taking weight off the bar before the heavy set"]},"technique-1coi4gi":{"q":"In the bench press, why is it usually recommended not to flare the elbows to 90 degrees from the body?","e":"Fully flared elbows increase the load on the front of the shoulder.","o":["An angle of about 45–70° loads the shoulder less","At 90° the chest doesn't work at all and the shoulder does everything","A 90° angle adds weight to the bar","It makes no practical difference"]},"technique-1efci3e":{"q":"In a squat, a trainee 'falls' forward onto the toes at the bottom of the movement. What is the common cause?","e":"You can work on ankle range, raise the heels slightly or choose a goblet squat for learning.","o":["Limited ankle range or weight shifting forward","Excessive quadriceps strength compared with the hamstrings","A stance too wide relative to the pelvis","Breathing too deeply before going down"]},"technique-1c839t3":{"q":"In a bent-over barbell row, what is a sign that the weight is too heavy?","e":"When the torso 'bounces', the load shifts to the lower back and less to the upper back.","o":["The torso rises and momentum comes from the back","The back stays stable throughout the set, even on the last rep","The bar reaches the belly on every rep","The shoulder blades squeeze at the end of every rep"]},"technique-7zoj2e":{"q":"In a pull-up, what is the role of scapular depression at the start of the movement?","e":"Starting from an 'active hang' protects the shoulder and improves lat recruitment.","o":["Engaging the back and stabilizing the shoulder","Increasing momentum from the legs","Relieving only the biceps","It has no real role"]},"technique-1o6cu8x":{"q":"In a hip thrust, where is the upper back usually placed?","e":"This position lets the pelvis move through a full range without loading the neck.","o":["On the edge of the bench, at the level of the bottom of the shoulder blades","On the neck, above the shoulder blades","On the lower back, above the pelvis","On the head and neck, like a gymnast's advanced bridge"]},"technique-19p3knm":{"q":"In a standing overhead press, what helps prevent excessive lower-back arching?","e":"Over-arching is a way of 'cheating' – and it loads the lower back.","o":["Squeezing the glutes and abs and keeping the ribs 'down'","Leaning the torso back to make room","Locking the knees hard throughout the set","Pressing very fast to finish quickly"]},"program-5v8o39":{"q":"What does 'volume' mean in strength training, when calculated as 'volume load'?","e":"For example: 3 sets × 10 reps × 50 kg = 1,500 kg.","o":["Sets × reps × weight","The number of workouts per month","Workout duration in minutes only","Average heart rate"]},"program-1ftxiu1":{"q":"What is 'block periodization'?","e":"Each block builds a base for the next one.","o":["Consecutive blocks, each emphasizing a different quality","Changing all the exercises every single workout","Identical full-body training all year round","A full month's break after every two months"]},"program-73syiw":{"q":"A trainee squats 100 kg × 5 reps. Using the Epley formula, what is the estimated 1RM?","e":"1RM ≈ weight × (1 + reps/30) = 100 × (1 + 5/30) ≈ 117.","o":["About 117 kg","About 105 kg","About 150 kg","About 125 kg"]},"program-91232":{"q":"What is the 'interference effect'?","e":"The effect is usually small, and depends mainly on the volume and type of aerobic work. Separating them in time helps.","o":["High-volume endurance work may slightly slow strength and muscle gains","Strength training always severely harms endurance","Long sleep harms training results","Music disturbs concentration during training"]},"program-1m1wvi4":{"q":"What characterizes 'potentiation' training (PAP / PAPE)?","e":"For example a heavy squat, a few minutes' rest, then jumps. The effect varies between people.","o":["A heavy exercise before a similar explosive exercise","A static stretch before every set","An hour's rest between exercises","Aerobic training before strength training"]},"trx-6at7dp":{"q":"In a TRX row, a trainee takes one step back (away from the anchor). What happens to the difficulty?","e":"In a row: feet forward (body horizontal) = harder; a step back = easier.","o":["It decreases – the body is more upright","It increases – the body is more horizontal","No change in difficulty","The exercise becomes a push"]},"trx-17uyfsg":{"q":"What increases the difficulty of a side plank with the feet in the straps?","e":"The lateral core muscles and shoulder also have to stabilize against swaying.","o":["An unstable base and a longer lever","Reducing the angle of the body to the floor","Holding a handle in the top hand","No difference compared with a plank on the floor"]},"trx-bqgfkj":{"q":"In a TRX reverse fly, which muscles mainly work?","e":"The movement is horizontal abduction of the arms with the shoulder blades squeezing together.","o":["The rear deltoid and the scapular retractors","The pectoralis major and anterior deltoid","The biceps and forearms","The quadriceps and glutes"]},"trx-1ykacud":{"q":"Which TRX change adds a stability challenge without changing the body angle?","e":"A smaller base of support raises the demand for balance and stabilization.","o":["One leg or a narrower base","Taking a step back","Shortening the straps significantly","Holding with both hands together"]},"trx-1ncj1kt":{"q":"What is the body's 'center of gravity' and how does it relate to stability?","e":"So a wide, low stance is more stable than a narrow, tall one.","o":["The point where weight is concentrated; more stable when low and inside the base","The point where the heart is, determining breathing rate and body stability","A fixed point in the middle of the chest that doesn't change with position","A concept unrelated to stability or balance"]},"trx-8vexdq":{"q":"Why does the machine leg press usually allow more weight than a barbell squat?","e":"Less stabilization demand = you can load the prime movers more.","o":["The machine stabilizes, and the load isn't on the spine","The legs are stronger seated than standing","The machine adds its own force to the movement","Not true – you lift more in the squat"]},"trx-chwzq4":{"q":"What is the difference between work and power in the physics of training?","e":"The same lift done faster requires more power.","o":["Work = force × distance; power = work per unit of time","They're exactly the same, just with different names","Power is only the weight lifted, regardless of time","Work is the speed at which the weight is lifted"]},"trx-1mv20jp":{"q":"In a TRX chest press, why might handles that are too wide load the shoulder?","e":"Keep the straps at shoulder width and the range controlled.","o":["The arms stretch into an extreme range without control","The handles weigh more and tire the shoulder","The chest doesn't work so the shoulder works alone","There's no risk with that handle width"]},"trx-17j0hwd":{"q":"What is the role of friction between the shoes and the floor in leaning TRX exercises?","e":"On a slippery surface it's hard to keep a stable angle – and that's a fall risk.","o":["It prevents slipping and lets you hold the angle","It increases body weight on the straps and makes it harder","It has no role in the exercise","It shortens the straps during the movement"]},"scenarios-1j3svh9":{"q":"A trainee with type 1 diabetes feels shaky, sweaty and confused mid-workout. What do you do first?","e":"These are signs of hypoglycemia. If they aren't fully conscious – give nothing by mouth and call for help.","o":["Stop and give fast-acting sugar according to the instructions","Let them continue slowly until the end of the workout","Give them only water and wait until they feel better","Wait for it to pass, it's just fatigue"]},"scenarios-wq65in":{"q":"A trainee asks you to approve continuing training after the doctor told them to stop. What is right?","e":"The instructor works within the medical framework – not against it.","o":["Don't approve it, and encourage them to go back to the doctor with their questions","Approve it, as long as they feel good in training and don't complain of pain","Give them light training without telling the doctor","Advise them to find another doctor who will approve it"]},"scenarios-2rivjz":{"q":"A trainee says she eats very little and trains twice a day to 'burn everything'. What is the professional response?","e":"This may indicate a problem that needs professional support. The instructor recognizes, supports and refers.","o":["Gently express concern and refer to a professional","Compliment her discipline and commitment","Add another workout so she burns more","Ignore it – it's her personal choice"]},"scenarios-1ai616t":{"q":"Two trainees want to use the same squat rack at a busy time. What is a good solution?","e":"Flexibility and courtesy keep a good atmosphere in the gym.","o":["Suggest alternating sets or an alternative exercise","Let the first one keep the rack for an hour, since he came first","Take both of them out of the rack","Choose based on which one is stronger"]},"scenarios-1sb089n":{"q":"A trainee asks for a program identical to a bodybuilding champion's that he saw online. What is right?","e":"You can take inspiration – but personalization is what works.","o":["Explain that it needs personalizing and build an adapted version","Give him exactly the same program, one to one, including the number of workouts","Refuse to help until he picks another goal","Tell him such programs will never work"]},"scenarios-nbj647":{"q":"In a group class, an older trainee can't keep up and looks embarrassed. What is better?","e":"Graded options for the whole group preserve dignity and enjoyment.","o":["Offer everyone difficulty options in every exercise","Stop the class and explain to her alone in front of everyone what to do","Suggest she skip the class","Ignore it and continue at the normal pace"]},"scenarios-ap2tdc":{"q":"A trainee says he takes steroids and asks how to build a program around them. What is right?","e":"The instructor doesn't advise on drug use. The trainee's health comes first.","o":["Explain the risks, don't advise and refer to a doctor","Build him a special program suited to the use and the cycle","Recommend an appropriate dose and cycle","Ignore the topic and continue with the program"]},"scenarios-14osse8":{"q":"A trainee wants to improve his 3 km run, but mostly does only strength training. Which principle is he missing?","e":"The body adapts mainly to what you train. Strength training helps, but doesn't replace running.","o":["Specificity – he needs to run, with a graded program","Overload in strength training – he needs more weight","Complete rest before every run","Flexibility and stretching only"]},"scenarios-1iw3pbf":{"q":"A trainee has trained for a month and says he 'doesn't see any change'. What is better to do?","e":"Improvement in performance comes long before change you can see in the mirror.","o":["Show him measures that improved and explain it takes time","Tell him to change the whole program from scratch and try another method","Double the number of workouts per week","Agree with him that the program probably isn't working"]},"safety-pdg87":{"q":"What is the correct sequence of actions when you find a person lying down and unresponsive?","e":"Every minute without compressions lowers the chance of survival.","o":["Safety, response and breathing, help and defibrillator, and compressions if not breathing","Give water, raise the legs and wait a few minutes for them to wake up, and only then call for help","Move them immediately to the shade and sit them up so they breathe better","Check the pulse for a full minute before deciding on any action"]},"safety-3q50lc":{"q":"At what rate is it recommended to perform chest compressions on an adult?","e":"At a depth of about 5–6 cm, letting the chest recoil between compressions.","o":["About 100–120 compressions per minute","About 30 per minute","About 200 per minute","About 60 per minute"]},"safety-lmgtkk":{"q":"What characterizes rhabdomyolysis (severe muscle breakdown), which may appear after extreme training?","e":"It's more common in new trainees who do far too intense a workout all at once. It can damage the kidneys.","o":["Unusual pain, swelling, weakness and dark urine","Mild muscle soreness the next day and a feeling of stiffness","Normal fatigue at the end of training","Strong thirst during training"]},"safety-1h7dta6":{"q":"How do you prevent rhabdomyolysis in new trainees?","e":"A 'crazy' first workout is one of the common causes.","o":["A gradual start and enough fluids","A first workout to complete failure","Training in heavy heat to sweat","Avoiding drinking during training"]},"safety-12mr5a1":{"q":"A trainee benches alone and gets stuck with the bar on their chest. What is the safest way out?","e":"Best of all – don't get there: train with a spotter or with safety bars.","o":["Roll the bar down toward the hips, or tilt it and let the plates slide off","Keep trying to push the bar until it goes up","Move the bar toward the neck and slide out from under it","Lie still, breathe deeply and wait for someone in the gym to notice and help"]},"safety-ojx71b":{"q":"In heat stroke, what is the most important step until help arrives?","e":"Heat stroke is an emergency – every minute of cooling matters.","o":["Rapid, aggressive cooling of the body","Covering them with a blanket to prevent shock","Letting them jog slowly to 'release heat'","Giving them a hot drink to balance out"]},"safety-gm5agz":{"q":"A trainee with a pacemaker wants to train. What is especially important?","e":"In some people with a pacemaker or heart medication, heart rate doesn't rise normally with exertion.","o":["Clearance, monitoring according to instructions and an effort scale","Not allowing them to train in any way","Training only by the usual target heart rate, like any other trainee","Ignoring the pacemaker and training as usual"]},"safety-1dwhtt5":{"q":"When is ice better than heat for an injury?","e":"Cooling relieves pain and swelling in the acute stage. Heat suits chronic muscle tension better.","o":["In an acute, fresh injury with swelling","For a tight, chronic muscle","Before training, as part of the warm-up","Never – heat is always better"]},"safety-w8xovg":{"q":"What warning signs require stopping training immediately and referring to a doctor?","e":"These are signs that may indicate a heart problem – don't ignore them.","o":["Chest pain, shortness of breath, dizziness or an irregular heartbeat","Mild fatigue at the end of a long workout","Heavy sweating on an especially hot day","Burning muscles in the last set and heavy legs the next day"]},"nutrition-c0byuh":{"q":"What characterizes a 'complete' protein?","e":"Animal sources, soy and quinoa are considered complete. Combining legumes and grains complements each other.","o":["All the essential amino acids","Mainly carbohydrate and a little protein","Lots of fat alongside the protein","Found only in plant foods"]},"nutrition-gvu3y5":{"q":"What is leucine and why is it important for building muscle?","e":"Animal protein sources and whey are rich in leucine.","o":["An amino acid that triggers muscle protein synthesis","A vitamin that helps absorb calcium and strengthen bone","A type of simple sugar that provides energy","A mineral found mainly in the bones"]},"nutrition-1ibt0ll":{"q":"What is true about a food's 'glycemic index'?","e":"Combining protein, fat and fiber in a meal slows the rise in blood sugar.","o":["How fast a food raises blood sugar","The amount of protein and fat in the food","The number of calories in a serving","The same value for all carbohydrates"]},"nutrition-3marsz":{"q":"What is the difference between 'glycemic index' and 'glycemic load'?","e":"Watermelon, for example, has a high index – but in a normal serving its load is low.","o":["The load also takes into account the amount of carbohydrate in the serving","No difference at all between the two measures","The load measures the amount of fat in the serving","The index is the one that accounts for serving size"]},"nutrition-uluf4c":{"q":"What is the role of calcium beyond bone health?","e":"Calcium ions are released inside the muscle cell and enable contraction.","o":["Essential for muscle contraction and nerve conduction","Directly producing energy in the cell during long exercise","Carrying oxygen in the blood to the tissues","Breaking down fat during exercise"]},"nutrition-tq0mn2":{"q":"How does caffeine affect performance in most people?","e":"There are big differences between people; caffeine late in the day may hurt sleep.","o":["It can improve endurance and reduce perceived effort","It always hurts performance and concentration, even in small amounts","It has no effect on performance","It directly builds muscle"]},"nutrition-1ybhme1":{"q":"What is creatine and how does it help?","e":"It's one of the most researched supplements. Personal supplement advice belongs with a dietitian or doctor.","o":["It increases creatine-phosphate stores and improves short efforts","A hormone that increases the rate of muscle building","A vitamin that improves protein absorption","A carbohydrate that provides energy in long efforts and improves aerobic endurance"]},"nutrition-hdv08u":{"q":"Why is weight gain in the first days of creatine use not a gain in fat?","e":"It's an increase in fluid in the muscle – not in body fat percentage.","o":["It increases water inside the muscle cells","It contains a large number of calories","It's stored in the body as fat","It slows the metabolic rate"]},"nutrition-1ns4bji":{"q":"What is the danger of drinking very large amounts of water during a long effort, without salts?","e":"In very long efforts, drink to thirst and include electrolytes.","o":["Hyponatremia – a dangerous drop in sodium","Dehydration despite drinking","A sharp rise in blood pressure","A stress fracture in the leg bones because of the fluid load"]},"nutrition-119df67":{"q":"Which of the following helps absorb iron from plant foods?","e":"Coffee, tea and calcium in the same meal may reduce absorption.","o":["Vitamin C in the same meal","Drinking coffee with the meal","Calcium in the same meal","Drinking tea with the meal"]},"nutrition-j74txa":{"q":"What is a moderate calorie deficit commonly recommended for weight loss while preserving muscle?","e":"A moderate deficit, enough protein and strength training help preserve muscle mass.","o":["About 300–500 calories a day","About 1,500 calories a day","A full fast of several days","No deficit needed at all"]},"special-jopgjy":{"q":"Regular physical activity benefits health at any age.","e":"True. The adaptation varies – but the benefit exists from childhood to old age."},"special-189xvjr":{"q":"What is especially important in training older adults?","e":"Balance and strength reduce fall risk and preserve independence.","o":["Balance, strength and a good warm-up","Long runs only, to protect the heart","Stretching only","Avoiding any weight"]},"special-1dnkcis":{"q":"What should be done before a trainee with a chronic disease starts training?","e":"Clearance and instructions from the care provider are the basis for safe training.","o":["Medical clearance and knowing the instructions they received","Nothing special – start as usual","Choose the right medications for them","A hard workout to test their limits"]},"special-drhgtn":{"q":"What is the right way to adapt training for children?","e":"For children, enjoyment and variety are the key to sticking with it.","o":["Through play, variety and enjoyable movement","Like adult training, just with fewer sets","Heavy weights only","Distance running only"]},"special-40at7f":{"q":"Why should people taking beta blockers monitor effort with an effort scale and not only heart rate?","e":"An effort scale (RPE) helps set intensity in such situations.","o":["The drug lowers heart rate, so it doesn't reflect intensity","The drug raises heart rate a lot, so they need to slow down","The drug has no effect on heart rate during exercise","Because they're not allowed to train while taking it"]},"special-x962kf":{"q":"What is recommended regarding breath-holding (Valsalva) in people with high blood pressure?","e":"Breath-holding sharply raises blood pressure.","o":["Avoid it and breathe continuously","Use it on every rep","It doesn't matter at all","Hold the breath only in the last set"]},"special-lsipei":{"q":"A trainee with type 1 diabetes has very high blood sugar before training and ketones are present. What is right?","e":"Exercising in this state may make it worse. The personal instructions decide.","o":["Don't train, and follow the care team's instructions","Train hard to lower the sugar","Eat a candy and train as usual","Ignore it and train as usual"]},"special-8byt9z":{"q":"What is true about strength training for children and teenagers?","e":"The claim that strength training 'stunts growth' isn't supported by research when training is planned and supervised.","o":["Supervised and with good technique – safe and beneficial","It stunts the child's growth and damages the growth plates","It's completely forbidden until age 18","Allowed only on machines, not with free weights"]},"special-1p6876j":{"q":"What is the main concern with prolonged lying on the back in late pregnancy?","e":"So side-lying, seated or inclined positions are preferred.","o":["The uterus pressing on blood vessels, which may cause dizziness","Damage to the back muscles from the load on the spine","A dangerous increase in joint flexibility","There's no special concern with lying on the back in pregnancy"]},"special-twwbd3":{"q":"Which type of activity is especially recommended for maintaining bone density?","e":"Bone strengthens in response to load. Swimming and cycling are great for the heart but less so for bone.","o":["Resistance and weight-bearing activity","Swimming only, at any intensity","Cycling only","Stretching only"]},"special-j2pu59":{"q":"What characterizes 'sarcopenia'?","e":"Strength training is one of the most effective tools for slowing the process.","o":["Loss of muscle and strength with age","Loss of bone density","Chronic joint inflammation","Increased flexibility with age"]},"special-in3g7q":{"q":"A trainee with type 2 diabetes who takes insulin has a certain risk in training. Which?","e":"It's important that they have a fast sugar source on hand and know the signs.","o":["Low blood sugar during or after training","A dangerous rise in eye pressure","An almost certain muscle tear every workout","There's no special risk in training"]},"psych-r66giw":{"q":"According to self-determination theory (SDT), which three needs feed intrinsic motivation?","e":"An instructor who gives choice, builds successes and creates connection strengthens all three.","o":["Autonomy, competence and relatedness","Money, fame, prizes and recognition from others","Fear, pressure and competition","Speed, strength and flexibility"]},"psych-1yirrfo":{"q":"What is the 'contemplation' stage in the stages-of-change model?","e":"At this stage, help explore the benefits and concerns – don't push.","o":["Considering changing soon but still undecided","Already acting regularly and consistently for several weeks","Not thinking about change at all","Has gone back to old habits"]},"psych-1qhpq4d":{"q":"What is the Yerkes–Dodson law regarding arousal and performance?","e":"The optimal level varies by task – a fine skill needs lower arousal.","o":["Best performance at moderate arousal","The higher the pressure, the better the performance","Arousal has no effect on performance","Best performance only in complete calm"]},"psych-tfwthk":{"q":"What is the 'overjustification effect'?","e":"So prefer feedback that emphasizes improvement and enjoyment, not just prizes.","o":["An external reward may weaken intrinsic motivation","External rewards always increase motivation","Harsh criticism strengthens consistency","Praise always hurts motivation"]},"psych-14ax2rm":{"q":"What are the 'sources of self-efficacy' according to Bandura?","e":"The strongest source is personal success. So build small successes.","o":["Mastery, observing others, persuasion and physical-emotional state","Innate talent only, which can't be changed by training or experience","External rewards only, like money and medals","Competing against others only, every workout"]},"program-1s5rf38":{"q":"Which of the following groups includes only components of physical fitness?","e":"In the narrow definition there are three basic components – endurance, speed and strength. The broad definition adds flexibility, coordination, balance, reaction time and agility. Combining them produces abilities like power and anaerobic endurance.","o":["Strength, endurance, speed and flexibility","Strength, weight, height and speed","Endurance, nutrition, sleep and water","Flexibility, posture, heart rate and breathing"]},"program-1dfwblo":{"q":"What is general physical fitness?","e":"General fitness lets you cope with unexpected physical demands in daily life – and supports health and quality of life.","o":["The ability of the heart, lungs and muscles to handle exertion without excessive fatigue","The ability to lift the heaviest weight in the gym and keep high muscle mass","A toned, muscular appearance","The ability to run a marathon in a competitive time"]},"program-1vipy7":{"q":"What is the difference between muscular endurance and cardio-respiratory endurance?","e":"For example: push-ups in a row = muscular endurance; a 5 km run = cardio-respiratory endurance.","o":["Muscular – a specific muscle over time; cardio-respiratory – the whole body in prolonged effort","No real difference – two names for the same ability, one used mainly by pro athletes","Muscular endurance is measured by running, and cardio-respiratory by push-ups","Cardio-respiratory endurance relates only to breathing, not the heart"]},"program-2hsyp":{"q":"Which fitness component is mainly expressed in a 100 m sprint?","e":"A short, maximal run relies mainly on speed and power.","o":["Speed","Flexibility","Cardio-respiratory endurance","Static balance"]},"program-ksitof":{"q":"What is agility?","e":"Agility combines speed, balance, strength and coordination – important in ball games.","o":["The ability to change direction quickly while staying in control","The ability to hold a static position for a long time without moving or losing balance","The ability to lift a heavy weight once","The ability to run slowly at a steady pace"]},"program-d4f5k":{"q":"Which of the following is considered a 'health-related' fitness component (not only performance-related)?","e":"Health-related components: cardio-respiratory endurance, muscular strength and endurance, flexibility and body composition. Agility, reaction time and coordination relate more to performance.","o":["Body composition","Reaction time","Agility","Throwing accuracy"]},"program-1wavcbp":{"q":"What is 'reaction time'?","e":"For example: the time from the starting gun to when the runner starts moving.","o":["The time from a stimulus appearing to the start of movement","The maximal speed reachable in a 100 m run","The time it takes heart rate to return to rest","The number of reps you can do in a minute"]},"program-1dl4tmn":{"q":"What is the difference between static and dynamic balance?","e":"Standing on one leg = static. Walking on a beam or landing from a jump = dynamic.","o":["Static – staying stable in place; dynamic – staying stable while moving","Static is done only lying on the back, and dynamic only standing on one leg with eyes open","No difference – both are measured with the same test","Dynamic exists only in professional athletes"]},"program-1ed26t3":{"q":"What is coordination?","e":"Coordination relies on the nervous system – and improves with varied training and skill practice.","o":["The ability to coordinate different body parts for smooth, accurate movement","The ability to lift an especially heavy weight even when technique isn't perfect","The amount of air the lungs can hold","The ability to stick with training over time"]},"program-1ejcaw3":{"q":"Power (explosive strength) combines strength and speed.","e":"True. Power = the ability to produce a lot of force in a short time, as in a jump or a throw."},"program-fcczet":{"q":"In which event is anaerobic endurance especially important?","e":"An intense effort of about a minute relies heavily on anaerobic energy systems.","o":["The 400 m run","A long walk in nature","Yoga","The marathon"]},"program-1ohcvnf":{"q":"What is the 'reversibility principle'?","e":"'Use it or lose it.' So consistency matters more than occasional intense training.","o":["When you stop training for a long time, fitness declines","You can turn any exercise into another without affecting results","Reverse training is faster than regular training","The body returns to full fitness after one rest day"]},"program-19j7t31":{"q":"What is the 'principle of individuality'?","e":"Two trainees with the same goal may respond differently to the same training – so you adapt.","o":["Tailoring training to each trainee's ability, goals and condition","All trainees should do exactly the same program, so it's easy to compare them","Every trainee should train alone, without a group","Only professional trainees need a program"]},"program-1p2k8rs":{"q":"What is the 'principle of continuity' in training?","e":"Adaptation is built through regular training. Long breaks lead to a decline in fitness.","o":["Regular training over time, without long breaks","Training for several hours straight with no rest between exercises","Moving from exercise to exercise with no break at all","Training every day of the week with no rest day"]},"program-1tbotb":{"q":"Why is the 'principle of variety' important in training?","e":"Smart variety keeps motivation and challenge – without giving up consistency in the main exercises.","o":["It prevents boredom and burnout and allows continued progress","It removes the need for progressive overload and lets you train without effort","It lets you change goals every workout","It's needed only for children's training"]},"program-1fhyzmx":{"q":"What is the right balance between training and recovery?","e":"The body breaks down in training and rebuilds during recovery – without balance there's stagnation or overload.","o":["Enough load to stimulate, and enough rest to adapt","As much training and as little rest as possible","Lots of rest, training only when feeling very strong","Intense training only once a month, rest the rest of the time"]},"program-3fyval":{"q":"According to the principle of adaptation, the body responds to load through a process of 'breakdown and rebuilding' and gets stronger.","e":"True. This is the basis of training planning: load, recovery, adaptation – and again a slightly higher load."},"program-8zkebk":{"q":"What is continuous aerobic training?","e":"For example running, cycling or brisk walking for 30–60 minutes at an even pace.","o":["Activity at a constant intensity over time, with no breaks","Short, very intense efforts with long rests in between","Heavy lifts with 3 minutes of rest","Flexibility exercises only, seated"]},"program-34zmmt":{"q":"What is 'fartlek' training?","e":"'Speed play' in Swedish – change pace by feel or by the terrain.","o":["Continuous running with free changes of pace along the way","Running at exactly the same pace throughout, with no change in speed","Walking only on steep climbs","Weight training between runs"]},"program-3fuphk":{"q":"What characterizes interval training?","e":"The ratio of work to rest is set by the goal – e.g. 1:1 or 1:2.","o":["Intense effort segments alternating with recovery segments","Continuous running at a steady, comfortable pace throughout, with no breaks","Static stretches between strength exercises","Only one long workout per week"]},"program-18t5eug":{"q":"In what heart-rate range is moderate aerobic training usually done?","e":"In this range you can train for a long time and improve cardio-respiratory endurance.","o":["About 60%–75% of max heart rate","About 20%–30% of max heart rate","About 95%–100% of max heart rate","Above max heart rate"]},"physio-2vvqxa":{"q":"How is estimated max heart rate usually calculated?","e":"It's a rough formula. People vary a lot, so an effort scale is used too.","o":["220 minus age","200 plus age","180 minus body weight","Resting heart rate times 3"]},"technique-1qm591r":{"q":"What is static stretching?","e":"Usually held 15–30 seconds. Especially suitable at the end of training.","o":["Holding the muscle in a stretched position for a while, without movement","Fast bouncing movements at the end of the range to increase it quickly","Controlled movements through the full range of motion","Contracting the muscle against resistance"]},"technique-1rchn8o":{"q":"What is dynamic stretching?","e":"For example leg swings or arm circles – suitable for warming up before training.","o":["Controlled movements that gradually increase range of motion","A long hold of the muscle in a stretched position, with no movement at all, for a minute","Strong bounces at the end of the range","A stretch with a partner who pushes"]},"technique-2uct1l":{"q":"What is ballistic stretching, and what's the problem with it?","e":"Ballistic stretching triggers the stretch reflex and may overload the muscle and tendon. Not suitable for regular trainees.","o":["Bouncing movements at the end of the range – raise injury risk","A long hold at the end of the range – tires the muscle and reduces max strength","A controlled movement – doesn't improve flexibility","A partner stretch – requires special equipment"]},"technique-1dfejrp":{"q":"What is PNF stretching?","e":"For example: stretch, a 5–6 second isometric contraction, relax and stretch deeper. Effective for improving range.","o":["A muscle contraction followed by a stretch, usually with a partner","A fast bouncing stretch at the end of the range, repeated several times in a row","A slow walk to warm up before training","A short 3-second static stretch"]},"technique-1tm8bje":{"q":"A long static stretch (over a minute per muscle) right before an explosive effort may temporarily reduce strength and speed.","e":"True. Before training a dynamic warm-up is better; long static stretches suit the end of training."},"technique-gbs6o2":{"q":"What is the common test for hamstring and lower-back flexibility?","e":"Sit with straight legs and reach forward as far as possible.","o":["The sit-and-reach test","The Cooper test – a 12-minute run for maximum distance","A one-minute push-up test","A dynamometer grip test"]},"technique-c0hpeq":{"q":"What is the main goal of a warm-up?","e":"A warm-up raises muscle temperature, heart rate and blood flow, and prepares the joints and nervous system.","o":["Prepare the body for effort and reduce injury risk","Tire the muscles before the main workout so they work harder","Burn as many calories as possible at the start","Replace the main part of the workout"]},"technique-1ohsaf1":{"q":"What is recommended in the cool-down at the end of training?","e":"A gradual cool-down helps the body return to rest and reduces dizziness after intense effort.","o":["Light activity that gradually lowers heart rate, and stretches","A final max sprint to use up all remaining energy","An especially heavy set of the main exercise","Stopping completely and lying down immediately"]},"technique-n1sjyo":{"q":"Stopping suddenly after intense aerobic effort may cause dizziness.","e":"True. Blood 'stays' in the legs when the muscles stop working all at once. So finish gradually."},"safety-yzsl5s":{"q":"What is the difference between a sprain and a muscle strain?","e":"Ankle sprain = ligament injury. A 'pull' in the hamstring = muscle injury.","o":["Sprain – injury to a ligament around a joint; strain – injury to a muscle or tendon","Sprain – injury to bone or cartilage; strain – injury to the skin and fat layer beneath it","No difference – two names for the same injury","A sprain happens only in the shoulder, and a strain only in the back"]},"safety-10lgtnd":{"q":"What should you do first after an acute injury like a sprain?","e":"In the acute stage you protect the area and reduce swelling. Return to activity – gradually.","o":["Stop, protect the area, cool and elevate – and refer for a check as needed","Keep training at low intensity so the muscle 'doesn't cool down' and stiffen","Massage hard and apply heat right away","Do strong stretches of the injured area"]},"safety-5blot9":{"q":"What are 'shin splints'?","e":"Usually linked to a sharp rise in running volume, a hard surface or unsuitable shoes.","o":["Pain along the shin bone, common in runners who increased load quickly","A partial Achilles tendon tear that happens during a bad jump or landing","Shoulder joint inflammation in swimmers","Lower-back pain after deadlifts"]},"safety-ci3bu5":{"q":"What is an 'overuse injury'?","e":"For example tendinopathies and stress fractures. Prevented by gradual load increases and enough rest.","o":["An injury that develops gradually from repeated load without enough recovery","An injury that happens in one moment, in a fall or a collision with another player","An injury caused only by very heavy weight","An injury that happens only in older people"]},"safety-1ehqko8":{"q":"What characterizes an acute injury?","e":"For example an ankle sprain from a bad landing or a muscle tear in a sprint.","o":["It happens at a specific moment, with sudden pain","It develops slowly over weeks, with no clear moment of injury","It doesn't hurt at all at first","It happens only because of genetics"]},"safety-7hwh0d":{"q":"Which factor especially raises injury risk in training?","e":"Big jumps in volume or intensity are one of the most common causes of injury.","o":["Too sharp an increase in load, without gradual progression","An organized warm-up before every workout and full range of motion","Good, controlled technique","Enough sleep at night"]},"safety-usxavf":{"q":"What is tendinopathy?","e":"Common in the Achilles, patellar tendon and elbow. Treated by adjusting load and gradual strengthening.","o":["Ongoing tendon damage, usually linked to repeated load","A small bone fracture caused by a fall or a direct blow","Skin inflammation around a joint","A passing muscle cramp after a run"]},"safety-11nh8z3":{"q":"After an injury, return to activity should be gradual and follow the instructions the trainee received.","e":"True. Returning too fast is one of the most common causes of re-injury."},"psych-47ekfc":{"q":"What are the three stages of motor learning (Fitts and Posner)?","e":"At first you think about every detail, then you start refining, and finally the movement becomes almost automatic.","o":["Cognitive, associative and autonomous","Beginner, intermediate, advanced and professional","Warm-up, training and cool-down","Sight, hearing and touch"]},"psych-ffjo9h":{"q":"What characterizes a trainee in the cognitive stage of learning a movement?","e":"At this stage it's better to give a few simple cues and a clear demonstration.","o":["They think about every detail and make many mistakes","They perform the movement without thinking and correct themselves","They already correct themselves easily","They don't need a demonstration or feedback"]},"psych-v7l9k":{"q":"How many cues should you give a beginner at once?","e":"Too many cues confuse. One point at a time – add more once it's stable.","o":["One or two, short and clear","Seven or eight, to cover everything","None – let them learn alone","As many as possible, quickly"]},"psych-xtc3ib":{"q":"What is the difference between teaching a movement with the 'whole' method and the 'part' method?","e":"Complex movements (like Olympic lifts) are usually taught in parts, and simple ones as a whole.","o":["Whole – the entire movement together; part – learn pieces, then combine","Whole – suits only advanced trainees; part – suits only children and absolute beginners","No difference – both teach the same thing","Part – teaches only the end of the movement"]},"psych-3ht2kr":{"q":"What is 'random' practice compared with 'blocked' practice?","e":"Blocked practice gives fast improvement during the session; random practice usually improves long-term retention and transfer.","o":["Random – mixing skills; blocked – repeating one skill in a row","Random – no plan or order at all; blocked – always on the same day and time each week","Random – for beginners only; blocked – for advanced only","Random – without an instructor; blocked – with an instructor"]},"psych-1v9p0tm":{"q":"What is 'muscle memory' in the everyday sense?","e":"In practice it's learning by the nervous system – a movement pattern that has become fixed.","o":["The ability to perform a familiar movement almost without thinking, after lots of practice","Memory stored in the muscle fibers themselves that remembers the max weight lifted","The ability to remember all the muscle names","A muscle that remembers the max weight lifted"]},"anatomy-1yi2xof":{"q":"What is kyphosis (rounded back)?","e":"Common in people who sit hunched a lot. Upper-back strengthening and posture exercises can help.","o":["Increased curvature of the spine in the upper back","Sideways curvature of the spine, seen from behind","An increased arch in the lower back","A completely straight spine"]},"anatomy-1tklrcm":{"q":"What is increased lordosis?","e":"The lumbar spine is naturally arched – in increased lordosis the arch is larger than normal.","o":["An exaggerated arch in the lower back","An exaggerated rounding of the upper back","Sideways curvature of the spine","Shortened neck muscles"]},"anatomy-68acty":{"q":"What is scoliosis?","e":"If suspected – refer for a check. Training is adapted according to the care provider's instructions.","o":["Sideways curvature of the spine","Increased forward curvature of the upper back","An exaggerated arch in the lower back","A herniated disc in the lower back"]},"anatomy-1ku71iz":{"q":"How many natural curves does the spine have when viewed from the side?","e":"The curves help absorb shock and distribute loads.","o":["Four: cervical, thoracic, lumbar and sacral","Only two: one in the neck and one in the lower back","Two: neck and back","None – the spine is completely straight"]},"anatomy-lak8x9":{"q":"How many vertebrae are in the cervical spine?","e":"7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, followed by the sacrum and coccyx.","o":["7","12","5","3"]},"anatomy-kupmo0":{"q":"In which plane is a squat performed?","e":"The sagittal plane divides the body into right and left – forward and backward movements.","o":["The sagittal plane","The frontal plane","The transverse plane","No plane"]},"anatomy-e2onq1":{"q":"In which plane does trunk rotation (as in a throw) occur?","e":"The transverse plane divides the body into upper and lower – rotational movements.","o":["The transverse plane","The sagittal plane","The frontal plane","Such a movement doesn't occur in a plane"]},"anatomy-15h8igr":{"q":"A side lunge is performed mainly in the frontal plane.","e":"True. The step is to the side – a movement in the frontal plane."},"program-2q8eh2":{"q":"What is the 'Cooper test'?","e":"The distance covered in 12 minutes is used to estimate cardio-respiratory endurance.","o":["A 12-minute run for maximum distance","Lifting the max weight in the squat and bench press","A seated flexibility test","Counting resting heart rate for a minute"]},"program-qh1ybz":{"q":"Which test checks upper-body muscular endurance?","e":"A simple, common test. Beginners can also do it on their knees.","o":["The number of push-ups in a row","A 2,000 m run in the shortest time","The sit-and-reach test","A standing long jump"]},"program-ndr8hm":{"q":"Which test checks explosive leg power?","e":"A jump expresses force applied in a short time – power.","o":["A standing long jump or vertical jump","A 6-minute walk at the fastest pace possible","A plank to failure","Sit and reach"]},"program-1rhl419":{"q":"Why is it important to repeat a test under the same conditions?","e":"Similar time of day, warm-up, surface and equipment make results truly comparable.","o":["So the comparison is fair and reflects real progress","So the trainee gets used to expecting a good result and feels confident","Because you can't change anything in the training program","To save time explaining the test"]},"program-1n69kid":{"q":"What does a 'step test' check?","e":"You step up and down at a set pace for a few minutes and measure heart rate afterward – the faster it drops, the better the fitness.","o":["Cardio-respiratory endurance, by heart rate after stepping up and down","Max leg strength in one step-up onto an especially high step","Ankle flexibility on the way down","Balance on one leg"]},"program-t6787s":{"q":"What does a hand dynamometer measure?","e":"Grip strength is linked to overall strength and health, especially in older adults.","o":["Grip strength","Wrist flexibility","Reaction speed","Cardio-respiratory endurance"]},"nutrition-1rnl5xf":{"q":"What is BMI?","e":"BMI is a simple measure, but it doesn't distinguish between muscle and fat.","o":["Weight in kg divided by height in meters squared","Body fat percentage, measured with calipers","Waist circumference in cm","Body muscle weight"]},"nutrition-h658c8":{"q":"What is the main drawback of BMI?","e":"A muscular athlete may get a 'high' BMI despite a low body fat percentage.","o":["It doesn't distinguish between muscle mass and fat","It requires expensive equipment and a lab to calculate correctly","It suits children only","It measures only waist circumference"]},"nutrition-lbm15q":{"q":"A trainee is 1.80 m tall and weighs 81 kg. What is his BMI?","e":"81 ÷ (1.8 × 1.8) = 81 ÷ 3.24 = 25.","o":["25","20","30","45"]},"nutrition-18dzu9f":{"q":"Which body-fat measurement method uses a weak electric current?","e":"BIA devices are accessible and convenient, but are affected by hydration, eating and activity.","o":["Bioelectrical impedance (BIA)","Skinfold measurement with calipers","Circumference measurement with a tape","Calculating BMI from height and weight"]},"nutrition-bi3exe":{"q":"Why is waist circumference an important health measure?","e":"Fat around the abdomen is more linked to health risk than fat elsewhere.","o":["It reflects abdominal fat, linked to heart disease and diabetes risk","It measures abdominal muscle mass and core strength","It reflects core muscle strength","It determines metabolic rate"]},"nutrition-i0s69g":{"q":"Body weight alone doesn't tell how much of it is muscle and how much is fat.","e":"True. So combine measures like circumferences, body fat percentage, photos and performance."},"physio-z7vnn7":{"q":"What usually happens to resting heart rate after a period of regular aerobic training?","e":"The heart gets stronger and pumps more blood per beat – so fewer beats are needed at rest.","o":["It drops","It rises","It disappears","It changes only in children"]},"physio-xvgh61":{"q":"Which blood vessel carries blood from the heart to the body?","e":"Arteries carry blood from the heart; veins return it to the heart.","o":["Arteries","Veins","Capillaries","Lymph vessels"]},"physio-472hvp":{"q":"In which organ does gas exchange between air and blood take place?","e":"In the alveoli oxygen passes into the blood, and carbon dioxide passes out.","o":["The lungs – in the alveoli","The heart – in the ventricles, as blood passes from side to side","The liver","The kidneys"]},"physio-1bdi6ta":{"q":"What is the 'oxygen deficit' at the start of exercise?","e":"At the start of exercise the aerobic system is still 'warming up', and the body relies on anaerobic systems too.","o":["The gap between oxygen needed and oxygen supplied in the first minutes","A permanent oxygen shortage that athletes have at rest","The amount of oxygen left in the blood after exercise","A state in which the lungs take in no oxygen at all"]},"physio-c08l1a":{"q":"What is the role of hemoglobin?","e":"Hemoglobin is found in red blood cells and contains iron.","o":["Carrying oxygen in the blood from the lungs to the tissues","Breaking down fat in the muscle during long exercise","Producing energy from protein","Transmitting nerve signals"]},"physio-jv4mln":{"q":"In aerobic exercise, breathing and heart rate rise to supply more oxygen to the muscles.","e":"True. As intensity rises – breathing rate, breathing volume and heart rate all increase."},"physio-4l55h8":{"q":"Roughly what is a normal resting blood pressure in an adult?","e":"The top number – systolic (heart contraction); the bottom – diastolic (heart relaxation).","o":["About 120/80","About 180/120","About 60/30","About 200/100"]},"special-ie8zxg":{"q":"What is a 'sedentary lifestyle'?","e":"A sedentary lifestyle is linked to higher risk of heart disease, diabetes and excess weight.","o":["Most of the day sitting, with very little movement","Short strength training three times a week, without cardio","Walking 10,000 steps a day","Hard physical work all day"]},"special-7mmcf4":{"q":"What benefit does regular physical activity have for mood?","e":"Physical activity is linked to better mood, sleep and sense of capability.","o":["It can reduce signs of stress and low mood","It always increases irritability","It has no effect on mood","It affects only the body, not the mind"]},"special-1cebjci":{"q":"How does physical activity affect the risk of cardiovascular disease?","e":"Regular activity improves blood pressure, blood lipids and insulin sensitivity.","o":["It reduces it","It always increases it","It has no effect","It matters only after age 80"]},"special-3iozp7":{"q":"Brisk walking also counts as physical activity that benefits health.","e":"True. Brisk walking is moderate-intensity activity – accessible to almost everyone."},"safety-1henool":{"q":"Under Israeli law, what must a trainee provide to a gym before starting to train?","e":"Under the Gyms Law, a trainee is accepted only after providing a medical questionnaire with a signed health declaration.","o":["A signed health declaration","A copy of their ID card only","A recommendation letter from a previous trainer","Fitness test results"]},"safety-ok7gbe":{"q":"How often must a gym require a new health declaration from a trainee?","e":"Under the Gym Law, the health declaration is renewed every two years. A trainee who was required to provide a medical certificate renews it once a year.","o":["Every two years","Once a week","Once every ten years","Only the first time"]},"safety-husy94":{"q":"Under the law, when may a minor train in a gym?","e":"The law requires an instructor trained to work with minors to be present – and parental signature is also required.","o":["Only in the presence of an instructor certified to train minors","At any time, even with no instructor","Only if over 17 and with a signed approval from both parents","Never – minors don't train in gyms"]},"safety-1xmqkhc":{"q":"Under which law is the fitness and health instructor certificate issued?","e":"A gym instructor holds a certificate under the Sports Law, 1988.","o":["The Sports Law","The Planning and Building Law","The Consumer Protection Law","The Hours of Work and Rest Law"]},"safety-1s0itk8":{"q":"What should be checked regularly on gym machines?","e":"A worn cable or loose bolt may cause an accident. Report and take a faulty machine out of use.","o":["Cables, bolts, padding and locks – for wear or faults","Only the machine's color, cleanliness and cushion condition","Only that the machine is plugged in","Nothing – machines don't wear out"]},"safety-1ghtb26":{"q":"What must always be available in a gym?","e":"Knowing where equipment is and how to call for help saves precious minutes in an emergency.","o":["A first-aid kit and a clear emergency procedure","A coffee machine and loud music","Digital scales in every corner and a body-fat meter for each trainee","Supplements for sale"]},"program-13l1ecs":{"q":"What is the purpose of a training card (written program) for a trainee?","e":"Records allow planning progressive overload and seeing improvement – and also boost motivation.","o":["To record exercises, loads and progress over time","To replace the instructor's guidance, so the trainee trains alone","To set a full meal plan for them","To prove they paid for the membership"]},"scenarios-ddwa95":{"q":"A new trainee arrives without a health declaration and wants to start right away. What is right?","e":"It's a legal requirement – and also a tool that helps identify risks.","o":["Explain it's mandatory and have them fill it in before training","Let them train and fill it in next time","Skip it because they look healthy","Ask them to sign a blank page"]},"scenarios-vrbt8n":{"q":"On the health declaration, a trainee marked that they have chest pain during exertion. What is right?","e":"A positive answer to such a question requires medical evaluation before training.","o":["Don't start training until they present medical clearance","Start with light training and see how they feel","Ignore it – they probably marked it by mistake","Give them intense aerobic training"]},"nutrition-si47ek":{"q":"Which three macronutrients provide energy?","e":"Vitamins and minerals are essential for the body – but they don't provide calories.","o":["Carbohydrates, proteins and fats","Vitamins, minerals and water","Calcium, iron and zinc","Fiber, salt and sugar"]},"nutrition-nsyt3d":{"q":"What is recommended to eat after strength training to support recovery?","e":"Protein supports muscle building, and carbohydrate replenishes glycogen stores.","o":["A meal with protein and carbohydrate","Only water, no food at all","Only fat, to prolong fullness","Only green vegetables"]},"nutrition-v07fg2":{"q":"Vitamins and minerals provide energy (calories) to the body.","e":"False. They're essential for body processes, but they contain no calories."},"nutrition-gjiwim":{"q":"What characterizes the common 'healthy plate'?","e":"A simple model that helps build a balanced meal without counting calories.","o":["Half vegetables and fruit, a quarter whole grains and a quarter protein","Half protein and half carbohydrates, with no vegetables","A quarter vegetables and three quarters grains","Only protein and vegetables, with no carbohydrate at all"]},"anatomy-m0ajuo":{"q":"What connects muscle to bone?","e":"A tendon connects muscle to bone; a ligament connects bone to bone.","o":["A tendon","A ligament","Cartilage","A nerve"]},"anatomy-xv48ut":{"q":"What connects bone to bone at a joint?","e":"Ligaments stabilize the joint and limit abnormal movements.","o":["A ligament","A tendon","A muscle","Skin"]},"anatomy-18ximpn":{"q":"What is the role of cartilage in a joint?","e":"Cartilage covers the ends of bones and reduces friction.","o":["To allow smooth gliding and absorb shock","To connect muscle to bone","To produce blood cells","To transmit nerve signals"]},"anatomy-1ommr52":{"q":"Which type of muscle do we work in gym training?","e":"Skeletal muscle is under voluntary control. Cardiac and smooth muscle (as in the gut) work on their own.","o":["Skeletal (voluntary) muscle","Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Nerve muscle"]},"anatomy-180o3q8":{"q":"What is an 'antagonist' in a movement?","e":"The antagonist lengthens during the movement and helps control it.","o":["The muscle that acts opposite to the working muscle","The muscle that does most of the work","The largest muscle at the joint","The muscle that works only in the warm-up"]},"anatomy-1666w0r":{"q":"A muscle can only pull (contract) – it can't push.","e":"True. So every movement has a muscle that performs it and a muscle that brings it back the other way."},"technique-1vvpdmc":{"q":"What is the first thing to do before starting work on a machine?","e":"Correct adjustment aligns the machine's axis with the joint and keeps it safe.","o":["Adjust the seat and pads to your body","Load the heaviest weight","Do a fast set to warm up","Start without checking anything"]},"technique-8mnkj":{"q":"On a treadmill, what is right about holding the handrails?","e":"Holding on hard reduces the work and energy cost and disrupts your gait.","o":["Better to walk without leaning, holding only for balance","Always lean your full weight on the handrails","Grip hard to raise heart rate","Handrails are only for hanging a towel"]},"technique-83qdbq":{"q":"On an exercise bike, what is a suitable seat height usually?","e":"Too low a seat loads the knee; too high makes the pelvis rock.","o":["With the pedal at the bottom, the knee is almost straight with a slight bend","With the pedal at the bottom, the knee is bent at 90 degrees","The seat as low as possible","A seat so high the leg doesn't reach the pedal"]},"technique-4v0x67":{"q":"In a seated cable row, where do you usually pull the handle?","e":"Pull to the belly with the shoulder blades squeezing at the end – and a stable torso.","o":["Toward the belly or lower chest","Toward the neck","Overhead","Toward the knees"]},"technique-1qq9tx8":{"q":"On the pec deck machine, what protects the shoulder?","e":"Excessive stretching back loads the front of the shoulder.","o":["Handles at chest height and a comfortable range, without over-stretching back","Handles at ear height and a fast movement","Opening the arms back as far as possible","Fully locked elbows throughout the movement"]},"trx-874682":{"q":"What is the main advantage of suspension (strap) training?","e":"A small change in foot position changes the load – suitable for every level.","o":["Easy to change difficulty with body angle, and body weight is the resistance","You can lift very heavy weights with it","It suits only the arm muscles","It doesn't work the core muscles"]},"trx-yn0e1z":{"q":"What should you check on a TRX anchor before training?","e":"An unstable anchor may cause a fall – it's the first safety check.","o":["That it's firmly attached to a stable, load-bearing point","That its color matches the straps","That it's as low as possible","That it moves freely in all directions"]},"trx-113tr6h":{"q":"In what stance is a TRX row performed?","e":"A row is a pulling movement – so you face the anchor.","o":["Facing the anchor, leaning back","Back to the anchor, leaning forward","Seated with back to the anchor","Lying face down"]},"trx-xwlkc8":{"q":"In TRX, the closer the body is to horizontal, the harder the exercise.","e":"True. More of the body weight 'hangs' on the arms – and the load increases."},"scenarios-xg1cjn":{"q":"A trainee comes for their first session. What should you do first?","e":"A good introduction builds trust and safety – and lets you build a suitable program.","o":["A gym tour, an explanation of the rules and learning their goals","Give them the hardest workout there is","Send them to train alone","Ask them to lift a max weight"]},"scenarios-vz4jj1":{"q":"A trainee asks about an exercise you don't know. What is right?","e":"Honesty builds trust. No instructor knows everything.","o":["Admit it honestly, check a reliable source and get back to them","Make up an explanation so you don't look unprofessional","Tell them the exercise is dangerous without checking","Ignore the question"]},"scenarios-13qag0u":{"q":"A trainee complains they're always hungry after training. What is a suitable response?","e":"General information – yes. A personal meal plan – that's a dietitian's field.","o":["Give general information about a balanced post-workout meal, and refer to a dietitian if needed","Build them an exact calorie meal plan for the whole day","Tell them not to eat anything after training","Recommend an appetite-suppressant supplement"]},"scenarios-aw0pc":{"q":"A trainee does all exercises very fast and with momentum. What is best?","e":"Controlling the movement increases muscle work and reduces injury risk.","o":["Explain control and tempo, and demonstrate a slow, controlled rep","Add weight so they slow down on their own","Let them continue – the main thing is they're training","Tell them to do fewer reps, faster"]},"psych-6c6fu0":{"q":"What helps more to stick with training over time?","e":"Enjoyment, realistic goals and a regular habit are the basis for consistency.","o":["Finding an activity you enjoy and setting small goals","Training only when motivation is high","Training very hard at first and then resting for a month","Constantly comparing yourself to others"]},"psych-uhbl0v":{"q":"A goal like 'get fit' is clearer and more measurable than a goal like 'do 3 workouts a week for a month'.","e":"False. 'Get fit' is too general. A specific, measurable goal helps you plan and track."},"physio-49ylk9":{"q": "What usually happens to stroke volume as exercise intensity rises from rest to moderate?", "e": "Stroke volume rises early in exercise and levels off at moderate intensity. Beyond that, cardiac output keeps rising mainly through heart rate. In trained endurance athletes it may keep rising.", "o": ["It rises, and in most people levels off at about 40%–60% of VO₂max", "It drops as soon as exercise starts", "It doesn't change – only heart rate rises", "It rises only after 30 minutes of exercise"]},"physio-1dngzn8":{"q": "Which hormone raises blood sugar mainly by breaking down liver glycogen, for example when fasting or during long exercise?", "e": "Glucagon works opposite to insulin: it releases sugar from the liver into the blood when levels drop.", "o": ["Glucagon", "Insulin", "Melatonin", "Oxytocin"]},"physio-d5hwsg":{"q": "A low resting heart rate (e.g. 45) in a healthy endurance athlete usually comes from a large stroke volume.", "e": "True. A trained heart pumps more blood per beat, so it needs fewer beats at rest. A low pulse with dizziness or fainting is a reason to get checked."},"physio-1l49b2w":{"q": "What is 'cardiovascular drift'?", "e": "In long exercise, especially in the heat, you lose fluid and more blood goes to the skin. Stroke volume drops and heart rate rises to keep cardiac output up – even at the same pace.", "o": ["A gradual rise in heart rate during long exercise at a steady intensity, mainly due to a drop in stroke volume", "A sudden drop in heart rate at the start of exercise", "A fixed gap between morning and evening heart rate", "Blood pressure swings during sleep"]},"physio-86fem4":{"q": "An athlete's respiratory exchange ratio (RER) during exercise is close to 1.0. What does that say about the fuel source?", "e": "RER is the ratio of carbon dioxide produced to oxygen used. About 0.7 points mainly to fat burning, and about 1.0 mainly to carbohydrates.", "o": ["The body relies mainly on carbohydrates", "The body relies mainly on fat", "The body relies mainly on protein", "RER has nothing to do with the fuel source"]},"physio-p34gin":{"q": "How does high altitude (thin air) affect aerobic exercise in the first days?", "e": "At altitude the partial pressure of oxygen is lower. In the first days the same pace feels harder. Over time the body adapts, partly by making more red blood cells.", "o": ["Less oxygen is available – aerobic performance drops and heart rate is higher at a given effort", "More oxygen is available – running is easier", "It has no effect on aerobic exercise", "Heart rate drops sharply at every effort"]},"physio-1d0rynf":{"q": "Fast muscle fibers (type II) are recruited at the start of every movement, even slow walking.", "e": "False. By the size principle, small slow motor units are used first. Fast fibers join when the force or speed needed rises."},"physio-1ma083v":{"q": "What happens to plasma volume (the liquid part of the blood) in the first weeks of endurance training?", "e": "Plasma volume rises within the first days to weeks. This helps increase stroke volume and improves heat regulation.", "o": ["It increases", "It drops sharply", "It doesn't change", "It increases only in children"]},"physio-1grpo3z":{"q": "What is the 'ventilatory threshold'?", "e": "Above the threshold breathing speeds up to clear more carbon dioxide. It happens around the same time as the rise in lactate, so it's a practical sign of the line between moderate and hard effort – for example, when it gets hard to keep talking.", "o": ["The intensity at which ventilation (breathing) starts to rise sharply relative to oxygen uptake", "The number of breaths per minute at full rest", "The maximum amount of air the lungs can hold", "The moment you stop breathing through the nose"]},"physio-znw8un":{"q": "A trainee does a set of 12 reps close to failure lasting about 40 seconds. Which energy system contributes most?", "e": "Hard effort lasting tens of seconds relies mainly on glycolysis. ATP–CP contributes in the first seconds, and all three systems always work together.", "o": ["Anaerobic glycolysis", "The aerobic system only", "The ATP–CP system only", "Protein breakdown"]},"physio-28nx2r":{"q": "After strength training, muscle protein synthesis stays elevated for about 24–48 hours, especially in less experienced trainees.", "e": "True. That's one reason training each muscle about twice a week, with protein spread through the day, works well."},"physio-1luxaok":{"q": "What is the main role of proprioceptors?", "e": "Muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs are examples. Balance and movement training improves how this information is used.", "o": ["Sensors that tell the brain about position, movement and tension in joints and muscles", "Cells that make sweat in the skin", "Blood cells that carry oxygen", "Glands that release growth hormones"]},"nutrition-61ljs9":{"q": "What is the usual recommendation for 'carb loading' before endurance exercise lasting more than 90 minutes?", "e": "Loading fills glycogen stores in muscle and liver. It's only relevant for long efforts, and you rehearse it in training before race day.", "o": ["About 10–12 g of carbohydrate per kg per day for the 36–48 hours before", "A full fast the day before", "Only protein and fat for the two days before", "One very large meal right before the start"]},"nutrition-1mi7iii":{"q": "How much carbohydrate per hour is usually recommended during endurance exercise lasting 1–2.5 hours?", "e": "For example a gel, a sports drink or a banana. In even longer efforts, practiced athletes take up to about 90 g per hour from mixed sugars.", "o": ["About 30–60 g per hour", "About 5 g per hour", "About 200 g per hour", "You shouldn't eat during exercise"]},"nutrition-1f6hfux":{"q": "Which mineral is lost in the largest amount in sweat?", "e": "Sweat tastes salty because it contains a lot of sodium. In long efforts with heavy sweating, you replace salts too, not just water.", "o": ["Sodium", "Iron", "Calcium", "Zinc"]},"nutrition-79nxcy":{"q": "What is 'low energy availability' in athletes (part of RED-S)?", "e": "It can happen unintentionally, for example when training increases and eating doesn't. Signs like missed periods, stress fractures and ongoing fatigue call for referral to a doctor and a dietitian.", "o": ["Food energy isn't enough for both training and body functions – it can harm hormones, bones and performance", "Eating too many carbs before training", "Normal tiredness that goes away after good sleep", "Missing caffeine on the morning of training"]},"nutrition-1nxqely":{"q": "Taking very high doses of vitamin C completely prevents colds in people who exercise.", "e": "False. In most people it doesn't prevent colds. There may be some benefit after extreme physical stress, but there's no 'complete protection'."},"nutrition-10jcebj":{"q": "A trainee lost 1.5 kg during a long workout in the heat. Roughly how much fluid should they drink in the hours after to make it up?", "e": "The usual advice is about 1.25–1.5 liters per kg lost, because some of the fluid leaves in urine. Here: 1.5 × 1.25–1.5 ≈ 1.9–2.25 liters, with a little salt or food.", "o": ["About 1.9–2.25 liters, gradually", "About 0.5 liter", "About 5 liters at once", "None – the body will make it up overnight"]},"nutrition-13g5v0m":{"q": "What does potassium do in the body?", "e": "Potassium is found in fruits, vegetables and legumes – for example bananas, potatoes and lentils.", "o": ["Fluid balance and nerve and muscle function, including the heart muscle", "Carries oxygen in the blood", "Builds bones only", "Makes vitamin D in the skin"]},"nutrition-idkzk":{"q": "What is a good calcium source for someone who doesn't eat dairy?", "e": "There are also calcium-fortified plant drinks. Calcium absorption also depends on vitamin D.", "o": ["Tahini, calcium-set tofu, almonds and leafy greens", "White bread and white rice", "Canola oil", "Coffee and tea"]},"nutrition-1wetv0i":{"q": "'Organic' food is always lower in calories than regular food.", "e": "False. 'Organic' describes how it's grown, not its calories. An organic cookie is still a cookie."},"nutrition-17u1aq9":{"q": "When can protein powder be useful?", "e": "Protein powder is simply protein from a source like milk or soy, in a convenient form. Whole food also brings vitamins, minerals and fiber.", "o": ["When it's hard to reach your protein target from food – it's a convenient food, not a must", "It's a must for anyone who trains", "It builds muscle even without training", "Only as a replacement for every meal of the day"]},"nutrition-8k63kb":{"q": "How much protein per meal is usually considered a good amount for building muscle in a young adult?", "e": "Spreading several such meals through the day helps reach the daily target. Older adults may need a bit more protein per meal.", "o": ["About 0.3–0.4 g per kg – roughly 20–40 g", "About 2 g for the whole meal", "About 150 g in one meal", "The amount per meal doesn't matter"]},"nutrition-1voh0kj":{"q": "A trainee has a long endurance session in the morning and another session the same evening. What's best to eat right after the first session?", "e": "When recovery time is short, carbohydrates soon after exercise refill glycogen faster. Protein supports muscle repair.", "o": ["A good amount of carbohydrate with protein – to refill glycogen quickly", "Only water, so as not to feel heavy", "Only fat, because it gives more energy", "Nothing until the evening"]},"special-1ykyrqf":{"q": "What does the World Health Organization recommend for aerobic activity in a healthy pregnancy?", "e": "In a healthy pregnancy, moderate activity benefits mother and baby. Gentle strength work and pelvic floor exercises are included too.", "o": ["At least 150 minutes a week at moderate intensity, with the care provider's approval", "Stop all activity until after birth", "Only 10 minutes a week", "Only hard workouts, to strengthen the heart"]},"special-2hsxrr":{"q": "What should you prepare before training older adults who are at risk of falling?", "e": "A safe space lets you challenge balance without needless risk. Progress gradually from full support to less support.", "o": ["A space clear of obstacles, support at hand (a chair or rail) and stable shoes", "A slippery floor so it's easy to move", "Dim lighting for a calm mood", "Eyes-closed exercises from the very first session"]},"special-1ck7zcq":{"q": "For people with high blood pressure, moderate strength training with steady breathing is usually considered safe and even helpful, with medical approval.", "e": "True. Regular strength training can help lower blood pressure. Avoid breath-holding and maximal efforts, and follow personal guidance."},"special-n0y34q":{"q": "An older trainee complains of calf pain while walking that goes away after a few minutes of rest and comes back when they continue. What might this indicate?", "e": "Pain that appears with walking and fades with rest is a typical sign of peripheral artery disease. Refer to a doctor. After diagnosis and approval, a structured walking program is a common part of treatment.", "o": ["Intermittent claudication – a possible blood-flow problem in the legs that needs medical assessment", "Normal DOMS from the last workout", "Just a lack of water", "Weak abdominal muscles"]},"special-78a61v":{"q": "A trainee with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) has approval to exercise. What's true?", "e": "Pulmonary rehab includes aerobic and strength training. Allow rests, use breathing techniques and follow the guidance they received.", "o": ["Adapted activity helps; progress slowly and monitor with a breathlessness and effort scale", "All physical activity is forbidden", "Train at maximal intensity to strengthen the lungs", "Only stretching, no walking or strength work"]},"special-1ef7ws5":{"q": "For a trainee with excess weight, what counts as a realistic first goal that already brings health benefits?", "e": "Even modest weight loss improves blood pressure, blood sugar and blood fats. Even without weight loss, physical activity improves health.", "o": ["Losing about 5%–10% of body weight", "Reaching an 'ideal' weight within a month", "Losing 30% in three months", "No benefit until a normal BMI is reached"]},"special-v69ybr":{"q": "Which kind of exercise is better for strengthening the back in people with osteoporosis?", "e": "Loaded forward bending and strong twisting stress the vertebrae. Strengthening the back extensors and keeping good posture help protect the spine.", "o": ["Posture work and gentle back extension with a neutral spine, like the bird dog", "Weighted sit-ups", "Deep forward bends holding a dumbbell", "Strong, fast trunk twists"]},"special-nc600a":{"q": "After a C-section you can return to any kind of training after just a week, even without approval.", "e": "False. It's abdominal surgery. Return gradually and with the care provider's approval – starting with walking, breathing and pelvic floor work, and only later adding load."},"special-3f1myx":{"q": "What's usually recommended when training people with Parkinson's disease, with approval and following their guidance?", "e": "Regular activity helps maintain mobility, stability and quality of life. Safety and fall prevention are especially important.", "o": ["Big movements, balance, strength and walking – regularly", "Full rest so as not to worsen the disease", "Only stretching lying down", "Only sprints, without a warm-up"]},"special-1ov85u4":{"q": "A trainee who had a hip replacement via the posterior approach has finished rehab. By common guidelines, which movements need caution?", "e": "Restrictions vary by surgery type and surgeon – always follow the trainee's own instructions.", "o": ["Deep hip flexion past 90°, internal rotation and crossing the leg past the midline", "Walking on flat ground", "Riding an exercise bike with a high seat", "Standing calf raises with support"]},"special-1v0xfp9":{"q": "What's important to check with a trainee with diabetes about their feet?", "e": "Some people with diabetes have nerve damage in the feet, and a small sore may go unnoticed. Comfortable shoes and self-checks prevent complications.", "o": ["Proper shoes and checking for sores and blisters – because sensation may be reduced", "That they train barefoot to strengthen the feet", "Nothing – it isn't related to diabetes", "That the shoes are tight so the foot is stable"]},"special-1g0i4wy":{"q": "What usually suits training for someone who uses a manual wheelchair?", "e": "Pushing the chair loads the pushing muscles and the front of the shoulder a lot. Pulling and rotator cuff exercises help balance this and lower the risk of shoulder pain.", "o": ["Upper-body strength with an emphasis on pulling to balance the shoulders, plus aerobic work like an arm ergometer", "Only pushing exercises, since they resemble pushing the chair", "No training – the shoulders already work all day", "Only leg training on machines"]}};
